WEBVTT

00:00:00.444 --> 00:00:12.733
I do not remember my name.

00:00:12.792 --> 00:00:34.898
This is the first thing that I think when I wake, and I look around nervously, confused by the dark and by the thick fog which surrounds me.

00:00:34.958 --> 00:00:41.124
I raise a hand to my face and feel a short nose and a small mouth.

00:00:41.184 --> 00:00:43.986
I try to remember my face.

00:00:44.046 --> 00:00:48.530
I try to remember the color of my hair or what my eyes look like.

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:53.501
I try to remember anything, but I cannot.

00:00:53.561 --> 00:00:57.270
I have no idea who I am.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:03.441
I am on the ground, lying on cold grass which is wet from the fog.

00:01:03.501 --> 00:01:05.281
And I am alone.

00:01:05.341 --> 00:01:07.031
Why am I here?

00:01:07.091 --> 00:01:08.462
I ask myself.

00:01:08.521 --> 00:01:10.392
But I have no answer.

00:01:10.452 --> 00:01:18.843
I do not even know where this is because the fog is so thick that I can only see for a few meters in any direction.

00:01:18.903 --> 00:01:23.226
I try to stand, but then I realize that my head hurts.

00:01:23.286 --> 00:01:26.830
and that there's a sharp pain behind my right ear.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:31.524
I carefully lift my hand and touch the large lump which is there.

00:01:31.584 --> 00:01:37.849
It hurts to touch, and I shout in the fog, the sound lost in the dark of the night.

00:01:37.909 --> 00:01:45.636
When I pull my fingers away, they are wet, and even with no light the blood is bright and easy to see.

00:01:45.696 --> 00:01:48.718
I begin to feel more than nervous now.

00:01:48.778 --> 00:01:50.958
I begin to feel scared.

00:01:51.018 --> 00:01:56.130
I imagine I can see shapes and figures in the fog, and I want to run.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:02:02.181
I feel that I need to run, that there is somewhere I must be, somewhere I must remember.

00:02:02.241 --> 00:02:05.412
But before I move, I need to know something.

00:02:05.472 --> 00:02:08.413
So I push myself up and sit on the ground.

00:02:08.473 --> 00:02:11.254
I look carefully at my clothes.

00:02:11.314 --> 00:02:13.398
But they mean nothing.

00:02:13.458 --> 00:02:17.916
The jeans are new, but now they're dirty.

00:02:17.976 --> 00:02:21.660
Muddy stains cover the legs from the wet grass.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:26.864
The t-shirt is not familiar either, just simple and black.

00:02:26.924 --> 00:02:33.218
So I empty my pockets and at first there is nothing helpful there, no wallet, no keys and no phone.

00:02:33.278 --> 00:02:36.932
But then I see the picture and I stop.

00:02:36.992 --> 00:02:38.362
It's a woman.

00:02:38.422 --> 00:02:42.636
And even in the dark and the fog, I recognize her.

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:46.278
Her long blonde hair is beautiful.

00:02:46.338 --> 00:02:50.742
And her kind, friendly blue eyes are perfect.

00:02:50.802 --> 00:02:53.344
Yes, I know her.

00:02:53.403 --> 00:02:59.158
And the thought is so strong that I smile, despite the pain in the back of my head.

00:02:59.218 --> 00:03:01.198
But what's her name?

00:03:01.258 --> 00:03:04.484
I pull the last item from my pocket.

00:03:04.544 --> 00:03:10.530
It's a red serviette with a single word written again and again in black ink.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:18.454
Catherine, I say quietly into the fog, and the sound of my voice seems strange and cold.

00:03:18.514 --> 00:03:20.095
Catherine.

00:03:20.155 --> 00:03:25.527
I am sure that I know her, but I still do not remember why.

00:03:25.587 --> 00:03:28.578
Is she my wife, my girlfriend?

00:03:28.638 --> 00:03:33.180
I think that she is, and I suddenly feel afraid for her.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.780
Katherine!

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:38.141
I shout into the dark, but there is no reply.

00:03:38.161 --> 00:03:42.961
I am about to shout again when suddenly I remember something.

00:03:43.021 --> 00:03:51.838
I remember Catherine's face, but she's not smiling like she is in the picture, and her blue eyes look scared and desperate.

00:03:51.898 --> 00:04:04.107
I try to remember the image, and I see that there's a piece of cloth in her mouth, so that she cannot speak, and that she's tied to a large grey stone by thick white ropes.

00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:05.033
No!

00:04:05.053 --> 00:04:10.387
I cry out, and I push myself to my feet to spite the pain in my head.

00:04:10.447 --> 00:04:13.359
I do not know what to do for a moment.

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:18.012
I only know that I must find her, that I must help her.

00:04:18.072 --> 00:04:22.616
I think about the lump on the back of my head and the fresh blood on my fingers.

00:04:22.676 --> 00:04:25.097
Is there somebody in the fog?

00:04:25.157 --> 00:04:31.992
Somebody who has Catherine, someone who wants to hurt her, who wants to kill her?

00:04:32.052 --> 00:04:34.197
I want to shout again.

00:04:34.257 --> 00:04:36.292
but then I do not.

00:04:36.352 --> 00:04:44.344
If the person who has Catherine hears me, she is in danger, and another image comes to me.

00:04:44.404 --> 00:04:52.307
It's the face of a man, an ordinary face, with small dark eyes and dirty brown hair.

00:04:52.367 --> 00:04:58.655
There's nothing cruel about the face, but I know instantly that this is the man that has Catherine.

00:04:58.714 --> 00:05:02.288
And I hate that face, with all of my heart.

00:05:02.348 --> 00:05:07.876
I begin to walk slowly at first, unsure of the direction that I must go.

00:05:07.936 --> 00:05:11.550
It seems like the ground is moving slowly up.

00:05:11.609 --> 00:05:13.601
And I believe that this is right.

00:05:13.661 --> 00:05:20.754
The image I remember of Catherine tied to the Grey Stone is in less fog and I think that it must be on higher ground.

00:05:20.814 --> 00:05:22.815
I begin to walk faster.

00:05:22.875 --> 00:05:24.626
But I'm soon running.

00:05:24.686 --> 00:05:28.496
The fog surrounds me and the dark is without end.

00:05:28.556 --> 00:05:35.368
I try not to think of anything but the direction I'm moving in, but I'm starting to remember things now.

00:05:35.428 --> 00:05:37.519
I remember Catherine.

00:05:37.579 --> 00:05:45.112
She has a black uniform, which she always wears at work in the restaurant, and on the uniform is a badge with her name.

00:05:45.172 --> 00:05:46.604
Hello, handsome.

00:05:46.664 --> 00:05:48.576
She always says to me.

00:05:48.636 --> 00:05:53.709
Another day at the office, and I never say much, but I do not have to.

00:05:53.769 --> 00:05:56.520
We understand each other without words.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:04.431
And I remember that after I eat, I wait for her in the car park until she finishes work, and she's surprised to see me there.

00:06:04.491 --> 00:06:06.452
But I suppose that's just who I am.

00:06:06.472 --> 00:06:08.362
A romantic.

00:06:08.422 --> 00:06:09.642
Suddenly.

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:13.343
There's a sound like a gunshot, and I fall.

00:06:13.403 --> 00:06:19.482
For a moment I think that I'm dead and that Catherine is alone, tied to the grey stone in the fog.

00:06:19.542 --> 00:06:24.941
But then I see the light in the sky and I realise that the shot was a flare.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:29.196
I watch the light fall and illuminate the hillside.

00:06:29.256 --> 00:06:32.868
Is it the man with the dark eyes and dirty brown hair?

00:06:32.928 --> 00:06:34.529
Does he know I'm here?

00:06:34.589 --> 00:06:35.400
Good.

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:39.083
If he looks for me, he's not with Catherine, I think.

00:06:39.142 --> 00:06:41.934
And I get up again and run faster now.

00:06:41.994 --> 00:06:45.066
Do I hear voices in the fog behind me?

00:06:45.126 --> 00:06:49.430
I try to turn to look, but the fog behind me is too thick.

00:06:49.489 --> 00:06:53.082
And I only see the occasional light in the distance.

00:06:53.142 --> 00:06:58.293
But in front of me the fog seems to be thinner, and I begin to slow.

00:06:58.353 --> 00:07:02.154
I'm scared now, because I know that I'm near.

00:07:02.214 --> 00:07:08.915
And in the dark I begin to see large grey stones standing like giants on the top of the hill.

00:07:08.975 --> 00:07:14.957
In amazement I think that I recognize this circle of ancient stones.

00:07:15.017 --> 00:07:18.337
Stonehenge, I say.

00:07:18.397 --> 00:07:21.382
Yes, a voice replies.

00:07:21.442 --> 00:07:28.318
and this is where it ends, and a tall man steps from behind one of the silent giants.

00:07:28.378 --> 00:07:33.709
I expect him to have dark eyes and dirty hair, but it is not.

00:07:33.769 --> 00:07:40.430
His hair is blonde, his eyes light, and I think that this is not the man who has Catherine.

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:48.160
Maybe he's his friend, his partner, and I'm about to run when I see the small gum in his hand.

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:50.850
I just want Catherine!"

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:52.080
I say.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:08:01.536
But I can see the hate in the man's eyes, and I know that the only thing I can do now is run to the stone where I know she's tied, and try to escape into the fog with her.

00:08:01.596 --> 00:08:06.939
Don't, says the man, as if he can see my intention in my eyes.

00:08:06.999 --> 00:08:08.470
But I have to.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:12.022
At first I think that maybe I'm quick enough.

00:08:12.082 --> 00:08:16.746
But then I hear the snap of the gun and feel the explosion in my back.

00:08:16.806 --> 00:08:24.082
For a moment more I run and I can see the shape of the stone where Catherine is tied and I fall to the floor in front of it.

00:08:24.142 --> 00:08:25.363
Catherine!

00:08:25.423 --> 00:08:28.376
I shout, but there is no reply.

00:08:28.436 --> 00:08:33.010
When I look to see why, I see that she is not there.

00:08:33.069 --> 00:08:36.903
The white ropes are still tied to the stone, but she is not.

00:08:36.963 --> 00:08:38.234
Catherine."

00:08:38.294 --> 00:08:45.847
I say again, smiling, because she is all that matters, and I can rest now because I know that she is safe.

00:08:45.907 --> 00:08:47.644
You got him, Detective?

00:08:47.704 --> 00:08:49.674
A voice says behind me.

00:08:49.734 --> 00:08:51.245
Yeah, that's him.

00:08:51.305 --> 00:08:52.925
The tall man says.

00:08:52.985 --> 00:08:55.506
He matches the girl's description perfectly.

00:08:55.566 --> 00:08:58.297
Dirty brown hair, small dark eyes.

00:08:58.357 --> 00:09:02.188
And look, the blood behind his ear is where she hit him before she escaped.

00:09:02.248 --> 00:09:03.508
Good for her.

00:09:03.568 --> 00:09:07.800
Yeah, we think she's his third victim this year.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:09.350
So why didn't he rum?

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:10.881
Why wait here for us?

00:09:10.941 --> 00:09:13.602
The tall man is silent for a moment.

00:09:13.661 --> 00:09:15.086
Who knows?

00:09:15.146 --> 00:09:19.122
I don't want to understand the mind of these psychopaths.

00:09:19.182 --> 00:09:20.573
I'm just happy.

00:09:20.633 --> 00:09:24.594
Catherine is okay.

00:09:24.654 --> 00:09:28.016
Catherine is okay.

00:09:28.076 --> 00:09:35.550
I do not understand anything else that they say, but I do understand these three words and I smile again.

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:40.723
Catherine is okay.

00:09:40.783 --> 00:09:45.608
Rob from the rich.

00:09:45.668 --> 00:09:57.428
The sun is high above Canary Wharf, high and hot, and looking down on the crowds of people which wait on both sides of the empty road.

00:09:57.488 --> 00:10:01.990
We are standing there, the three of us in front of the bank.

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:07.147
We are standing there in front of the glass doors and no one is looking at us.

00:10:07.208 --> 00:10:13.266
They are all focused on the road, and in the distance I can hear the music and the commentator.

00:10:13.326 --> 00:10:16.338
And I know that the race is starting soon.

00:10:16.398 --> 00:10:18.589
I look at our reflections.

00:10:18.649 --> 00:10:26.084
We all have the same white and orange uniforms, the same caps, the same silver sunglasses.

00:10:26.144 --> 00:10:33.902
I almost do not recognize the two men beside me, and I think that from a distance people cannot see that I am a woman.

00:10:33.962 --> 00:10:35.794
Okay, this is it.

00:10:35.853 --> 00:10:37.446
Ten minutes exactly.

00:10:37.506 --> 00:10:41.099
Remember your jobs, and remember no real names.

00:10:41.159 --> 00:10:45.012
I'm Robin, your little John, one of the men says to the other.

00:10:45.072 --> 00:10:50.463
And you're Marion, he says to me, the only woman, and he gives me a quick smile.

00:10:50.523 --> 00:10:52.439
Little John looks up.

00:10:52.499 --> 00:10:54.580
The camera is still in position.

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:56.701
You can't see us enter or leave.

00:10:56.761 --> 00:10:59.222
I feel sweat running down my neck.

00:10:59.282 --> 00:11:00.513
Here he comes.

00:11:00.573 --> 00:11:02.054
Remember the music, Marion?

00:11:02.114 --> 00:11:03.135
Robin says.

00:11:03.195 --> 00:11:04.986
And then the guard is at the door.

00:11:05.046 --> 00:11:10.589
A short fat man who looks at us smiles and then opens it without a single question.

00:11:10.649 --> 00:11:11.729
You're quick.

00:11:11.789 --> 00:11:14.341
The air conditioning only broke this morning.

00:11:14.401 --> 00:11:17.067
We know, little John says.

00:11:17.127 --> 00:11:18.724
We broke it."

00:11:18.784 --> 00:11:21.666
And the guard's smile disappears.

00:11:21.726 --> 00:11:23.298
But it's too late.

00:11:23.358 --> 00:11:28.252
Robin pushes him back into the bank and John follows with the ladders on his shoulders.

00:11:28.312 --> 00:11:34.048
I can see Robin's gun appear in his hand and then I'm inside too and I pull the door shut.

00:11:34.108 --> 00:11:38.622
Music Marion, Robin says, there is no stopping now.

00:11:38.682 --> 00:11:43.795
So I pull the small stereo from one of the black bags on my shoulder and I press play.

00:11:43.855 --> 00:11:48.808
Beethoven's piano concerto number two fills the marble stairs.

00:11:48.868 --> 00:11:53.110
We run up the stairs together, robbing with his gun at the guard's back.

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:55.932
Then little John stops to cover the first camera.

00:11:55.992 --> 00:12:00.026
He climbs up the ladder in a second and we run into the main room of the bank.

00:12:00.086 --> 00:12:06.710
It's a large room with oak tables, a dark marble counter and expensive leather sofers.

00:12:06.770 --> 00:12:09.021
Behind the counter there are two women.

00:12:09.081 --> 00:12:11.742
Out here, now, I shout at them.

00:12:11.802 --> 00:12:16.806
To my left I see the short fat man sit down on the floor with his hands on his head.

00:12:16.866 --> 00:12:20.129
Then Robin runs to the office and kicks open the door.

00:12:20.189 --> 00:12:27.004
The two women move, but slowly, one of them looks down at the counter where I know there is a secret alarm.

00:12:27.065 --> 00:12:29.746
Not if you want to live, I shout.

00:12:29.806 --> 00:12:34.990
On the sofa, I pull two blindfold from a bag and cover their eyes.

00:12:35.050 --> 00:12:38.063
Then I tie their hands and I look at my watch.

00:12:38.123 --> 00:12:39.476
Seven minutes!

00:12:39.536 --> 00:12:43.494
I shout to little John and he covers the last camera with plastic.

00:12:43.554 --> 00:12:45.278
I take a deep breath.

00:12:45.338 --> 00:12:48.866
The music is so loud and the bank so hot.

00:12:48.926 --> 00:12:50.469
Can we do this?

00:12:50.529 --> 00:12:52.760
Can we really do this?"

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:57.252
Then Robin leaves the office and he has the manager, Mr. Charles M.

00:12:57.312 --> 00:12:58.502
Hastings.

00:12:58.562 --> 00:13:00.304
Hastings.

00:13:00.364 --> 00:13:03.506
I hate the name, and I hate the man.

00:13:03.566 --> 00:13:10.840
Tall, arrogant, dressed in his expensive suit with a watch that costs more than most people make in a year.

00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:12.544
What is this?"

00:13:12.604 --> 00:13:16.488
he says, and he looks nervous, but not nervous enough.

00:13:16.548 --> 00:13:17.910
What do you think?

00:13:17.969 --> 00:13:21.092
Robin asks, and he tells him to sit at one of the tables.

00:13:21.152 --> 00:13:23.014
Hastings looks around.

00:13:23.074 --> 00:13:25.097
You can't be serious.

00:13:25.157 --> 00:13:26.839
A bank robbery.

00:13:26.898 --> 00:13:29.372
And for a moment he looks surprised.

00:13:29.432 --> 00:13:31.432
But then he laughs.

00:13:31.493 --> 00:13:32.972
idiots.

00:13:33.032 --> 00:13:35.364
This is an investment bank.

00:13:35.424 --> 00:13:36.994
There's no money here.

00:13:37.054 --> 00:13:39.815
Everything is done by transfer.

00:13:39.876 --> 00:13:45.348
And he looks at us all like we're children, with that arrogant expression on his face.

00:13:45.408 --> 00:13:51.040
Good Lord, you should leave now before the police get here and maybe you can still escape.

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:52.331
He laughs again.

00:13:52.391 --> 00:13:53.950
But I doubt it.

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:58.658
Little John puts a blindfold on the guard, only Hastings can see now.

00:13:58.718 --> 00:14:00.558
But we stand behind him.

00:14:00.618 --> 00:14:06.650
And for a few seconds there is only the sound of Beethoven as the music begins to reach its multi-legro.

00:14:06.710 --> 00:14:12.251
That's okay, Robin says, and he picks up one of the bags and puts it on the table.

00:14:12.311 --> 00:14:13.822
We brought our own money.

00:14:13.882 --> 00:14:16.962
Twenty thousand pounds in small notes.

00:14:17.022 --> 00:14:20.613
Banks are difficult to rob, but houses aren't.

00:14:20.673 --> 00:14:24.294
Even big houses with alarms and safes.

00:14:24.354 --> 00:14:25.954
Like yours.

00:14:26.014 --> 00:14:27.225
What?

00:14:27.285 --> 00:14:28.986
Hastings says.

00:14:29.046 --> 00:14:31.056
And then he looks at the bag.

00:14:31.116 --> 00:14:32.727
My house.

00:14:32.787 --> 00:14:34.238
My money.

00:14:34.298 --> 00:14:36.548
You robbed my house.

00:14:36.608 --> 00:14:39.239
I can see sweat on Hastings' face now.

00:14:39.299 --> 00:14:40.880
But it's on mine too.

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:42.350
I look at my watch.

00:14:42.410 --> 00:14:44.271
Four minutes.

00:14:44.331 --> 00:14:45.711
But what do you want?

00:14:45.771 --> 00:14:48.302
You have my money.

00:14:48.363 --> 00:14:50.988
Robin laughs.

00:14:51.048 --> 00:14:52.563
We don't want it.

00:14:52.623 --> 00:14:53.594
Not that.

00:14:53.654 --> 00:14:55.736
We're not common thieves."

00:14:55.796 --> 00:14:58.328
And he throws the bag to Little John.

00:14:58.388 --> 00:15:00.540
Get it ready, he says.

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:05.264
And Little John takes the ladders back down the stairs to the glass doors.

00:15:05.324 --> 00:15:07.016
Then what?

00:15:07.076 --> 00:15:08.868
Shouts Hastings.

00:15:08.928 --> 00:15:10.770
And this is my part.

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:15.495
I take the laptop from the bag and put it in front of him and open it.

00:15:15.555 --> 00:15:17.076
Do you know what this is?

00:15:17.137 --> 00:15:18.428
He looks.

00:15:18.488 --> 00:15:21.342
It's a bank account, a transfer.

00:15:21.402 --> 00:15:24.400
So, he says arrogantly.

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:30.440
So we need you to enter your details and then transfer £240,000 to this account.

00:15:30.500 --> 00:15:32.302
Do it, and then we go.

00:15:32.362 --> 00:15:34.916
And he looks more than nervous now.

00:15:34.976 --> 00:15:36.758
He looks scared.

00:15:36.818 --> 00:15:38.209
No, I can't.

00:15:38.269 --> 00:15:39.300
I don't have that sort of money.

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:40.222
I can't.

00:15:40.282 --> 00:15:42.954
I put the newspaper down on the computer.

00:15:43.014 --> 00:15:43.983
Really?

00:15:44.044 --> 00:15:47.516
He looks at the newspaper for a moment that says nothing.

00:15:47.576 --> 00:15:50.240
One minute, says little John.

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:52.904
Read it, Robin shouts.

00:15:52.964 --> 00:15:56.580
Hastings is silent, but Robin points the gun at him.

00:15:56.640 --> 00:15:59.204
Read it, he shouts again.

00:15:59.264 --> 00:16:05.678
Okay, okay, bank manager receives £240,000 salary despite bank failure.

00:16:05.738 --> 00:16:11.363
He stops and tries to turn to look at us, but Robin pulls the trigger back on the gun.

00:16:11.423 --> 00:16:14.675
You don't understand, Hastings shouts.

00:16:14.735 --> 00:16:16.809
It's more complicated than that.

00:16:16.869 --> 00:16:23.724
We understand that hundreds of local businesses are in serious trouble because of you and this bank," I say.

00:16:23.784 --> 00:16:31.776
And you now have a decision to make because we have no time enter the details or, I say, and I know that this is it.

00:16:31.836 --> 00:16:33.706
It has to be now.

00:16:33.766 --> 00:16:40.458
Beethoven fills the silence again, and I look at Robin and see that there's sweat on his face too.

00:16:40.518 --> 00:16:42.549
But Hastings does not move.

00:16:42.609 --> 00:16:45.161
Then goodbye, Mr. Hastings."

00:16:45.222 --> 00:16:47.724
Robin says, and he moves the gun.

00:16:47.784 --> 00:16:50.228
But, no, please, look!

00:16:50.288 --> 00:16:52.900
And Hastings enters his details.

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:54.642
I do not believe it.

00:16:54.702 --> 00:16:56.834
And I take the computer.

00:16:56.894 --> 00:16:58.938
Well, Robin says.

00:16:58.998 --> 00:16:59.928
It's done.

00:16:59.988 --> 00:17:00.828
It's gone.

00:17:00.888 --> 00:17:01.950
Direct.

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:03.460
No one can trace it.

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:07.774
Then the music stops, and I want to smile that there is no time.

00:17:07.834 --> 00:17:12.238
In the distance I hear the sound of a gun and the crowd outside cheers.

00:17:12.297 --> 00:17:17.531
Robin takes the last blindfold from the bag and puts it over Hastings' eyes.

00:17:17.592 --> 00:17:18.932
And what now?

00:17:18.993 --> 00:17:22.265
Hastings says, you think you can just walk out of here?

00:17:22.325 --> 00:17:23.736
Robin laughs.

00:17:23.796 --> 00:17:25.458
Something like that?

00:17:25.517 --> 00:17:27.428
He says quietly.

00:17:27.488 --> 00:17:28.730
The police are out there.

00:17:28.750 --> 00:17:29.330
They'll see you.

00:17:29.370 --> 00:17:30.480
They'll stop you."

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:33.562
Robin then puts his mouth to Hastings' ear.

00:17:33.622 --> 00:17:37.125
Not if there's a big enough distraction, he says.

00:17:37.185 --> 00:17:41.158
Oh, and just to let you know, there are no bullets in any of our guns.

00:17:41.218 --> 00:17:44.650
Hastings starts to shout, but we do not listen.

00:17:44.710 --> 00:17:46.150
This is it.

00:17:46.211 --> 00:17:47.991
We have to go now.

00:17:48.052 --> 00:17:51.593
We take our uniforms off and put them in the bag.

00:17:51.653 --> 00:17:53.384
Then we're at the doors.

00:17:53.444 --> 00:17:57.985
Outside we hear the crowd cheer and we see the first runners pass by.

00:17:58.045 --> 00:17:59.285
Then there are more.

00:17:59.345 --> 00:18:00.276
Many more.

00:18:00.336 --> 00:18:01.476
Hundreds more.

00:18:01.536 --> 00:18:03.266
Thousands more.

00:18:03.327 --> 00:18:05.517
And now we start to smile.

00:18:05.577 --> 00:18:07.892
Because we know we're nearly out.

00:18:07.952 --> 00:18:12.898
Now, says Robin, and little John presses the control on the air conditioning.

00:18:12.958 --> 00:18:14.648
For a moment there is nothing.

00:18:14.708 --> 00:18:16.800
Then the fans start.

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:20.472
And then money fills the air outside.

00:18:20.532 --> 00:18:23.094
Twenty thousand pounds.

00:18:23.154 --> 00:18:27.606
It flies over the heads of the crowd into the runners and into the road.

00:18:27.666 --> 00:18:30.958
And then there is nothing but chaos.

00:18:31.018 --> 00:18:35.009
And no one sees us walk slowly from the bank.

00:18:35.069 --> 00:18:37.500
And no one remembers us later.

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:41.311
And maybe you want to know what we plan to do with the money.

00:18:41.371 --> 00:18:45.672
Unfortunately this, I can never tell you.

00:18:45.732 --> 00:18:49.774
The lock and the monster.

00:18:49.834 --> 00:18:52.704
No darling, she means nothing to me.

00:18:52.764 --> 00:18:53.685
Less than nothing.

00:18:53.745 --> 00:18:54.755
I hate her.

00:18:54.815 --> 00:18:56.645
It's only you, Emily.

00:18:56.705 --> 00:18:57.706
I promise.

00:18:57.766 --> 00:18:58.894
What?

00:18:58.954 --> 00:19:00.482
Divorce her?

00:19:00.542 --> 00:19:01.974
That's impossible.

00:19:02.034 --> 00:19:02.735
Why?

00:19:02.795 --> 00:19:05.008
Because of the money, the house, the cars?

00:19:05.068 --> 00:19:07.971
No, Emily, of course, you're more important than them, but...

00:19:08.032 --> 00:19:08.913
No.

00:19:08.973 --> 00:19:10.024
No, listen, don't go.

00:19:10.064 --> 00:19:10.545
Listen to me.

00:19:10.585 --> 00:19:13.678
When I return to London, everything is going to be different.

00:19:13.738 --> 00:19:15.522
I have a plan, darling.

00:19:15.582 --> 00:19:17.576
Darling, I have a plan.

00:19:17.636 --> 00:19:18.886
Emily?

00:19:18.946 --> 00:19:20.926
But there is no answer.

00:19:20.986 --> 00:19:26.258
And Oliver Stern puts his mobile back into his coat pocket and looks around.

00:19:26.318 --> 00:19:28.948
There is no one near to him to hear.

00:19:29.008 --> 00:19:30.719
He is alone on the jetty.

00:19:30.779 --> 00:19:36.480
Apart from the boat attendant at his chair 20 metres away, his feet on a boat and a cigarette in his mouth.

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:38.471
And he cannot hear from there.

00:19:38.532 --> 00:19:40.184
Oliver is sure.

00:19:40.244 --> 00:19:44.448
But just to be safe, he decides not to phone Emily again.

00:19:44.508 --> 00:19:47.832
Perhaps the less she knows the better.

00:19:47.892 --> 00:19:49.693
A plan.

00:19:49.754 --> 00:19:51.004
Yes.

00:19:51.064 --> 00:19:53.176
He has a plan.

00:19:53.236 --> 00:19:54.758
A good plan.

00:19:54.818 --> 00:19:56.670
And a simple plan.

00:19:56.731 --> 00:20:00.154
He looks out across the horrible grey lock.

00:20:00.214 --> 00:20:07.474
The sun is almost down behind the green snowy mountains now, and it's colder than before.

00:20:07.534 --> 00:20:13.896
What sort of person wants to have a holiday in this remote, ugly part of the world?

00:20:13.956 --> 00:20:20.957
Then he hears the sound of his wife's laughter from the terrace bar of the hotel, and he shakes his head.

00:20:21.017 --> 00:20:24.649
The sort of person like my monster of a wife.

00:20:24.709 --> 00:20:27.170
He tries to picture Emily.

00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:31.511
Her eyes are so loving, her laugh so light and pretty.

00:20:31.571 --> 00:20:35.302
But when his wife laughs, she sounds like a donkey.

00:20:35.362 --> 00:20:41.564
He looks at the lock again at the jetty and the small boats waiting there for the tourists in the hotel.

00:20:41.624 --> 00:20:46.944
And he asks himself once more if he can do it.

00:20:47.004 --> 00:20:52.996
She bays again, and he knows that he can.

00:20:53.056 --> 00:20:55.096
It's 10 to 6 now.

00:20:55.156 --> 00:21:00.207
The boats stop going out at 6, and the sun is almost gone.

00:21:00.267 --> 00:21:04.329
She needs to come now, he knows, but he cannot shout for her.

00:21:04.389 --> 00:21:07.320
No, it's her idea to go on a boat.

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:10.061
Everyone in the bar knows she wants to go.

00:21:10.121 --> 00:21:14.702
I just need to wait for her, like a good husband, he thinks.

00:21:14.762 --> 00:21:21.965
The type of husband who is happy to go out on the huge, horrible lock in the dark with his monster of a wife.

00:21:22.025 --> 00:21:23.766
But can she still swim?

00:21:23.786 --> 00:21:25.747
He's not sure.

00:21:25.807 --> 00:21:33.692
He thinks that with her clothes and her coat on she'll be too heavy and that the cold grey water of the lock will pull her down.

00:21:33.752 --> 00:21:35.483
But is that enough?

00:21:35.543 --> 00:21:40.210
He looks at the bag by his feet, the romantic picnic.

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:47.689
filled with all the food his wife loves, and the bottle of champagne that he knows she cannot resist.

00:21:47.749 --> 00:21:49.500
Oliver smiles.

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:51.532
It is a heavy bag.

00:21:51.592 --> 00:21:58.517
All he has to do is make her hold it for a second, perhaps quickly wrap the straps around her hands.

00:21:58.577 --> 00:22:01.930
Then, no more monster.

00:22:01.990 --> 00:22:04.231
But it's nearly six now.

00:22:04.291 --> 00:22:07.094
The attendant is about to finish for the night.

00:22:07.154 --> 00:22:08.382
Excuse me?

00:22:08.442 --> 00:22:10.532
Oliver shouts with a smile.

00:22:10.592 --> 00:22:14.302
He pulls his cigarette packet from his pocket and walks over.

00:22:14.362 --> 00:22:16.783
Sorry to bother you, but do you have a lighter?

00:22:16.843 --> 00:22:21.365
The attendant has a hard face and fierce red hair.

00:22:21.425 --> 00:22:23.625
He looks at Oliver for a moment.

00:22:23.685 --> 00:22:27.626
Nods, pulls his lighter from his pocket and offers it.

00:22:27.686 --> 00:22:29.737
Look, continues Oliver.

00:22:29.797 --> 00:22:31.468
My wife wants to take a boat out.

00:22:31.488 --> 00:22:32.946
She's going to be here in a second.

00:22:33.006 --> 00:22:35.316
The man looks at the sky.

00:22:35.376 --> 00:22:38.106
A boat, man, we want a boat.

00:22:38.166 --> 00:22:40.257
Mmm, then I can't about that.

00:22:40.317 --> 00:22:42.158
Better than Marla.

00:22:42.218 --> 00:22:44.998
The idiot does not speak English.

00:22:45.058 --> 00:22:46.409
All of the things.

00:22:46.469 --> 00:22:48.210
Bloody country.

00:22:48.270 --> 00:22:50.260
I'm sorry, I don't understand.

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:51.820
I'm from England.

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:55.862
The man laughs, stands up, and says nothing.

00:22:55.922 --> 00:23:00.200
Oliver tries to stay calm, but he knows there's too little time.

00:23:00.260 --> 00:23:01.690
Don't you speak English?

00:23:01.750 --> 00:23:03.662
My wife wants to go out now.

00:23:03.722 --> 00:23:05.265
She's only going to be one minute.

00:23:05.325 --> 00:23:11.928
He shouts and the attendant stops and gives him a long hard look with his cold blue eyes.

00:23:11.988 --> 00:23:13.722
Oliver, here I am!"

00:23:13.782 --> 00:23:20.865
a loud cheerful voice says, and the two men turn to see Mrs. Sylvia Stern.

00:23:20.925 --> 00:23:28.878
She smiles that stupid cow-like smile at them, and Oliver sees the idiot attendant smile back.

00:23:28.938 --> 00:23:36.910
She is dressed in a horrible tartan skirt and woolen jumper, and in her arms she has the heavy wax coat.

00:23:36.970 --> 00:23:39.481
Good, Oliver thinks.

00:23:39.541 --> 00:23:42.320
Darling, he says with a smile.

00:23:42.380 --> 00:23:44.420
This good man wants to close.

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:46.864
I think we must wait until tomorrow now.

00:23:46.924 --> 00:23:48.436
Oh no, surely not.

00:23:48.496 --> 00:23:50.850
She turns to the man and smiles again.

00:23:50.910 --> 00:23:53.514
Can't we just go out for a few minutes, please?

00:23:53.574 --> 00:23:55.445
Just to enjoy the sunset.

00:23:55.505 --> 00:23:59.684
And the attendant ignores Oliver but returns her smile.

00:23:59.744 --> 00:24:02.516
Aye, well just for ten minutes then.

00:24:02.576 --> 00:24:04.866
Oh thank you, Sylvia says.

00:24:04.926 --> 00:24:10.358
And the attendant helps them into the small rowing boat and passes Oliver the picnic bag.

00:24:10.418 --> 00:24:13.729
He then gives him another long hard look.

00:24:13.789 --> 00:24:15.920
But Oliver does not care.

00:24:15.980 --> 00:24:22.774
Bloody idiot, he says to Sylvia as they slowly move away from the jetty onto the dark water.

00:24:22.834 --> 00:24:30.887
I don't understand a thing he says, but Sylvia does not seem to hear, and instead looks out at the dark lock.

00:24:30.948 --> 00:24:42.834
For five minutes Oliver rose, and soon the jetty and the hotel look much smaller, while the attendant is almost impossible to see in the half-light of the new evening.

00:24:42.894 --> 00:24:45.406
And so this is it.

00:24:45.466 --> 00:24:49.288
Oliver thinks about loving ironic Emily again.

00:24:49.348 --> 00:24:52.539
Then he looks at his monster of a wife.

00:24:52.599 --> 00:24:55.500
Oh yes, this has to be it.

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:58.542
No more listening to that awful laugh.

00:24:58.602 --> 00:25:00.973
No more looking at her boring face.

00:25:01.033 --> 00:25:06.206
And perhaps more importantly, no more sharing his bank account with her.

00:25:06.266 --> 00:25:10.019
Darling, he says, shall we have a little drink?

00:25:10.078 --> 00:25:11.861
Oh yes, that would be very nice.

00:25:11.921 --> 00:25:17.316
She smiles, but in the half-light Oliver thinks he sees something strange in her expression.

00:25:17.375 --> 00:25:20.648
Can you pass me over the bag, then you can come and sit here next to me.

00:25:20.708 --> 00:25:22.990
He says as casually as he can.

00:25:23.050 --> 00:25:24.171
Of course.

00:25:24.231 --> 00:25:26.591
Sylvia says and she stands up.

00:25:26.651 --> 00:25:28.722
which makes the boat rock.

00:25:28.782 --> 00:25:32.436
Oliver thinks that this is going to be very easy indeed.

00:25:32.496 --> 00:25:41.028
Just one push, and then he can shout for the attendant and pretend to help that really just watch the monster go down into the lock.

00:25:41.088 --> 00:25:47.266
Just one look back at the jetty to be sure no one can see, he thinks, and he turns his head.

00:25:47.326 --> 00:25:49.799
And then it happens.

00:25:49.859 --> 00:25:54.483
Pain explodes at the back of his head and his eyes go dark.

00:25:54.544 --> 00:25:56.976
For a second he does not know where he is.

00:25:57.036 --> 00:26:01.100
Then he feels hands on him, pushing him and moving him.

00:26:01.160 --> 00:26:05.534
As he tries to say something, he sees the broken champagne bottle at his feet.

00:26:05.594 --> 00:26:07.525
Then he falls from the boat.

00:26:07.585 --> 00:26:12.219
And the ice-cold water takes the words and the air from his mouth.

00:26:12.279 --> 00:26:18.814
For a moment he goes down, but then he kicks his legs and he comes to the surface and looks up at Sylvia in the boat.

00:26:18.875 --> 00:26:21.997
She has an oar in her hands and she's reaching it out to him.

00:26:22.057 --> 00:26:24.039
Yes, closer, help me!

00:26:24.099 --> 00:26:29.036
He shouts, and it's then that he sees the strange expression in her face again.

00:26:29.096 --> 00:26:33.500
Sylvia, Darling, don't you dare call me that.

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:36.911
She says in a tone as cold as the water.

00:26:36.971 --> 00:26:40.802
You're a cheating, lying, horrible monster of a man.

00:26:40.862 --> 00:26:44.593
You can call that stupid girl of yours, Darling, but not me.

00:26:44.653 --> 00:26:48.295
Never again, Oliver Stern, never again.

00:26:48.355 --> 00:26:50.946
And then the awe hits him on the head.

00:26:51.006 --> 00:26:55.538
And he cannot think, and he cannot swim, and the water takes him.

00:26:55.598 --> 00:27:00.370
For a few moments he sinks slowly into the great lock.

00:27:00.430 --> 00:27:07.416
But then, somehow, miraculously, he kicks again and he comes to the surface once more.

00:27:07.476 --> 00:27:10.337
Silvia and the boat are gone.

00:27:10.398 --> 00:27:13.835
And the half light is complete darkness now.

00:27:13.895 --> 00:27:19.004
He tries to swim, but his clothes and his coat are too heavy.

00:27:19.064 --> 00:27:23.127
The jetty, the attendant, he has to still be there.

00:27:23.187 --> 00:27:25.349
If he hears me, he can bring a boat out.

00:27:25.409 --> 00:27:26.610
Help me!

00:27:26.670 --> 00:27:28.642
He shouts into the darkness.

00:27:28.702 --> 00:27:29.942
Help me!

00:27:30.002 --> 00:27:32.628
For the love of God help me!

00:27:32.688 --> 00:27:37.303
And on the small jetty on the bank of Loch Ness, the attendant hears.

00:27:37.363 --> 00:27:40.445
He takes the cigarette from his mouth and smiles.

00:27:40.505 --> 00:27:42.006
Sorry, laddie.

00:27:42.066 --> 00:27:44.488
He says quietly to the lock.

00:27:44.548 --> 00:27:48.721
I can't understand the word you're saying.

00:27:48.781 --> 00:27:52.463
Follow me.

00:27:52.524 --> 00:27:55.294
Follow me.

00:27:55.354 --> 00:27:58.754
says Detective Branwell with a sigh.

00:27:58.814 --> 00:28:05.055
His shift finishes in five minutes, but there's no one around in Howarth's quiet police station.

00:28:05.115 --> 00:28:12.576
And so he leads the woman to the first interview room and tries to suppress the image of a pint of beer in the black bull pub.

00:28:12.636 --> 00:28:17.007
The interview room is cold, the lights weak and grey.

00:28:17.067 --> 00:28:21.177
But he offers the woman a seat and hopes that this will be quick.

00:28:21.237 --> 00:28:25.156
As she takes her coat off, Branwell watches her.

00:28:25.216 --> 00:28:29.956
She has long brown hair that right now looks a little untidy.

00:28:30.016 --> 00:28:38.884
And she has deep green eyes that at the moment are tired and nervous and show something which he does not completely recognize.

00:28:38.944 --> 00:28:44.870
Right, he says, and tries to smile to relax her.

00:28:44.930 --> 00:28:47.144
What's all this about then?

00:28:47.204 --> 00:28:51.018
The officer at the front desk says you need to speak to someone.

00:28:51.078 --> 00:28:57.142
I do, she says, and her voice is thin and tense.

00:28:57.202 --> 00:28:58.822
About what?

00:28:58.883 --> 00:29:03.375
She waits for a moment, uncertain, and then she takes a breath.

00:29:03.436 --> 00:29:10.710
About a crime, Branwell pulls his notepad from his pocket and places it in front of him on the table.

00:29:10.770 --> 00:29:15.044
He has a bad feeling about his pint in the black bull pub.

00:29:15.104 --> 00:29:17.820
I see, he says.

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:20.626
Maybe you can tell me your name first.

00:29:20.686 --> 00:29:22.408
You're addressed too.

00:29:22.468 --> 00:29:28.822
She puts her hands on the table and he sees that her red fingernails are chipped and bitten.

00:29:28.882 --> 00:29:32.565
Jane Thornton, I live at brow top cottage.

00:29:32.625 --> 00:29:34.216
Brow top?

00:29:34.277 --> 00:29:35.988
Oh yes, I remember.

00:29:36.048 --> 00:29:38.769
You have that little red sports card, don't you?

00:29:38.830 --> 00:29:40.480
Not now, no.

00:29:40.540 --> 00:29:43.512
She says and she picks up her handbag.

00:29:43.572 --> 00:29:45.014
Can I smoke?

00:29:45.074 --> 00:29:48.454
No, sorry love, rules and regulations.

00:29:48.514 --> 00:29:50.654
I can get you a tea if you like."

00:29:50.714 --> 00:29:53.978
But she shakes her head and puts the bag at her feet.

00:29:54.038 --> 00:29:56.860
And maybe you can tell me why you're here.

00:29:56.920 --> 00:29:58.430
He suggests.

00:29:58.490 --> 00:30:00.582
Again she waits.

00:30:00.643 --> 00:30:06.108
And again Branwell thinks that he sees something in her deep green eyes.

00:30:06.168 --> 00:30:08.159
There's a man who follows me.

00:30:08.219 --> 00:30:09.390
She begins.

00:30:09.450 --> 00:30:11.012
Every evening.

00:30:11.072 --> 00:30:14.384
Every evening when I return home from work.

00:30:14.444 --> 00:30:16.686
Branwell nods.

00:30:16.746 --> 00:30:18.456
I see.

00:30:18.516 --> 00:30:20.226
No, you don't.

00:30:20.286 --> 00:30:22.116
Every evening.

00:30:22.176 --> 00:30:24.798
Every single evening.

00:30:24.858 --> 00:30:28.700
For almost a year now, he's always there, detective.

00:30:28.760 --> 00:30:33.953
Sometimes he's so close that I think he can touch me, and other times he's far away.

00:30:34.014 --> 00:30:36.485
But I still know that he's there.

00:30:36.545 --> 00:30:43.630
And, detective, you might think that I'm crazy, but I know what this man wants.

00:30:43.690 --> 00:30:44.910
What?

00:30:44.970 --> 00:30:46.631
Branwell says.

00:30:46.691 --> 00:30:48.338
What does he want?

00:30:48.398 --> 00:30:52.427
She pauses for a second and he sees her hands shake.

00:30:52.487 --> 00:30:54.258
He wants to kill me.

00:30:54.318 --> 00:30:58.490
Branwell says nothing but watches the woman's face.

00:30:58.550 --> 00:31:00.612
She is scared.

00:31:00.672 --> 00:31:02.382
Very scared.

00:31:02.442 --> 00:31:04.804
Does he speak to you this man?

00:31:04.864 --> 00:31:06.645
No, never.

00:31:06.705 --> 00:31:09.466
And when I try to speak to him, me...

00:31:09.527 --> 00:31:10.659
Yes?

00:31:10.719 --> 00:31:12.541
It disappears.

00:31:12.601 --> 00:31:16.442
Branwell opens his notebook and takes his pen out.

00:31:16.502 --> 00:31:19.573
Where do you work, Mrs. Thorne?

00:31:19.633 --> 00:31:21.434
Miss Thorne.

00:31:21.494 --> 00:31:23.524
I work in Leeds.

00:31:23.584 --> 00:31:26.225
I have a small media company.

00:31:26.285 --> 00:31:28.025
In Leeds?

00:31:28.085 --> 00:31:30.906
Then where's this man follow you from?

00:31:30.966 --> 00:31:33.176
From the station at Keithley.

00:31:33.236 --> 00:31:36.044
You work in Leeds, but you don't drive.

00:31:36.105 --> 00:31:42.374
I'll think your sports car can get you there a lot faster than the bus to Keithley and then the train to Leeds.

00:31:42.434 --> 00:31:44.625
I told you, I don't drive it.

00:31:44.685 --> 00:31:45.875
Not now.

00:31:45.935 --> 00:31:46.915
Right.

00:31:46.975 --> 00:31:48.676
So he followed you.

00:31:48.736 --> 00:31:53.158
From Keithley, I see him at the train station sometimes.

00:31:53.218 --> 00:31:58.125
Then he's there again when I get off the bus in the town center here in Arworth.

00:31:58.185 --> 00:32:00.721
and then he follows me to my cottage.

00:32:00.782 --> 00:32:06.653
You walk from here to brow top, up over the moors' road, every evening."

00:32:06.713 --> 00:32:12.114
That's a dangerous road, and it must be more than three miles.

00:32:12.174 --> 00:32:14.134
Dangerous, yes.

00:32:14.194 --> 00:32:18.085
But she does not say anything else, and Branwell continues.

00:32:18.145 --> 00:32:20.436
Can you describe the man?

00:32:20.496 --> 00:32:23.066
Easily, she says.

00:32:23.126 --> 00:32:28.518
But again she pauses, and Branwell sees her turn quickly to look over her shoulder.

00:32:28.578 --> 00:32:30.612
He's tall with dark hair.

00:32:30.672 --> 00:32:33.804
His skin is pale and his eyes cold.

00:32:33.864 --> 00:32:36.227
Dead, she says.

00:32:36.287 --> 00:32:38.440
He has dead eyes.

00:32:38.500 --> 00:32:43.280
And they watch me every day, and I think that they watch me at night too.

00:32:43.340 --> 00:32:47.210
Branwell feels a shiver run over his back.

00:32:47.270 --> 00:32:50.812
You think that this man enters your premises?

00:32:50.872 --> 00:32:54.076
That he enters your cottage during the night?

00:32:54.136 --> 00:32:55.516
I do.

00:32:55.577 --> 00:32:58.619
Sometimes I wake and I know that he's there.

00:32:58.679 --> 00:33:04.233
I turn on the lights and I see nothing but when I go to the windows, I can see him in the field.

00:33:04.294 --> 00:33:09.418
He stands there like a scarecrow in the dark, and he accuses me.

00:33:09.478 --> 00:33:14.322
Every day, his cold, dead eyes accuse me.

00:33:14.382 --> 00:33:16.694
Branwell shakes his head.

00:33:16.754 --> 00:33:20.808
Is she crazy or just terrified?

00:33:20.868 --> 00:33:27.090
Miss Thorne, if this man enters your house, we can arrest him.

00:33:27.150 --> 00:33:30.438
But to do that we need evidence.

00:33:30.498 --> 00:33:40.201
As he broke in a window, or entered the property by force, she lets out a laugh, a laugh that is horrible to Branwell.

00:33:40.261 --> 00:33:44.142
No, of course not, there's nothing.

00:33:44.202 --> 00:33:46.883
And he never says anything threatening.

00:33:46.943 --> 00:33:49.214
He never says that he wants to hurt you.

00:33:49.274 --> 00:33:55.645
She looks over her shoulder again quickly and automatically as if she does it a hundred times a day.

00:33:55.706 --> 00:33:58.324
No, he never says a thing.

00:33:58.384 --> 00:34:00.263
But I know.

00:34:00.323 --> 00:34:02.594
I know what he wants.

00:34:02.654 --> 00:34:04.574
I know what he wants.

00:34:04.634 --> 00:34:06.505
And she begins to cry.

00:34:06.565 --> 00:34:09.166
Branwell puts the notepad away.

00:34:09.226 --> 00:34:13.416
I'm sorry, Miss Thorne, but there's very little that we can do.

00:34:13.477 --> 00:34:16.227
Let me make you that tea, a nice tea to calm you down.

00:34:16.247 --> 00:34:20.058
Let me can start again and see if there are any other details.

00:34:20.118 --> 00:34:24.019
She seems to nod and he stands up and goes to the door.

00:34:24.079 --> 00:34:28.266
We need a better description, you see, if we want to identify the man.

00:34:28.327 --> 00:34:29.293
What?

00:34:29.353 --> 00:34:32.004
She says before he can open the door.

00:34:32.064 --> 00:34:33.125
Oh, no, no.

00:34:33.145 --> 00:34:34.305
You don't understand the detective.

00:34:34.365 --> 00:34:35.646
I know who the man is.

00:34:35.706 --> 00:34:36.586
I know his name.

00:34:36.646 --> 00:34:37.746
I know everything.

00:34:37.807 --> 00:34:39.628
Branwell stops.

00:34:39.688 --> 00:34:40.848
You mean?

00:34:40.908 --> 00:34:42.168
She continues.

00:34:42.228 --> 00:34:44.859
And if I tell you his name, you can help me.

00:34:44.919 --> 00:34:46.472
You can stop this.

00:34:46.533 --> 00:34:48.616
She says desperately.

00:34:48.676 --> 00:34:50.587
You know his name.

00:34:50.647 --> 00:34:51.908
Do you promise?

00:34:51.968 --> 00:34:53.348
You can stop it.

00:34:53.408 --> 00:34:55.340
Branwell does not sit.

00:34:55.400 --> 00:34:58.502
He sees that expression in her eyes again.

00:34:58.562 --> 00:35:01.904
I can't promise love, no, but I can try.

00:35:01.964 --> 00:35:05.926
She nods and bites her red fingernails for a moment.

00:35:05.986 --> 00:35:08.984
His name is William Gray.

00:35:09.044 --> 00:35:11.353
William Gray.

00:35:11.413 --> 00:35:13.654
Branwell knows the name.

00:35:13.714 --> 00:35:15.606
William Gray.

00:35:15.666 --> 00:35:23.250
And he remembers something horrible from more than a year ago, an accident on the roads across the moors.

00:35:23.310 --> 00:35:25.142
William Gray.

00:35:25.202 --> 00:35:28.254
A local cyclist and a good man.

00:35:28.314 --> 00:35:30.165
William Gray.

00:35:30.225 --> 00:35:33.057
Miss Thornton, William Gray is dead.

00:35:33.117 --> 00:35:37.840
He was it by a car last year while he cycled across brow top road.

00:35:37.900 --> 00:35:42.034
We never found the driver, but William died instantly, they say.

00:35:42.094 --> 00:35:43.444
Oh, I know.

00:35:43.504 --> 00:35:47.630
She says, and he sees that something in her deep green eyes.

00:35:47.690 --> 00:35:50.663
And he thinks that he knows what it is now.

00:35:50.723 --> 00:35:54.870
I know, detective, because I am that driver.

00:35:54.930 --> 00:35:59.813
Then there is only the sound of her crying to fill the cold interview room.

00:35:59.874 --> 00:36:04.385
And Branwell sits down again and picks up his pen and notepad.

00:36:04.445 --> 00:36:13.678
I think you should tell me everything, he says, and he can see clearly now the guilt that fills her deep green eyes.

00:36:13.738 --> 00:36:19.370
An hour later Branwell closes the door of the interview room and sighs.

00:36:19.430 --> 00:36:22.081
He does not feel any sympathy for her.

00:36:22.141 --> 00:36:23.182
He cannot.

00:36:23.242 --> 00:36:25.513
Not for someone who can do that.

00:36:25.573 --> 00:36:29.255
To leave someone on the grey maws to die.

00:36:29.315 --> 00:36:32.497
No, he cannot feel any sympathy for her.

00:36:32.557 --> 00:36:36.168
But maybe now she can find some rest.

00:36:36.228 --> 00:36:40.763
Maybe now she can forget the ghost of William Grey.

00:36:40.823 --> 00:36:49.830
He walks up the corridor to the front desk and is about to shout for an officer when he sees the tall figure standing at the door of the police station.

00:36:49.890 --> 00:36:51.330
He stops.

00:36:51.390 --> 00:36:55.212
His heart suddenly beating faster.

00:36:55.272 --> 00:36:59.955
The man's dark hair is wet from the rain outside.

00:37:00.015 --> 00:37:03.957
His skin pale and his eyes cold and dead.

00:37:04.017 --> 00:37:05.607
It's him.

00:37:05.668 --> 00:37:10.336
Branwell knows it, but he cannot believe it.

00:37:10.396 --> 00:37:13.342
Do you have her confession?"

00:37:13.402 --> 00:37:16.213
The figure says, what?

00:37:16.273 --> 00:37:18.973
Do you have Miss Thornton's confession?

00:37:19.034 --> 00:37:20.714
The figure repeats.

00:37:20.774 --> 00:37:22.485
Branwell nods.

00:37:22.545 --> 00:37:24.146
Yes, I do.

00:37:24.206 --> 00:37:26.486
She's in the interview room now.

00:37:26.546 --> 00:37:28.427
The figure nods.

00:37:28.487 --> 00:37:29.797
Good.

00:37:29.857 --> 00:37:31.858
Then it's over.

00:37:31.918 --> 00:37:35.560
And the impossible figure turns to leave.

00:37:35.620 --> 00:37:41.083
Branwell still cannot believe it, yet the similarity is perfect.

00:37:41.143 --> 00:37:45.067
Wait, Mr. Gray, William, wait, I need to ask you.

00:37:45.127 --> 00:37:47.979
But the figure turns and smiles.

00:37:48.039 --> 00:37:54.562
It's a cold smile, but Branwell sees that the man's eyes look a little more alive now.

00:37:54.622 --> 00:37:58.976
My name is Gray Detective, but not William.

00:37:59.036 --> 00:38:01.337
My name is Matthew.

00:38:01.397 --> 00:38:03.378
Matthew Gray.

00:38:03.438 --> 00:38:06.230
William was my brother.

00:38:06.290 --> 00:38:19.464
He says as he opens the door to the rain, my twin brother How far is far enough?

00:38:19.524 --> 00:38:29.092
Jerry pushes open the door of the small Australian pub and steps onto the terrace with a pint of cold beer in his hand.

00:38:29.152 --> 00:38:40.298
Inside the pub, it's small and dark and the barman is rude and suspicious, but Jerry has to agree that the view from the terrace is more than spectacular.

00:38:40.358 --> 00:38:53.101
Red sandy plains stretch out in almost every direction, and only to the east are their thin, light green trees that lead to the low orange mountains.

00:38:53.162 --> 00:39:01.946
Jerry smiles, sits down at one of the small wooden tables and places his bag carefully next to his feet.

00:39:02.006 --> 00:39:05.347
The rest of his luggage is in the pickup truck.

00:39:05.407 --> 00:39:07.409
But not this bag.

00:39:07.469 --> 00:39:11.524
No, this one stays with him.

00:39:11.584 --> 00:39:19.424
He takes a cigarette from his packet, lights it and lets the hot evening sun shine down on his bald head.

00:39:19.484 --> 00:39:21.206
The outback.

00:39:21.266 --> 00:39:23.098
This is it.

00:39:23.158 --> 00:39:25.458
This is far enough.

00:39:25.518 --> 00:39:33.306
And for the first time in a week Jerry relaxes and lets his anxious pale face smile.

00:39:33.366 --> 00:39:38.327
You do not steal from Big Jones and stay in London.

00:39:38.387 --> 00:39:40.607
Everybody knows that.

00:39:40.667 --> 00:39:46.768
And Jerry understands better than most people exactly how true that is.

00:39:46.828 --> 00:39:53.640
No, you either get out of the city for life or Big Jones finds you.

00:39:53.700 --> 00:39:59.058
And when Big Jones finds you, that is when you go see the world.

00:39:59.118 --> 00:40:01.662
In little pieces.

00:40:01.722 --> 00:40:04.934
Most people think it's just a story.

00:40:04.994 --> 00:40:06.861
But not Jerry.

00:40:06.921 --> 00:40:07.942
No.

00:40:08.002 --> 00:40:10.662
Big Jones does not just kill you.

00:40:10.722 --> 00:40:20.594
He takes you to one of his old factories on the Thames and then he shows you the boxes and the addresses of all the different locations in the world.

00:40:20.654 --> 00:40:23.475
And then he starts to chop.

00:40:23.535 --> 00:40:27.156
Jerry feels sweat run down his back.

00:40:27.216 --> 00:40:31.825
But he laughs and takes a long drink of the cold beer.

00:40:31.885 --> 00:40:38.024
Well, Big Jones cannot chop me up if he cannot find me, he thinks.

00:40:38.085 --> 00:40:40.785
And he looks at the bag at his feet.

00:40:40.845 --> 00:40:44.176
It was easy to get it through customs.

00:40:44.236 --> 00:40:47.297
They look for drugs and weapons.

00:40:47.357 --> 00:40:52.488
They do not look for 60,000 pounds of uncut diamonds.

00:40:52.548 --> 00:40:56.609
And he smiles and looks up at the hot sun.

00:40:56.669 --> 00:40:59.592
Well, someone looks bloody happy.

00:40:59.652 --> 00:41:02.706
A voice says and Jerry jumps.

00:41:02.766 --> 00:41:07.348
He looks forwards but cannot see because of the sunlight.

00:41:07.408 --> 00:41:12.680
Then two tall strangely dressed men walk into the shade of the terrace.

00:41:12.740 --> 00:41:17.803
One with three dead rabbits in one hand and a long rifle in the other.

00:41:17.863 --> 00:41:19.264
G'day!

00:41:19.324 --> 00:41:20.914
says the other one.

00:41:20.974 --> 00:41:23.175
They both look similar.

00:41:23.235 --> 00:41:30.196
Messy blonde hair under hats that are the same colour as their khaki shorts and shirts.

00:41:30.256 --> 00:41:32.786
and they are both dirty.

00:41:32.846 --> 00:41:38.138
Their skin, boots and clothes all covered in red sand.

00:41:38.198 --> 00:41:41.120
Even him, Jerry says.

00:41:41.180 --> 00:41:46.373
Ha, a bloody palm, says the first and drops the rabbits on the table.

00:41:46.433 --> 00:41:47.393
Is that right?

00:41:47.453 --> 00:41:48.554
Are you a palm mate?

00:41:48.614 --> 00:41:49.514
A what?

00:41:49.574 --> 00:41:51.056
asks Jerry.

00:41:51.116 --> 00:41:54.412
A palm, you know, an English bloke.

00:41:54.472 --> 00:41:56.528
Oh, yes.

00:41:56.588 --> 00:42:01.562
Nice one, says the first before he disappears into the bar.

00:42:01.622 --> 00:42:06.896
Jerry is not sure, but he thinks that the man looked at the bag by his feet.

00:42:06.956 --> 00:42:08.848
Are you on holiday, mate?

00:42:08.908 --> 00:42:11.620
asks the other who sits down at the table.

00:42:11.680 --> 00:42:14.222
Yeah, something like that.

00:42:14.283 --> 00:42:15.093
Yeah?

00:42:15.153 --> 00:42:16.334
Where are you staying?

00:42:16.394 --> 00:42:18.545
There are no hotels around here.

00:42:18.606 --> 00:42:23.971
Jerry considers lying, but Big Jones can never find him here.

00:42:24.032 --> 00:42:26.694
Upper of Farmer Hobbes Creek.

00:42:26.754 --> 00:42:28.264
Oh, is that right?

00:42:28.324 --> 00:42:30.255
Me and Darwin live near there.

00:42:30.315 --> 00:42:32.016
Looks like we're neighbours.

00:42:32.076 --> 00:42:33.256
Darwin?

00:42:33.316 --> 00:42:34.907
Yeah, my bloody brother.

00:42:34.967 --> 00:42:36.538
I'm Charles, he's Darwin.

00:42:36.598 --> 00:42:38.238
Here he is with the stubby's.

00:42:38.298 --> 00:42:43.460
And the brother called Darwin comes out of the bar with two small bottles of beer.

00:42:43.520 --> 00:42:47.052
The palm is staying at the old Hobbes farm, Darwin.

00:42:47.112 --> 00:42:48.844
Well, you're no worse, then.

00:42:48.904 --> 00:42:50.265
Be careful up there, mate.

00:42:50.305 --> 00:42:52.096
That's salty territory now.

00:42:52.156 --> 00:42:54.518
And both of the brothers smile.

00:42:54.578 --> 00:42:56.280
Salty territory?

00:42:56.340 --> 00:42:57.420
What's that?

00:42:57.480 --> 00:42:58.692
Crocs, mate.

00:42:58.752 --> 00:42:59.762
Crocodiles.

00:42:59.822 --> 00:43:01.864
There are a lot of them up there in the creek.

00:43:01.924 --> 00:43:03.195
Best be careful.

00:43:03.255 --> 00:43:04.766
And they laugh again.

00:43:04.826 --> 00:43:06.638
And Jerry smiles.

00:43:06.698 --> 00:43:07.949
Yeah, sure.

00:43:08.009 --> 00:43:09.199
I'll be careful.

00:43:09.260 --> 00:43:10.241
He says.

00:43:10.301 --> 00:43:13.593
And he thinks he sees one of the brothers look at his bag again.

00:43:13.653 --> 00:43:18.064
Well, it's nice to meet you both, but I need to get up to the farm before dark.

00:43:18.124 --> 00:43:21.745
Sure mate, no worries," says Charles.

00:43:21.805 --> 00:43:23.695
Watch out for the salties, mate.

00:43:23.755 --> 00:43:26.306
They can chop you up in their little pieces.

00:43:26.366 --> 00:43:31.708
Says Darwin, and Jerry thinks about Big Jones and the old factory on the Thames.

00:43:31.768 --> 00:43:33.038
I'll be okay.

00:43:33.098 --> 00:43:34.208
See you later.

00:43:34.268 --> 00:43:37.714
He says to the brothers, then he heads for the truck.

00:43:37.774 --> 00:43:41.760
A bloody palm, says Charlie quietly.

00:43:41.821 --> 00:43:45.943
He seems to like that bag very much, says Darlene.

00:43:46.003 --> 00:43:47.414
He sure does.

00:43:47.474 --> 00:43:50.736
You know, I don't think he believes us about the salties.

00:43:50.796 --> 00:43:54.318
Bloody palms never believe us about the salties.

00:43:54.378 --> 00:44:05.181
And the brothers laugh as they watch Jerry and the truck drive up the road to Hobbes Creek, while the great Australian sun begins to fall behind the horizon.

00:44:05.241 --> 00:44:09.415
It's almost dark when Jerry reaches the farm.

00:44:09.475 --> 00:44:12.922
The building is old, but in okay shape.

00:44:12.982 --> 00:44:17.110
And he turns the generator on and the electric lights start.

00:44:17.170 --> 00:44:20.212
For half an hour he unpacks his things.

00:44:20.272 --> 00:44:25.838
Then he sits down on the terrace with his bag at his feet and lights another cigarette.

00:44:25.898 --> 00:44:35.989
The creek is 20 metres from the farmhouse, and the evening seems to be alive with insects and birds that live in the trees near the green water.

00:44:36.049 --> 00:44:40.133
Saltys, he thinks, and he laughs.

00:44:40.193 --> 00:44:49.180
He does not believe the brothers, not for a moment, but just to be safe he pulls his new gun from the bag and checks it.

00:44:49.240 --> 00:44:55.534
Then he pulls a box from the bag, opens it carefully and looks at the diamonds.

00:44:55.594 --> 00:44:58.317
Beautiful, he thinks.

00:44:58.377 --> 00:45:02.214
Beautiful, and they belong to me.

00:45:02.274 --> 00:45:08.364
And he is about to laugh again, when he hears a noise from the trees.

00:45:08.424 --> 00:45:13.806
The box shuts in an instant, and then the gun is in his hand.

00:45:13.866 --> 00:45:18.960
He thinks he sees a dark shape, but he does not shoot.

00:45:19.020 --> 00:45:20.390
Who's there?

00:45:20.451 --> 00:45:21.701
He says.

00:45:21.761 --> 00:45:23.642
But there is no answer.

00:45:23.702 --> 00:45:27.224
He picks the bag up and puts it on his shoulder.

00:45:27.284 --> 00:45:30.526
He takes a few steps towards the trees.

00:45:30.586 --> 00:45:32.129
I've got a gun.

00:45:32.189 --> 00:45:34.332
but there is silence.

00:45:34.392 --> 00:45:40.535
He tries to see what the dark shape is, but the evening light is too weak.

00:45:40.595 --> 00:45:43.056
He moves a little closer.

00:45:43.116 --> 00:45:46.328
Maybe it's the brothers, he thinks.

00:45:46.388 --> 00:45:49.980
And he remembers the way one of them looked at the bag.

00:45:50.040 --> 00:45:52.882
Maybe they want to steal his bag.

00:45:52.942 --> 00:45:54.932
Well, they can try.

00:45:54.993 --> 00:46:00.375
He raises his gun and is about to shoot when the dark shape moves again.

00:46:00.435 --> 00:46:02.246
And he laughs.

00:46:02.306 --> 00:46:05.368
It's just a small crocodile.

00:46:05.428 --> 00:46:07.070
A tiny thing.

00:46:07.130 --> 00:46:09.414
No bigger than his arm.

00:46:09.474 --> 00:46:11.807
He looks at it and laughs again.

00:46:11.827 --> 00:46:15.732
Well, you can't chop me in a little piece, can you?

00:46:15.792 --> 00:46:17.766
He says to the crocodile.

00:46:17.826 --> 00:46:21.652
And that is when he feels the knife at his neck.

00:46:21.712 --> 00:46:25.934
No, says a terrifyingly familiar voice.

00:46:25.995 --> 00:46:27.886
But I can, Jerry.

00:46:27.946 --> 00:46:29.477
Drop the gun.

00:46:29.537 --> 00:46:32.359
Jerry drops it and slowly turns.

00:46:32.419 --> 00:46:34.520
The knife still at his neck.

00:46:34.580 --> 00:46:36.462
Pig Jones.

00:46:36.522 --> 00:46:38.213
But ow.

00:46:38.273 --> 00:46:40.584
Big Jones smiles.

00:46:40.644 --> 00:46:43.920
He is tall and extremely muscly.

00:46:43.980 --> 00:46:49.166
His face is bearded and his eyes are full of violence and pain.

00:46:49.226 --> 00:46:52.017
But it's his teeth Jerry hates.

00:46:52.077 --> 00:46:56.239
Big white teeth as sharp as crocodiles.

00:46:56.300 --> 00:46:57.490
Ow!

00:46:57.550 --> 00:47:01.112
Do you think you can steal from me and escape?

00:47:01.172 --> 00:47:04.694
Jerry, you know me better than that.

00:47:04.754 --> 00:47:07.136
Please, big Jones, I'm sorry.

00:47:07.196 --> 00:47:09.168
I have all the diamonds right here.

00:47:09.228 --> 00:47:10.710
Can't you let me go?

00:47:10.770 --> 00:47:13.012
You won't ever see me again, I promise.

00:47:13.072 --> 00:47:14.673
Jerry begs.

00:47:14.733 --> 00:47:16.735
I know I won't, Jerry.

00:47:16.795 --> 00:47:19.444
Because you're going a little trip.

00:47:19.504 --> 00:47:21.602
all around the world.

00:47:21.663 --> 00:47:31.746
And Jerry remembers the old factory on the Thames and the boxes with the different addresses and the story about what Big Jones does when people steal from him.

00:47:31.806 --> 00:47:35.267
And Jerry opens his mouth to scream.

00:47:35.327 --> 00:47:38.968
But the scream he hears is not his own.

00:47:39.028 --> 00:47:44.490
The scream comes from Big Jones, who suddenly lets go of the knife.

00:47:44.550 --> 00:47:50.664
Jerry looks down and sees the small crocodile biting Big Jones' leg.

00:47:50.724 --> 00:47:58.984
Jerry laughs and he runs to his gun and points it at Big Jones who falls to the floor and tries to kick the crocodile.

00:47:59.044 --> 00:48:03.358
Don't move Big Jones, let it chop you in a little pieces.

00:48:03.418 --> 00:48:12.362
And Big Jones looks up at him and is about to shout when suddenly he looks behind Jerry and the colour disappears from his face.

00:48:12.422 --> 00:48:17.571
says Jerry, and this is the last thing he ever says.

00:48:17.631 --> 00:48:26.969
Jerry's gun fires three times into the evening light, and screams fill the creek, but there is no escape.

00:48:27.030 --> 00:48:36.188
Soon, all is quiet again, apart from the sound of three large crocodiles slowly creeping back to the green water.

00:48:36.248 --> 00:48:39.892
Their big mouths now full of dinner.

00:48:39.953 --> 00:48:45.848
and of £60,000 of uncut diamonds.

00:48:45.908 --> 00:48:52.872
The Wrong Bag The sky is not there.

00:48:52.933 --> 00:49:02.996
In its place are a thousand adverts on a hundred television screens, a sea of lights that constantly move and change.

00:49:03.056 --> 00:49:06.404
Red, white, blue, green, orange, gold.

00:49:06.464 --> 00:49:09.496
Red, white, blue, green, orange, gold.

00:49:09.556 --> 00:49:11.708
They never stop.

00:49:11.768 --> 00:49:22.563
And on the television screens there are giant faces, clips from movies, news headlines, commercials for cars and perfume, chocolate and beer.

00:49:22.624 --> 00:49:25.375
The sounds never stop.

00:49:25.436 --> 00:49:31.009
The horns of the big yellow taxis, the music from the bars and the restaurants.

00:49:31.069 --> 00:49:33.246
And the voices.

00:49:33.306 --> 00:49:48.032
so many voices, so many that they are impossible to understand, and so many languages, English, Spanish, Chinese, Italian, German, Portuguese.

00:49:48.092 --> 00:49:55.828
They all mix together and are little more than noise to an onlooker, but the smells are the worst.

00:49:55.888 --> 00:50:02.103
One moment it's the scent of hot dogs, onions, tomatoes, fries or donuts.

00:50:02.163 --> 00:50:06.024
delicious smells that make the stomach rumble.

00:50:06.084 --> 00:50:10.746
But then it's cigarettes, alcohol, garbage and vomit.

00:50:10.806 --> 00:50:16.648
And all of this mixes with the sweat of the thousands of people in one place.

00:50:16.708 --> 00:50:22.459
Jake begins to feel sick again and looks down at his dirty old sneakers.

00:50:22.519 --> 00:50:25.509
Next to him, Nick smiles.

00:50:25.570 --> 00:50:27.721
You don't like it here, do you, kid?

00:50:27.781 --> 00:50:28.872
He says.

00:50:28.932 --> 00:50:35.977
He always calls Jake Kidd, but he must be the same age as him, 12 or 13.

00:50:36.037 --> 00:50:40.090
It's okay, Jake says, and looks up again.

00:50:40.150 --> 00:50:43.142
He has to be stronger, he knows.

00:50:43.202 --> 00:50:45.784
He has to be more like Nick.

00:50:45.844 --> 00:50:47.836
You're just hungry.

00:50:47.896 --> 00:50:52.144
After we eat, you will think this place is the best in the world.

00:50:52.204 --> 00:50:56.024
With a full belly, you can sit and watch this all day.

00:50:56.084 --> 00:50:59.527
It's just, well, why don't we come at night?

00:50:59.588 --> 00:51:01.949
Isn't it better at night, Nick?

00:51:02.010 --> 00:51:03.741
How dumb are you?

00:51:03.802 --> 00:51:06.914
His new friend says, and he looks annoyed.

00:51:06.975 --> 00:51:09.146
At night they worry.

00:51:09.206 --> 00:51:12.370
At night everyone worries in New York.

00:51:12.430 --> 00:51:17.528
In the day when it's hot and sunny like today, they don't worry about anything.

00:51:17.589 --> 00:51:22.258
They just watch those big screens, take pictures, eat and drink.

00:51:22.318 --> 00:51:24.530
They don't even look at us twice.

00:51:24.591 --> 00:51:27.424
And Jake knows that he's right.

00:51:27.484 --> 00:51:28.905
Look, it's easy.

00:51:28.965 --> 00:51:31.238
You do what I say, no problem.

00:51:31.298 --> 00:51:33.840
And Nick looks at him for a moment.

00:51:33.900 --> 00:51:35.902
You look okay now.

00:51:35.962 --> 00:51:38.386
These new clothes ain't bad.

00:51:38.446 --> 00:51:42.370
The new clothes are not new, Jake knows.

00:51:42.430 --> 00:51:43.860
Not really.

00:51:43.920 --> 00:51:48.473
Nick steals clothes from the washing-lines on the backstreets.

00:51:48.533 --> 00:51:55.627
But they are better than the dirty old jeans and green jacket that are now in a bin behind the shop where they sit.

00:51:55.687 --> 00:51:57.958
You have to look good, kid.

00:51:58.018 --> 00:51:59.279
Remember that.

00:51:59.339 --> 00:52:02.880
You have to look like them, he says.

00:52:02.940 --> 00:52:04.731
Then they don't worry.

00:52:04.791 --> 00:52:06.562
You can get near to them.

00:52:06.622 --> 00:52:07.822
Be casual.

00:52:07.882 --> 00:52:09.323
Look up at the big screens.

00:52:09.383 --> 00:52:13.085
It's good if you get a camera out and pretend to take some snaps, you know?

00:52:13.145 --> 00:52:16.126
Then when you get the chance, you do it.

00:52:16.187 --> 00:52:18.148
You grab a bag.

00:52:18.208 --> 00:52:19.948
A woman's bag is the best.

00:52:19.988 --> 00:52:21.649
They have more things in them.

00:52:21.709 --> 00:52:24.290
But a guy's bag is easier.

00:52:24.350 --> 00:52:27.540
A guy's bag you can just walk away with.

00:52:27.600 --> 00:52:31.249
If you get a woman's bag, you sometimes have to run.

00:52:31.309 --> 00:52:33.900
Jake feels really sick now.

00:52:33.960 --> 00:52:39.091
The sun is somewhere up there above the skyscrapers of Times Square.

00:52:39.151 --> 00:52:41.671
And the afternoon is hot.

00:52:41.731 --> 00:52:45.542
The smells and sounds make a horrible mixture.

00:52:45.602 --> 00:52:48.633
He thinks about home for a moment.

00:52:48.693 --> 00:52:52.784
But that is too far away now, and too long ago.

00:52:52.844 --> 00:52:55.054
You want to eat, don't you, kid?

00:52:55.114 --> 00:52:56.700
Jake nods.

00:52:56.760 --> 00:52:59.176
And you remember where the cameras are?

00:52:59.236 --> 00:53:01.246
The NYPD ones?

00:53:01.306 --> 00:53:03.147
The others are nothing.

00:53:03.207 --> 00:53:07.708
But don't let the NYPD ones see you or you're not coming back here, not with me.

00:53:07.768 --> 00:53:09.669
Jake nods again.

00:53:09.729 --> 00:53:13.320
So we do it now and we meet back on 9th Avenue.

00:53:13.380 --> 00:53:14.561
You got it?

00:53:14.621 --> 00:53:15.891
Nick says.

00:53:15.952 --> 00:53:17.972
They both stand up.

00:53:18.032 --> 00:53:22.313
But before Nick moves into the crowds, Jake stops him.

00:53:22.374 --> 00:53:24.734
But what if someone sees me?

00:53:24.794 --> 00:53:26.508
What if they stop me?

00:53:26.568 --> 00:53:28.193
Nick smiles.

00:53:28.253 --> 00:53:30.995
No worries just see it's the wrong bag.

00:53:31.055 --> 00:53:32.836
And then he goes.

00:53:32.896 --> 00:53:34.888
And Jake is alone.

00:53:34.948 --> 00:53:38.009
He moves out into the crowds of people.

00:53:38.070 --> 00:53:41.852
He knows where he has to go to the red stairs.

00:53:41.913 --> 00:53:44.251
He moves slowly.

00:53:44.311 --> 00:53:46.288
No one really sees him.

00:53:46.348 --> 00:53:47.759
No one really looks.

00:53:47.819 --> 00:53:49.239
But he sees.

00:53:49.299 --> 00:53:50.449
He looks.

00:53:50.510 --> 00:53:52.000
He watches.

00:53:52.060 --> 00:53:56.231
He sees kids his own age laughing and joking.

00:53:56.291 --> 00:54:00.882
He looks at the food on the small tables in front of the cafes and bars.

00:54:00.942 --> 00:54:10.535
He sees two policemen in the distance and he moves the other way, walking between families and couples, businessmen and tourists.

00:54:10.595 --> 00:54:16.109
When he reaches the red steps, he is hot and sweaty, hungry too.

00:54:16.169 --> 00:54:19.161
But you can't think about that, he tells himself.

00:54:19.222 --> 00:54:22.084
He needs to think clearly now.

00:54:22.144 --> 00:54:24.786
He needs to think like Nick.

00:54:24.846 --> 00:54:29.690
On the red steps, there is a group of maybe 30 Japanese tourists.

00:54:29.750 --> 00:54:33.160
They all have a camera and are all taking photos.

00:54:33.220 --> 00:54:38.210
but their bags are on their shoulders, and there are too many of them, he knows.

00:54:38.270 --> 00:54:46.182
Most do not see anything, but one always sees something, and one is enough.

00:54:46.242 --> 00:54:47.842
So who?

00:54:47.902 --> 00:54:50.793
And then he sees the man.

00:54:50.853 --> 00:54:54.024
He looks like any other foreign tourist.

00:54:54.084 --> 00:54:59.915
He has a camera around his neck, sunglasses on his face, and a map in his hand.

00:54:59.975 --> 00:55:03.516
Except, Jake is not sure he is a tourist.

00:55:03.576 --> 00:55:06.016
There is something different about him.

00:55:06.076 --> 00:55:10.588
The man does not use the camera and does not look at the map.

00:55:10.648 --> 00:55:13.768
In fact, the man does nothing at all.

00:55:13.828 --> 00:55:17.099
Occasionally he just looks at his watch.

00:55:17.159 --> 00:55:20.240
But the bag is by his feet.

00:55:20.300 --> 00:55:24.326
It looks expensive, made from black leather.

00:55:24.386 --> 00:55:27.054
Nick calls them weekend bags.

00:55:27.114 --> 00:55:29.327
He says they are the best.

00:55:29.387 --> 00:55:34.244
Inside them there are wallets, phones, tablets, everything.

00:55:34.304 --> 00:55:35.526
Okay.

00:55:35.587 --> 00:55:37.358
Okay, you can do this.

00:55:37.418 --> 00:55:39.196
Jake tells himself.

00:55:39.256 --> 00:55:42.092
He just needs to be quick.

00:55:42.152 --> 00:55:48.614
The man never even looks at the bag, so Jake begins to move in his direction, casually.

00:55:48.674 --> 00:55:52.195
He looks up at the lights and the television screens.

00:55:52.255 --> 00:55:56.336
Then he is near enough, and he looks at the man.

00:55:56.396 --> 00:56:00.087
He thinks again that he is a strange tourist.

00:56:00.147 --> 00:56:03.472
From here, Jake can see that he looks nervous.

00:56:03.533 --> 00:56:08.892
But then the man looks to the right and Jake knows it is his best chance.

00:56:08.952 --> 00:56:11.716
He grabs the bag and moves away.

00:56:11.776 --> 00:56:15.750
He expects to hear a shout, but there is nothing.

00:56:15.810 --> 00:56:19.444
So he moves into the crowds like Nick does.

00:56:19.504 --> 00:56:26.704
He wants to run, but he walks and notices that the bag is heavy, really heavy.

00:56:26.764 --> 00:56:35.022
And he begins to imagine Nick's face when he sees the bag, and he already begins to imagine the food that he can buy.

00:56:35.082 --> 00:56:38.614
But the bag is heavy, he thinks again.

00:56:38.674 --> 00:56:40.415
So heavy.

00:56:40.475 --> 00:56:42.126
And he slows.

00:56:42.186 --> 00:56:46.468
Maybe he should look inside, he thinks, just to make sure.

00:56:46.528 --> 00:56:50.801
He looks around and steps into the doorway of a shop for a moment.

00:56:50.861 --> 00:56:54.258
Then he kneels down and opens the bag.

00:56:54.318 --> 00:56:57.464
and for a moment he does not understand.

00:56:57.524 --> 00:57:02.748
There is no wallet, no phone, no tablet, no clothes.

00:57:02.808 --> 00:57:04.818
There is only?

00:57:04.878 --> 00:57:12.113
Then he hears the man shout, and he looks up and sees his angry face running towards him through the crowd.

00:57:12.173 --> 00:57:15.274
He has no time to think, but he must.

00:57:15.334 --> 00:57:17.156
Can he leave the bag?

00:57:17.216 --> 00:57:19.206
Can he just run away?

00:57:19.266 --> 00:57:20.808
Run away?

00:57:20.868 --> 00:57:25.382
When he knows what the man is going to do, No.

00:57:25.442 --> 00:57:27.374
Not this time.

00:57:27.435 --> 00:57:32.179
And then he is up and runs too and he has the bag in his hands.

00:57:32.239 --> 00:57:36.243
For a moment he has no idea what to do or where to go.

00:57:36.263 --> 00:57:40.876
He thinks that maybe Nick would know that Nick would have some advice.

00:57:40.937 --> 00:57:43.970
And then he realizes that he does know.

00:57:43.990 --> 00:57:47.082
And you remember where the cameras are?

00:57:47.142 --> 00:57:49.224
The NYPD ones?

00:57:49.284 --> 00:57:52.295
Yes, I remember, Jake thinks.

00:57:52.356 --> 00:57:55.820
But can he get there before the man catches him?

00:57:55.880 --> 00:58:00.432
There is another shout from behind him, but Jake keeps moving.

00:58:00.492 --> 00:58:09.364
Above the crowd he can see the NYPD camera on top of a tall post, and he pushes past the tourists to reach it.

00:58:09.424 --> 00:58:15.156
For a moment there are people all around him, and he thinks he will never get there.

00:58:15.216 --> 00:58:21.657
But then he pushes past some kids who shout at him and he is there, directly below the camera.

00:58:21.717 --> 00:58:22.888
And now?

00:58:22.948 --> 00:58:24.150
Now what?

00:58:24.210 --> 00:58:30.640
He hears another shout and knows that the man is nearly here, but the camera is not looking at him.

00:58:30.700 --> 00:58:33.802
It is turning slowly in the other direction.

00:58:33.862 --> 00:58:34.724
Hey!

00:58:34.784 --> 00:58:37.667
He shouts and he begins to jump up and down.

00:58:37.727 --> 00:58:38.798
Down here!

00:58:38.858 --> 00:58:40.080
Look down here!

00:58:40.140 --> 00:58:43.264
But he has no idea if anyone can hear him.

00:58:43.324 --> 00:58:50.227
He opens the bag for everyone to see and there is a cry from a woman near to him, followed by another and another.

00:58:50.287 --> 00:58:53.308
This must be why the camera turns.

00:58:53.368 --> 00:59:00.279
Jake holds up the bag, but he is scared, so scared, though he has to do it.

00:59:00.340 --> 00:59:09.004
That's mine, the man's voice says behind him, and there are more cries from the crowd and everyone seems to know what is happening now.

00:59:09.064 --> 00:59:11.748
Give it to me," the man says.

00:59:11.808 --> 00:59:14.801
Jake looks at the bag one more time.

00:59:14.861 --> 00:59:18.894
What he sees inside looks just like from the movies.

00:59:18.954 --> 00:59:24.148
The clock, the wires and the heavy packs of something horrible and dangerous.

00:59:24.208 --> 00:59:28.131
Jake wants to give it to the man because he is so scared.

00:59:28.191 --> 00:59:29.702
But he will not.

00:59:29.762 --> 00:59:31.894
No, he answers.

00:59:31.954 --> 00:59:35.976
Then someone in the crowd says something and the man looks around.

00:59:36.036 --> 00:59:39.923
The scared cries now become angry shouts.

00:59:39.983 --> 00:59:43.580
Jake is pushed and he falls and loses the bag.

00:59:43.640 --> 00:59:47.490
He thinks that the people are shouting at him and he wants to explain.

00:59:47.551 --> 00:59:51.317
But it was not the man who pushed Jake and took the bag.

00:59:51.377 --> 00:59:53.088
It was a policeman.

00:59:53.149 --> 00:59:57.441
The man is underneath four NYPD officers.

00:59:57.501 --> 01:00:01.574
Jake now realizes that the crowd is not shouting.

01:00:01.634 --> 01:00:03.284
It is cheering.

01:00:03.344 --> 01:00:08.538
And a police officer helped him to stand and says something about him being a hero.

01:00:08.598 --> 01:00:10.539
How did you know, kid?"

01:00:10.599 --> 01:00:15.832
A voice says, but this time it's not Nick.

01:00:15.892 --> 01:00:18.564
I didn't, he manages to say.

01:00:18.624 --> 01:00:22.146
It's just the wrong bag.

01:00:22.206 --> 01:00:24.808
The perfect murder.

01:00:24.868 --> 01:00:29.671
The QM2 darling is the finest ocean liner in the world.

01:00:29.731 --> 01:00:35.547
Sure, there are one or two bigger than her, but none of them has her romance or her luxury.

01:00:35.607 --> 01:00:40.241
Mrs. Carolina Heath says as they enter the dining room.

01:00:40.301 --> 01:00:43.812
Her young new friend only smiles.

01:00:43.872 --> 01:00:47.544
She is a quiet young thing and beautiful too.

01:00:47.604 --> 01:00:51.426
And Carolina likes to have beautiful things around her.

01:00:51.486 --> 01:00:54.788
So she is more than happy to talk for both of them.

01:00:54.848 --> 01:00:57.339
This is your first cruise, you say?

01:00:57.399 --> 01:00:59.290
You're going to love it.

01:00:59.350 --> 01:01:01.711
Just look at the view out there.

01:01:01.771 --> 01:01:14.657
and from the large glass windows they can see the majestic figure of the statue of Liberty as the ship slowly moves away from the port of New York and heads into the deep of the ocean.

01:01:14.717 --> 01:01:22.731
It's fantastic, Eleanor says, and again Carolina is very happy to have a new companion.

01:01:22.791 --> 01:01:26.032
The dining room is magnificent.

01:01:26.092 --> 01:01:31.410
The tables are covered in white and the lights are low and atmospheric.

01:01:31.470 --> 01:01:35.708
The band in the corner is playing soft, classical music.

01:01:35.768 --> 01:01:38.860
Well, I wonder what the other people at our table are like.

01:01:38.920 --> 01:01:41.871
Oh my, look, here we are, table six.

01:01:41.932 --> 01:01:45.624
Well, good evening, gentlemen, Carolina says.

01:01:45.684 --> 01:01:49.496
And the three men stop their conversation and smile.

01:01:49.556 --> 01:01:54.060
Mrs. Heath, one says, how good to see you again.

01:01:54.120 --> 01:01:57.431
Another evening of champagne and dancing?

01:01:57.492 --> 01:02:01.514
Every night, Peter, my doctor tells me that I must.

01:02:01.574 --> 01:02:02.754
She laughs.

01:02:02.814 --> 01:02:07.738
Gentlemen, let me introduce you to my new friend, Miss Eleanor Chance.

01:02:07.798 --> 01:02:10.569
Eleanor only nods and smiles.

01:02:10.629 --> 01:02:14.121
And these, Eleanor, well, how to describe them.

01:02:14.181 --> 01:02:16.742
I suppose every ship needs some pirates.

01:02:16.802 --> 01:02:18.133
And these are they.

01:02:18.193 --> 01:02:20.874
Meet Peter, Edward, and Michael.

01:02:20.934 --> 01:02:23.936
The men stand and nod politely.

01:02:23.996 --> 01:02:29.179
And Carolina is amused by the interested looks they give Eleanor Chance.

01:02:29.240 --> 01:02:32.172
Pirates, Eleanor asks.

01:02:32.232 --> 01:02:34.674
Well, maybe not pirates.

01:02:34.734 --> 01:02:36.885
But you should still be careful.

01:02:36.945 --> 01:02:38.946
They are in fact writers.

01:02:39.007 --> 01:02:41.108
And I don't know which is worse.

01:02:41.168 --> 01:02:44.064
Writers, how interesting.

01:02:44.124 --> 01:02:45.628
Please sit down."

01:02:45.688 --> 01:02:50.201
Edward says with a smile that shows early signs of infatuation.

01:02:50.261 --> 01:02:51.372
No, I can't.

01:02:51.432 --> 01:02:52.762
I must find the captain.

01:02:52.822 --> 01:02:56.264
He promised to tell me all about the new people on board today.

01:02:56.324 --> 01:02:59.576
You know how I must know who I'm sailing with.

01:02:59.636 --> 01:03:01.507
She looks at the men.

01:03:01.568 --> 01:03:04.929
Oh, but can I trust you three to look after Eleanor?

01:03:04.989 --> 01:03:07.971
This is her first night and her first cruise.

01:03:08.031 --> 01:03:13.330
She does not need to hear any of your horrible stories or strange ideas.

01:03:13.390 --> 01:03:16.449
Oh, well, I suppose we can change the subject.

01:03:16.509 --> 01:03:19.482
But we are having a really interesting conversation.

01:03:19.542 --> 01:03:21.424
Carolina laughs.

01:03:21.484 --> 01:03:24.728
I don't want to know, just be nice.

01:03:24.788 --> 01:03:27.240
And she smiles at Eleanor.

01:03:27.301 --> 01:03:31.044
If they scare you, just scream, and I can rescue you.

01:03:31.104 --> 01:03:33.246
Oh, I think I can look after myself.

01:03:33.306 --> 01:03:34.873
Responds Eleanor.

01:03:34.933 --> 01:03:39.340
And again Carolina thinks what a sweet lovely thing she is.

01:03:39.400 --> 01:03:42.722
She must be only 25 or 26.

01:03:42.782 --> 01:03:48.245
Her long blonde hair is elegant and her face full of classical beauty.

01:03:48.305 --> 01:03:50.116
And those eyes.

01:03:50.176 --> 01:03:53.138
Such innocent blue eyes.

01:03:53.198 --> 01:03:57.496
Yes, a lovely new companion she thinks as she leaves.

01:03:57.556 --> 01:04:03.344
At the table, Edward offers Eleanor a chair, and Michael passes her a glass of champagne.

01:04:03.404 --> 01:04:05.585
So, are you all writers?

01:04:05.645 --> 01:04:06.856
She asks.

01:04:06.916 --> 01:04:10.567
Yes, says Peter, of different types.

01:04:10.628 --> 01:04:15.009
I'm in film, Edward here as a novelist, and Michael writes plays.

01:04:15.070 --> 01:04:20.700
Fascinating, she says, and all three look happy that she thinks so.

01:04:20.760 --> 01:04:24.760
And what is this interesting conversation that I'm interrupting?

01:04:24.820 --> 01:04:26.561
Oh, we can't bore you with that.

01:04:26.621 --> 01:04:29.362
Please tell us about yourself," says Edward.

01:04:29.422 --> 01:04:37.305
Eleanor smiles shyly and moves the elegant white silk scarf that lies around her neck above her black dress.

01:04:37.365 --> 01:04:43.950
There is very little to say, I'm sailing from here to Southampton, then I intend to stay in England for some time.

01:04:44.010 --> 01:04:46.001
For the summer, perhaps.

01:04:46.061 --> 01:04:51.596
Then she is quiet, and the three men think how polite and gentle she seems.

01:04:51.656 --> 01:04:54.124
I see, says Peter.

01:04:54.184 --> 01:04:56.353
Well, this is a tradition of ours.

01:04:56.413 --> 01:04:59.285
Every year we take a cruise on Old Mary.

01:04:59.345 --> 01:05:01.456
This is the sixth year.

01:05:01.516 --> 01:05:04.578
A good escape from editors and deadlines.

01:05:04.638 --> 01:05:07.520
And wives, says Michael.

01:05:07.580 --> 01:05:11.323
Well, for them, not for me, I'm not married, says Edward.

01:05:11.383 --> 01:05:13.104
A little too quickly.

01:05:13.164 --> 01:05:18.828
For a moment, there's an awkward silence, and Peter and Michael exchange a smile.

01:05:18.888 --> 01:05:24.220
Well, our conversation is about murder," says Peter to fill the silence.

01:05:24.280 --> 01:05:26.251
The perfect murder.

01:05:26.311 --> 01:05:28.032
Eleanor smiles.

01:05:28.092 --> 01:05:29.322
I see.

01:05:29.382 --> 01:05:32.974
I'm sure she's not interested, Pete, says Edward.

01:05:33.034 --> 01:05:34.394
Oh, but I am.

01:05:34.454 --> 01:05:38.964
Mrs. Heath is lovely, but she only talks about scandal and gossip.

01:05:39.024 --> 01:05:40.502
See, Edward?

01:05:40.562 --> 01:05:42.594
We can continue to argue.

01:05:42.654 --> 01:05:44.334
Michael jokes.

01:05:44.394 --> 01:05:49.476
You see, we all have very different ideas about what the perfect murder is.

01:05:49.536 --> 01:05:54.588
Peter thinks that for a film, there must be something visual about the murder weapon.

01:05:54.649 --> 01:05:59.010
For me, the important things are the character and the motive.

01:05:59.070 --> 01:06:03.862
And for me, Edward says as he refills Eleanor's glass.

01:06:03.922 --> 01:06:06.544
The important thing is the twist.

01:06:06.604 --> 01:06:07.978
The twist.

01:06:08.038 --> 01:06:12.406
You know, the surprise at the end of the story, the thing you don't expect.

01:06:12.466 --> 01:06:15.428
Eleanor smiles and takes a drink.

01:06:15.488 --> 01:06:21.984
The Statue of Liberty is still visible in the distance, but they will soon be alone on the dark ocean.

01:06:22.044 --> 01:06:24.366
You know, I think you're all correct.

01:06:24.426 --> 01:06:29.156
In fact, I have an idea for a story, an idea I think about occasionally.

01:06:29.216 --> 01:06:31.185
Really, says Peter.

01:06:31.245 --> 01:06:32.777
Well, you must tell us.

01:06:32.838 --> 01:06:36.241
Maybe, she says with another shy smile.

01:06:36.302 --> 01:06:40.386
But you first, what's your idea for the perfect murder weapon?

01:06:40.446 --> 01:06:41.848
Peter laughs.

01:06:41.908 --> 01:06:46.524
Okay, well, it's not my idea and it's old, but I think it's great for a film.

01:06:46.584 --> 01:06:48.199
It's an icicle.

01:06:48.259 --> 01:06:50.264
The other two men laugh.

01:06:50.324 --> 01:06:51.985
He always says this.

01:06:52.045 --> 01:06:53.846
Edward informs Eleanor.

01:06:53.906 --> 01:06:55.667
Because it's perfect.

01:06:55.727 --> 01:06:59.619
After the murderer kills the victim, they just watch the weapon melt.

01:06:59.679 --> 01:07:02.110
No fingerprints, no evidence.

01:07:02.170 --> 01:07:03.861
What do you think, Eleanor?

01:07:03.921 --> 01:07:05.222
She smiles.

01:07:05.282 --> 01:07:09.746
It's very clever, but, well, I prefer fire.

01:07:09.806 --> 01:07:10.986
Fire?

01:07:11.046 --> 01:07:15.060
Yes, fire kills, and it destroys the evidence.

01:07:15.120 --> 01:07:17.732
And also a fire can look like an accident.

01:07:17.792 --> 01:07:19.854
The icicle never can.

01:07:19.915 --> 01:07:22.006
Haha, she's right!

01:07:22.067 --> 01:07:23.527
laughs, Michael.

01:07:23.588 --> 01:07:26.180
So what about characters and motive?

01:07:26.240 --> 01:07:27.511
He continues.

01:07:27.571 --> 01:07:31.274
A beautiful young couple, who are happy and in love.

01:07:31.335 --> 01:07:33.102
What, and he kills her?

01:07:33.162 --> 01:07:35.090
No, she kills him.

01:07:35.150 --> 01:07:36.221
Let me guess.

01:07:36.281 --> 01:07:37.062
They're rich.

01:07:37.122 --> 01:07:38.413
She wants his money.

01:07:38.473 --> 01:07:40.465
Eleanor takes another drink.

01:07:40.525 --> 01:07:43.097
No, she's the rich one.

01:07:43.157 --> 01:07:44.758
So what's the motive?

01:07:44.819 --> 01:07:46.179
Michael asks.

01:07:46.240 --> 01:07:47.470
Wait a minute.

01:07:47.530 --> 01:07:48.962
Interrupts Peter.

01:07:49.022 --> 01:07:51.184
You say she kills him.

01:07:51.244 --> 01:07:52.575
With fire.

01:07:52.635 --> 01:07:53.605
How?

01:07:53.666 --> 01:07:56.581
She locks him in a room in a burning house.

01:07:56.641 --> 01:07:59.026
No, because then he might escape.

01:07:59.086 --> 01:08:02.352
She drugs him with sleeping pills during dinner.

01:08:02.412 --> 01:08:05.176
She always goes for a run in the evening.

01:08:05.236 --> 01:08:12.536
But this time she waits until he falls asleep in a chair in the lounge and she puts a candle to the curtains.

01:08:12.596 --> 01:08:17.058
Then she goes for her run and makes sure that many people see her.

01:08:17.118 --> 01:08:19.109
Peter nods his head.

01:08:19.168 --> 01:08:24.140
Interesting, but a coroner will find traces of sleeping pills in the blood.

01:08:24.200 --> 01:08:25.220
They'll know.

01:08:25.280 --> 01:08:27.370
No, not with this type of pill.

01:08:27.430 --> 01:08:30.981
At a certain temperature it completely burns away.

01:08:31.041 --> 01:08:33.872
Only a small trace of it can be detected.

01:08:33.932 --> 01:08:36.672
And if they are detected, so what?

01:08:36.732 --> 01:08:38.413
They are his pills.

01:08:38.473 --> 01:08:40.754
Peter laughs this time.

01:08:40.815 --> 01:08:42.426
Excellent.

01:08:42.486 --> 01:08:48.761
Very clever, Edward agrees, but unfortunately the wife is always a suspect in a murder.

01:08:48.820 --> 01:08:56.266
Normally yes, says Eleanor, and all three men notice how her smile fades a little.

01:08:56.326 --> 01:08:58.501
But she has a plan for that.

01:08:58.561 --> 01:09:02.207
You see, she runs until she hears the fire engines.

01:09:02.268 --> 01:09:04.769
Then she returns to the house.

01:09:04.830 --> 01:09:07.562
There are people there already, neighbors.

01:09:07.622 --> 01:09:10.065
And this is just what she wants.

01:09:10.124 --> 01:09:14.198
They try to stop her, but she runs into the burning building.

01:09:14.258 --> 01:09:15.890
No one follows her.

01:09:15.949 --> 01:09:17.041
They can't.

01:09:17.101 --> 01:09:18.953
They know it's too dangerous.

01:09:19.012 --> 01:09:24.619
And so she goes to her husband as the flames spread over his body and she watches.

01:09:24.679 --> 01:09:28.836
And while she watches, she lets the flames touch her too.

01:09:28.896 --> 01:09:33.062
They reach out and they burn her back and her neck and her hair.

01:09:33.122 --> 01:09:38.018
And at this point she screams and runs from the building and falls to the floor.

01:09:38.078 --> 01:09:40.582
Eleanor stops speaking.

01:09:40.641 --> 01:09:44.225
And for a moment there is silence at the table.

01:09:44.284 --> 01:09:46.786
My God," says Edward.

01:09:46.846 --> 01:09:49.408
She lets the flames burn her.

01:09:49.467 --> 01:09:53.811
Michael takes a moment to think about it and then nods.

01:09:53.871 --> 01:09:55.242
It's perfect.

01:09:55.302 --> 01:09:57.914
The perfect way to avoid suspicion.

01:09:57.974 --> 01:10:02.958
Everyone thinks it's an accident and that she is heartbroken, says Peter.

01:10:03.018 --> 01:10:05.770
Yes, Eleanor says.

01:10:05.830 --> 01:10:09.313
And for a moment more, there is silence again.

01:10:09.373 --> 01:10:14.628
Then the band starts to play a waltz, and Eleanor looks at Edward.

01:10:14.688 --> 01:10:16.920
I don't suppose you dance, Edward.

01:10:16.979 --> 01:10:19.232
And the writer goes a little red.

01:10:19.292 --> 01:10:20.594
Actually, I do.

01:10:20.654 --> 01:10:22.095
Would you like to join me?

01:10:22.155 --> 01:10:25.258
Eleanor smiles, and they both stand up.

01:10:25.318 --> 01:10:28.612
She adjusts the white scarf around her neck.

01:10:28.671 --> 01:10:30.764
But what about the woman's motive?

01:10:30.824 --> 01:10:32.201
Michael asks.

01:10:32.261 --> 01:10:33.829
Eleanor Smiles.

01:10:33.889 --> 01:10:36.061
Yes, that's the difficult part.

01:10:36.121 --> 01:10:38.323
And I'm afraid I just don't know.

01:10:38.383 --> 01:10:43.195
I suppose sometimes a person just enjoys murder.

01:10:43.255 --> 01:10:51.736
And as Eleanor and Edward move away to the dance floor, Peter and Michael see her adjust the scarf around her neck one last time.

01:10:51.796 --> 01:11:02.770
And in the low romantic light of the ballroom, they both think they see something strange on her back and neck, something which makes them stop and look at each other.

01:11:02.830 --> 01:11:06.701
But no, it cannot be what they think it is.

01:11:06.761 --> 01:11:11.662
It cannot be burnt skin under the elegant white scarf.

01:11:11.722 --> 01:11:14.188
Can it?

01:11:14.248 --> 01:11:18.214
Case closed.

01:11:18.274 --> 01:11:24.106
Normally Nick likes his job, but not today.

01:11:24.166 --> 01:11:34.652
Today he just wants to get out of the hot courtroom away from the serious lawyers in their black gowns and white wigs, and go and enjoy the sun.

01:11:34.712 --> 01:11:37.004
It's a good job, he thinks.

01:11:37.064 --> 01:11:41.666
It's better than serving drinks in a pub or helping out at his dad's office.

01:11:41.726 --> 01:11:46.410
but only if it's a short day and only if the case is closed quickly.

01:11:46.470 --> 01:11:51.252
But today it's nearly three and the case is nowhere near closed.

01:11:51.312 --> 01:11:54.164
Still he likes the title of his job.

01:11:54.224 --> 01:11:56.986
Court reporter sounds good.

01:11:57.046 --> 01:11:58.766
It sounds important.

01:11:58.826 --> 01:12:03.578
And that is exactly how he describes it to any girl that asks.

01:12:03.638 --> 01:12:16.089
In reality, however, all he does is press play on the recording device and write down a few notes about when the judge enters, when the prosecution or defence speaks, or what the final verdict is.

01:12:16.149 --> 01:12:22.720
And sometimes the cases are interesting, and he likes having some good stories to tell the guys in the pub.

01:12:22.780 --> 01:12:26.591
He also enjoys deciding who is innocent and who is guilty.

01:12:26.651 --> 01:12:29.268
But today, that's too easy.

01:12:29.328 --> 01:12:32.566
So he looks out of the window and waits for the judge.

01:12:32.626 --> 01:12:39.902
Sometimes he looks across at the defendant's sister, a young woman with long dark hair and sad eyes.

01:12:39.962 --> 01:12:47.640
But only sometimes, because the defendant and the rest of her family, the Lee family, are wild.

01:12:47.700 --> 01:12:51.362
And he does not want to make her or them angry.

01:12:51.422 --> 01:12:57.848
They have a terrible reputation in Bristol, and there is not one of them that does not scare Nick.

01:12:57.908 --> 01:13:01.942
All rise, the court clerk says.

01:13:02.002 --> 01:13:06.704
And the jury, the lawyers, and the family of the defendant all stand.

01:13:06.764 --> 01:13:11.257
Nick does too, and he remembers to press the button on the device.

01:13:11.317 --> 01:13:15.650
He forgot to do it once, and they refused to pay him for the day.

01:13:15.710 --> 01:13:22.934
Please be seated, says the judge, a woman of about 40 years of age with a serious face and tone.

01:13:22.994 --> 01:13:25.456
My summary of this case is brief.

01:13:25.516 --> 01:13:28.122
The jury know all the details.

01:13:28.182 --> 01:13:34.362
On Wednesday, the 5th of January, Mrs. Dawson went to the house of her sister, the defendant, Miss Lee.

01:13:34.422 --> 01:13:37.794
Mrs. Dawson was bleeding from her nose and mouth.

01:13:37.854 --> 01:13:40.386
She also had marks on her face.

01:13:40.446 --> 01:13:45.910
She says her husband, the deceased Mr. Dawson, hit her there several times.

01:13:45.970 --> 01:13:51.881
There are pictures of these injuries, but there is no proof that Mr. Dawson hit Mrs. Dawson.

01:13:51.941 --> 01:13:54.358
However, that is not why you are here.

01:13:54.418 --> 01:14:03.462
You are here because when Miss Lee saw her young sister, she took a pair of scissors from her kitchen table, said something to her sister and left the house.

01:14:03.522 --> 01:14:06.984
The words, she said, were, I will kill him.

01:14:07.044 --> 01:14:10.096
But the defendant says she does not remember that.

01:14:10.156 --> 01:14:15.148
Several people saw the defendant walking around Bristol, looking for Mr. Dawson.

01:14:15.208 --> 01:14:17.300
She stopped twice.

01:14:17.360 --> 01:14:22.526
once at the house of one of Mrs. Dawson's friends and once at a local pub.

01:14:22.586 --> 01:14:29.672
Both times she had the scissors with her, and both times witnesses testified that she said, I will kill him.

01:14:29.732 --> 01:14:35.838
At half past two in the afternoon on the 5th of January, Miss Stella Lee found Mr. Dawson.

01:14:35.898 --> 01:14:38.590
He was leaving a café with a friend.

01:14:38.650 --> 01:14:44.286
The friend, Mr. Harris, says that Miss Lee did not say anything to Mr. Dawson.

01:14:44.345 --> 01:14:48.009
and that the only thing Mr. Dawson said was hello.

01:14:48.069 --> 01:14:55.964
Miss Lee then stabbed Mr. Dawson in the neck with the scissors, and he died in seconds due to loss of blood.

01:14:56.024 --> 01:14:59.987
Miss Lee waited until the police came and was arrested.

01:15:00.047 --> 01:15:04.650
Ladies and gentlemen of the jury, you must decide only one thing.

01:15:04.710 --> 01:15:09.108
The defence says that this is not murder but manslaughter.

01:15:09.168 --> 01:15:15.393
They say this is the case because Miss Lee was not in the sane state of mind when she killed Mr. Dawson.

01:15:15.453 --> 01:15:19.024
They say that this crime was in defence of her sister.

01:15:19.084 --> 01:15:22.034
The prosecution says this is not true.

01:15:22.094 --> 01:15:28.125
They say this is premeditated murder because for half an hour Miss Lee looked for Mr. Dawson.

01:15:28.185 --> 01:15:32.946
They also say she made her intention clear on three different occasions.

01:15:33.006 --> 01:15:37.464
Please leave us now and take some time to consider these events.

01:15:37.524 --> 01:15:41.766
All rise," the court clerk says again.

01:15:41.826 --> 01:15:49.475
The judge leaves, the jury returns to its room to make a verdict and the Lee family exit to wait in the lobby.

01:15:49.535 --> 01:15:51.688
Nick sighs.

01:15:51.747 --> 01:15:57.775
It is obvious what the decision will be, but it looks like he will be here until four now.

01:15:57.835 --> 01:16:02.038
Cigarette time then, he thinks, and he leaves the courtroom.

01:16:02.098 --> 01:16:04.660
He has at least ten minutes.

01:16:04.720 --> 01:16:08.292
No jury can make a decision in less than ten minutes.

01:16:08.352 --> 01:16:15.346
He takes the back stairs, passes three or four lawyers in their black gowns and white wigs, and feels sorry for them.

01:16:15.406 --> 01:16:17.506
It is so hot today.

01:16:17.566 --> 01:16:25.109
Then he pushes open a fire-door to his personal smoking area at the back of the court next to the bins and lights his cigarette.

01:16:25.169 --> 01:16:32.392
The feeling of the sun on his face is good, and the taste of the smoke is even better.

01:16:32.452 --> 01:16:40.818
Tonight is a good night to go out for a few drinks, he thinks, and he is deciding whom to call when he hears a female voice.

01:16:40.878 --> 01:16:44.220
Yeah, everything is fine, perfect.

01:16:44.280 --> 01:16:45.152
No.

01:16:45.212 --> 01:16:46.714
No one has an idea.

01:16:46.774 --> 01:16:49.538
Not the police, not my mental family.

01:16:49.598 --> 01:16:51.620
Of course it'll be murder.

01:16:51.680 --> 01:16:52.778
What?

01:16:52.838 --> 01:16:54.806
Oh, who cares about her?

01:16:54.866 --> 01:17:00.039
She'll probably like prison, the crazy cow, more people for her to work.

01:17:00.098 --> 01:17:05.580
And then I get his money, and you and I can get the hell out of this city, and out of this country.

01:17:05.640 --> 01:17:08.732
Nick walks to the bins and looks around them.

01:17:08.792 --> 01:17:12.132
On the other side is the sister of the defendant.

01:17:12.192 --> 01:17:15.914
In the courtroom, she always looks sad and confused.

01:17:15.974 --> 01:17:19.684
Now, however, she looks only victorious.

01:17:19.744 --> 01:17:23.594
Jesus, Nick says before he can stop himself.

01:17:23.653 --> 01:17:28.396
And the girl sees him and closes the mobile phone in her hand.

01:17:28.456 --> 01:17:33.528
Nick looks at her for a second and then turns and runs back to the door.

01:17:33.588 --> 01:17:38.380
He walks back up the stairs, feeling confused and uncertain.

01:17:38.440 --> 01:17:42.282
He has to say something to someone, but who?

01:17:42.342 --> 01:17:43.853
The judge?

01:17:43.913 --> 01:17:45.394
Maybe.

01:17:45.454 --> 01:17:48.368
He pushes the door to the court from open.

01:17:48.428 --> 01:17:50.141
No one is there.

01:17:50.201 --> 01:17:54.454
He moves towards another door and then hears someone else enter the court.

01:17:54.514 --> 01:17:58.806
Wait, Mrs. Dawson says, and she looks like she might cry.

01:17:58.866 --> 01:18:01.708
Please, wait, I need to explain.

01:18:01.768 --> 01:18:03.370
Nick waits.

01:18:03.430 --> 01:18:04.660
Why not?

01:18:04.720 --> 01:18:06.902
What can she do to him in here?

01:18:06.961 --> 01:18:13.836
She stands near to him, and he can see the tears in her eyes and the sad expression on her face.

01:18:13.896 --> 01:18:16.943
But this time he knows it's not real.

01:18:17.003 --> 01:18:18.450
I heard you.

01:18:18.510 --> 01:18:20.082
Your own sister.

01:18:20.142 --> 01:18:22.994
You wanted her to kill your husband.

01:18:23.054 --> 01:18:25.188
No, you don't understand.

01:18:25.247 --> 01:18:29.858
I don't know what you were but I love my sister and I used to love my husband.

01:18:29.918 --> 01:18:33.214
For a second only, Nick waits.

01:18:33.274 --> 01:18:36.575
Then he shakes his head and is about to turn.

01:18:36.635 --> 01:18:40.396
Look, she says, her voice different now.

01:18:40.456 --> 01:18:43.058
I saw you looking at me yesterday.

01:18:43.118 --> 01:18:46.989
Maybe there is something we can do to make you forget this?

01:18:47.049 --> 01:18:49.480
Maybe you can take me for a drink.

01:18:49.540 --> 01:18:52.301
Then she smiles and takes his hand.

01:18:52.361 --> 01:18:55.482
For a second he does not know what to say.

01:18:55.542 --> 01:19:01.255
But he knows what to do, and he pulls his hand away and moves to the door.

01:19:01.315 --> 01:19:04.097
For a second, she is silent.

01:19:04.157 --> 01:19:09.540
But then she speaks again, though this time her voice is hard and angry.

01:19:09.600 --> 01:19:12.252
Fine, go and tell them what you heard.

01:19:12.312 --> 01:19:14.392
Do you have any proof?

01:19:14.452 --> 01:19:18.618
No, so you can tell them what you want, but they can do nothing.

01:19:18.678 --> 01:19:21.392
And you are still here in Bristol.

01:19:21.452 --> 01:19:23.556
And so is my family.

01:19:23.616 --> 01:19:26.418
And they will not like what you say.

01:19:26.478 --> 01:19:28.230
Do you understand me?

01:19:28.290 --> 01:19:29.972
Nick stops.

01:19:30.032 --> 01:19:31.784
He understands.

01:19:31.844 --> 01:19:33.526
And she's right.

01:19:33.586 --> 01:19:35.790
There's nothing he can do.

01:19:35.850 --> 01:19:40.456
That's right little boy, keep quiet and you're going to be okay.

01:19:40.515 --> 01:19:43.090
I never hurt anyone, she did.

01:19:43.150 --> 01:19:45.452
Is it my fault he's so stupid?

01:19:45.512 --> 01:19:47.636
So easy to manipulate?

01:19:47.696 --> 01:19:53.493
A little bit of blood on my face, a few tears, a fake story about him and me?

01:19:53.553 --> 01:20:01.285
It was so easy, and if you tell anyone, it's going to be just as easy to finish you, understand?

01:20:01.345 --> 01:20:08.166
Then the door opens and the lawyers return, and Mrs. Dawson's expression of sadness returns.

01:20:08.226 --> 01:20:13.968
Slowly they all come back in, and Nick knows he has to say something.

01:20:14.028 --> 01:20:15.998
He has to do something.

01:20:16.058 --> 01:20:19.890
But there's no time, and there's nothing he can do.

01:20:19.950 --> 01:20:24.454
The Lee family is back in the room now, and he looks at them.

01:20:24.514 --> 01:20:29.078
They are savage, terrible people who love violence.

01:20:29.138 --> 01:20:35.072
So he moves back to his desk and he sits down in the seat and feels defeated.

01:20:35.132 --> 01:20:42.158
He wants to say something, but without proof he has nothing and the judge can do nothing.

01:20:42.218 --> 01:20:45.882
And he hears the door to the judge's chamber open.

01:20:45.942 --> 01:20:48.005
All rise!"

01:20:48.065 --> 01:20:52.206
John says, and Nick knows it's too late now.

01:20:52.266 --> 01:20:58.958
He stands and is about to press the button on the recording device when he sees the red light flashing.

01:20:59.018 --> 01:21:01.088
The red light.

01:21:01.148 --> 01:21:04.188
The red light means it's recording.

01:21:04.249 --> 01:21:06.099
It's recording.

01:21:06.159 --> 01:21:08.940
It was recording.

01:21:09.000 --> 01:21:11.492
It recorded.

01:21:11.552 --> 01:21:17.738
Nick shouts, and he hands the recording device to the court clerk.

01:21:17.798 --> 01:21:21.151
I think the judge needs to listen to this first.

01:21:21.211 --> 01:21:37.232
He says, and he looks back at the beautiful Mrs. Dawson and sees that she is not sad now, and that she is not victorious, and not angry, and he smiles at her, because he thinks this expression suits her best.

01:21:37.292 --> 01:21:40.969
Yes, he thinks Mrs. Dawson looks best.

01:21:41.029 --> 01:21:43.514
when she is scared.

01:21:43.574 --> 01:21:48.759
Don't move.

01:21:48.819 --> 01:21:55.615
It feels like ice cold water.

01:21:55.674 --> 01:21:58.264
One moment you are asleep.

01:21:58.324 --> 01:22:04.060
Your dreams calm and gentle and your body warm in the blankets of your bed.

01:22:04.120 --> 01:22:06.622
The next moment you are awake.

01:22:06.682 --> 01:22:10.096
A horrible sensation filling your body.

01:22:10.156 --> 01:22:14.132
And you are sure that ice cold water is covering you.

01:22:14.192 --> 01:22:16.054
But it's not.

01:22:16.113 --> 01:22:19.876
No, it's something much worse.

01:22:19.936 --> 01:22:23.389
Something that makes your heart beat faster.

01:22:23.449 --> 01:22:31.544
Something that makes the hairs on your arms stand up and that makes your eyes search the horrible complete darkness of your room.

01:22:31.604 --> 01:22:35.068
No, it's not ice cold water.

01:22:35.128 --> 01:22:36.818
It's fear.

01:22:36.878 --> 01:22:41.602
And for a few moments you are so afraid that you cannot think.

01:22:41.662 --> 01:22:49.784
You know you are still in your bed, still in your house, still in your quiet street in the north of Manchester.

01:22:49.844 --> 01:22:55.569
And you want to tell yourself to relax, that everything is okay.

01:22:55.629 --> 01:23:01.070
and to close your eyes and return to your calm and gentle dreams.

01:23:01.130 --> 01:23:03.070
But you cannot.

01:23:03.130 --> 01:23:08.163
No, you cannot because you know that everything is not okay.

01:23:08.223 --> 01:23:21.358
You know that you are awake because something woke you, some noise, some movement, somebody, and you try to remember what it was, but you cannot.

01:23:21.418 --> 01:23:29.630
So you lie there, and you let your eyes adjust to the dark, and you do not tell yourself to relax.

01:23:29.690 --> 01:23:38.104
You do not tell yourself that everything is okay, and you do not tell yourself to return to your calm and gentle dreams.

01:23:38.164 --> 01:23:42.416
You tell yourself this, don't move.

01:23:42.476 --> 01:23:49.669
The blanket of your bed is covering all of you except for your head, and part of you thinks that this is good.

01:23:49.729 --> 01:23:57.374
Part of you wants to cover your head too, to hide, to be completely silent and to wait.

01:23:57.434 --> 01:24:02.758
But another part of you wants to know, needs to know.

01:24:02.818 --> 01:24:11.964
So you listen, and you look, and you try to remember what it was that woke you, a dog outside in the street.

01:24:12.024 --> 01:24:13.966
The alarm of a car.

01:24:14.026 --> 01:24:16.940
A baby crying in the next house.

01:24:17.000 --> 01:24:19.273
Or something closer.

01:24:19.333 --> 01:24:22.096
Something inside the house.

01:24:22.156 --> 01:24:24.860
The sound of breaking glass.

01:24:24.920 --> 01:24:27.522
The noise of the front door opening.

01:24:27.582 --> 01:24:29.756
A voice in the dark.

01:24:29.816 --> 01:24:31.948
So you do not move.

01:24:32.008 --> 01:24:38.131
But you let your eyes adjust and soon the complete darkness is not so complete.

01:24:38.191 --> 01:24:46.036
And you can see your room now, though everything seems strange and different and everything makes you feel more afraid.

01:24:46.096 --> 01:24:54.393
The door of your wardrobe is open and the clothes inside look like three or four small burglars watching you.

01:24:54.453 --> 01:25:00.110
The lamp in the corner of the room looks like the tall, silent figure of a murderer.

01:25:00.170 --> 01:25:04.774
And the coat on the back of your door has hands that almost touch you.

01:25:04.834 --> 01:25:12.262
But this is just your imagination, your fear, and you know that, and try to stay calm and focused.

01:25:12.322 --> 01:25:17.246
So you listen, and you think that everything seems to be normal.

01:25:17.306 --> 01:25:27.856
Inside the room everything is silent, apart from the slow and constant tick of the small clock on the wall, and the fast irregular beat of your heart.

01:25:27.916 --> 01:25:36.524
You try to see the clock in the dark, but it's impossible, and you think that it must be somewhere between four and six o'clock.

01:25:36.584 --> 01:25:42.662
The darkest hours of the night when even the lively and vibrant city of Manchester sleeps.

01:25:42.722 --> 01:25:46.264
Yes, outside the house the streets are silent.

01:25:46.324 --> 01:25:53.633
There are no shouts or loud voices coming from university students returning home from the pubs and bars.

01:25:53.692 --> 01:25:57.824
There is not yet the sound of the milk van or rubbish collectors.

01:25:57.884 --> 01:26:03.536
And there is no bird song to let you know that the sun and the new day are nearly here.

01:26:03.596 --> 01:26:06.058
You can imagine the street outside.

01:26:06.078 --> 01:26:10.099
20 terraced houses stretching down the road.

01:26:10.159 --> 01:26:14.901
Small houses with neither rich nor poor people inside them.

01:26:14.961 --> 01:26:19.466
All with tidy and ordinary gardens and ordinary cars.

01:26:19.526 --> 01:26:23.041
All silent and all dark.

01:26:23.101 --> 01:26:26.916
You think that if you scream, someone will hear you.

01:26:26.976 --> 01:26:28.838
But you are not sure.

01:26:28.898 --> 01:26:31.090
Not completely sure.

01:26:31.150 --> 01:26:33.024
And so you wait.

01:26:33.084 --> 01:26:34.883
And you listen.

01:26:34.942 --> 01:26:40.091
and you look, and you tell yourself one more time.

01:26:40.151 --> 01:26:42.292
Don't move.

01:26:42.352 --> 01:26:46.983
After a few more moments you begin to feel a little more relaxed.

01:26:47.043 --> 01:26:51.364
The street is silent, and the house is too.

01:26:51.424 --> 01:26:54.844
Maybe it was only a bad dream that woke you.

01:26:54.904 --> 01:26:58.324
Maybe it was a text on your mobile phone.

01:26:58.384 --> 01:27:00.796
Maybe it was an animal in the garden.

01:27:00.856 --> 01:27:04.258
And then you think of Sophie, your cat.

01:27:04.318 --> 01:27:07.179
But you know that the noise was not her.

01:27:07.239 --> 01:27:14.982
You know that Sophie is sleeping in her usual position at the top of the stairs and that she never makes a noise in the night.

01:27:15.043 --> 01:27:23.877
And you wish for the first time that you also had a dog, a large brave dog to bark and growl at any intruders.

01:27:23.937 --> 01:27:31.024
Yes, a brave dog to protect you, instead of just a lazy cat that lies in dangerous places.

01:27:31.084 --> 01:27:35.468
And you almost smile, and you relax a little more.

01:27:35.528 --> 01:27:45.058
It was just a bad dream, you think, and your heart slows, and the sensation of ice-cold fear begins to leave you.

01:27:45.119 --> 01:27:47.010
And now you move.

01:27:47.070 --> 01:27:51.702
You pull the covers down to your chest and make yourself comfortable again.

01:27:51.762 --> 01:27:54.273
And you know that you can rest now.

01:27:54.333 --> 01:27:56.084
Everything is okay.

01:27:56.144 --> 01:28:00.815
And your eyes close and you feel sleep begin to take you.

01:28:00.875 --> 01:28:02.546
And then you hear it.

01:28:02.606 --> 01:28:04.416
There, a sound.

01:28:04.476 --> 01:28:10.068
Not a dream, but movement in the spare room where you keep the computer.

01:28:10.128 --> 01:28:11.543
Don't move.

01:28:11.602 --> 01:28:17.760
part of you says, the part that feels the sensation of cold water on your skin again.

01:28:17.820 --> 01:28:22.824
But there is another part of you now, and it's not cold with fear.

01:28:22.884 --> 01:28:24.565
It is hot.

01:28:24.625 --> 01:28:26.586
Hot with anger.

01:28:26.646 --> 01:28:30.679
Because you know now that someone is in your house.

01:28:30.739 --> 01:28:31.980
A burglar.

01:28:32.040 --> 01:28:35.162
Someone is here to steal your things.

01:28:35.222 --> 01:28:36.824
Don't move.

01:28:36.884 --> 01:28:38.876
part of you says again.

01:28:38.936 --> 01:28:40.596
But you do.

01:28:40.656 --> 01:28:47.560
Quietly you move the blankets from your body and you take the dressing gown from the end of your bed and you put it on.

01:28:47.620 --> 01:28:52.592
You know you should call the police but you need to do something more, something quick.

01:28:52.652 --> 01:28:58.514
At the side of your bed there is a table, and on the table there is a small statue.

01:28:58.574 --> 01:29:00.866
It's a statue of a woman.

01:29:00.926 --> 01:29:03.277
And it's your favorite statue.

01:29:03.337 --> 01:29:06.059
It's also a very heavy statue.

01:29:06.119 --> 01:29:10.332
You pick it up, hold it like a club and swing it.

01:29:10.392 --> 01:29:12.653
Yes, it's heavy enough.

01:29:12.713 --> 01:29:14.764
One hit to the head.

01:29:14.824 --> 01:29:16.635
But can you do it?

01:29:16.696 --> 01:29:18.517
Can you hit someone?

01:29:18.577 --> 01:29:20.588
Can you hit a burglar?

01:29:20.648 --> 01:29:24.126
Then you hear another noise from the spare room.

01:29:24.186 --> 01:29:26.504
My computer, you think.

01:29:26.564 --> 01:29:28.378
My new computer.

01:29:28.438 --> 01:29:32.372
And then you know that yes, you can do it if you need to.

01:29:32.432 --> 01:29:33.744
If you have to.

01:29:33.804 --> 01:29:38.040
If there is someone in the next room trying to steal your new computer.

01:29:38.100 --> 01:29:42.466
Someone who thinks they can enter your house in the middle of the night.

01:29:42.526 --> 01:29:45.116
What if there are two people?

01:29:45.177 --> 01:29:47.057
Two burglars?

01:29:47.117 --> 01:29:48.957
Or three?

01:29:49.017 --> 01:29:50.738
Don't move!

01:29:50.798 --> 01:29:53.098
The scared part of you says.

01:29:53.158 --> 01:29:54.709
But you must.

01:29:54.769 --> 01:29:58.240
You walk silently to the door of your room.

01:29:58.300 --> 01:30:03.911
You quietly open it, just a little, so you can see the dark landing.

01:30:03.971 --> 01:30:06.982
Now you can hear the movement more easily.

01:30:07.041 --> 01:30:08.852
There are no voices.

01:30:08.912 --> 01:30:13.093
No reason for you to think that there is more than one burglar.

01:30:13.153 --> 01:30:17.294
You open the door a little more and step out onto the landing.

01:30:17.354 --> 01:30:20.604
It's colder here, but you do not feel it.

01:30:20.664 --> 01:30:28.126
Your heart is beating so fast that you do not feel anything but the strange mixture of fear and anger.

01:30:28.186 --> 01:30:30.256
You move quietly.

01:30:30.316 --> 01:30:32.107
You're back to the wall.

01:30:32.167 --> 01:30:37.102
The light switch is not near your door but halfway to the stairs.

01:30:37.162 --> 01:30:43.698
The door to the spare room is half open, and you can hear the sound of the wind outside.

01:30:43.758 --> 01:30:50.990
You know now that the window to the spare room is open, and you know this is how the burglar entered.

01:30:51.050 --> 01:30:54.052
You take one more step to the door.

01:30:54.112 --> 01:30:59.216
Your hand is nearly on the light switch when you hear another noise from the room.

01:30:59.276 --> 01:31:03.330
Then the door begins to open slowly and quietly.

01:31:03.390 --> 01:31:10.220
You want to turn back and run to your room and hide under the blanket and be completely silent and not move.

01:31:10.280 --> 01:31:12.593
But this is your house.

01:31:12.653 --> 01:31:15.484
And you know you must stay.

01:31:15.544 --> 01:31:20.675
Then standing right in front of you in the dark, you see the burglar.

01:31:20.735 --> 01:31:23.315
And he looks back at you and shouts.

01:31:23.375 --> 01:31:28.116
You turn on the lights and swing the heavy statue in his direction.

01:31:28.176 --> 01:31:31.967
But you cannot see him now because the light is so bright.

01:31:32.027 --> 01:31:38.214
You hear your new computer fall to the floor and break, and the burglar shouts again.

01:31:38.274 --> 01:31:40.850
And then you can see his face.

01:31:40.910 --> 01:31:45.034
And you realize that he looks more afraid than you do.

01:31:45.094 --> 01:31:48.516
And he is stepping quickly backwards to the stairs.

01:31:48.576 --> 01:31:53.610
He is young, just a kid, just a stupid kid.

01:31:53.670 --> 01:31:58.844
Then you see Sophie, in her normal place at the top of the stairs.

01:31:58.904 --> 01:32:01.656
And you see how near the burglar is.

01:32:01.715 --> 01:32:04.858
You only have time to shout two words.

01:32:04.918 --> 01:32:06.361
Don't move!

01:32:06.421 --> 01:32:13.796
but it is too late, and he does move, and then he falls.

01:32:13.856 --> 01:32:17.578
God is in the detail.

01:32:17.638 --> 01:32:24.484
The back streets of Dublin are quiet, and the sun is slowly setting on the old city.

01:32:24.544 --> 01:32:29.330
Barry Brennan stands in the doorway of an empty shop and waits.

01:32:29.390 --> 01:32:35.146
He smokes his cigarettes continuously and enjoys the taste of each one.

01:32:35.206 --> 01:32:38.658
He enjoys another sweeter taste too.

01:32:38.718 --> 01:32:41.100
The taste of freedom.

01:32:41.160 --> 01:32:43.162
But he has to be careful.

01:32:43.222 --> 01:32:44.703
He knows that.

01:32:44.763 --> 01:32:48.946
The guardie are everywhere, and they are looking for him.

01:32:49.006 --> 01:32:53.277
And if he wants to keep his freedom, he has to be clever.

01:32:53.337 --> 01:33:00.514
He has to be patient, and so he watches the street and the small red brick houses.

01:33:00.574 --> 01:33:11.148
Most importantly though, he watches the church, his own parish church, the church of his childhood, St. Anthony's.

01:33:11.208 --> 01:33:16.510
How long ago was his last visit to this cold stone building?

01:33:16.570 --> 01:33:18.812
More than 20 years?

01:33:18.872 --> 01:33:22.084
Well, that's not important at the moment.

01:33:22.144 --> 01:33:23.314
No.

01:33:23.374 --> 01:33:28.478
The only thing that's important is something that the church can offer him right now.

01:33:28.538 --> 01:33:35.782
The one thing he needs if he wants to keep his freedom and get the suitcase that's buried in his brother's old garage.

01:33:35.842 --> 01:33:43.894
The suitcase that can help him escape the country and live the rest of his life on a tropical island in the Bahamas.

01:33:43.954 --> 01:33:45.634
Sanctuary.

01:33:45.694 --> 01:33:47.515
And so he waits.

01:33:47.575 --> 01:33:50.146
And the evening becomes darker.

01:33:50.206 --> 01:33:55.387
And he thinks that there are now only one or two people left in the small old church.

01:33:55.447 --> 01:33:58.018
He decides that it's time.

01:33:58.078 --> 01:34:08.499
He pulls the collar of his stolen coat up to cover his face, and he checks that no one can see the grey prison uniform that he's still wearing beneath it.

01:34:08.559 --> 01:34:13.244
He finishes his last cigarette and stops to listen.

01:34:13.304 --> 01:34:18.360
In the distance he can hear the siren of a guardy car in the city centre.

01:34:18.420 --> 01:34:20.801
but he's sure it's not for him.

01:34:20.861 --> 01:34:32.776
So he crosses the road slowly and casually, walks up the stone steps of the church and looks around once more, before he pushes open the wooden doors and goes inside.

01:34:32.836 --> 01:34:34.806
St. Antony's.

01:34:34.866 --> 01:34:38.758
It smells no different, he thinks, as he enters.

01:34:38.818 --> 01:34:41.420
And it looks identical too.

01:34:41.480 --> 01:34:47.292
The same cold grey walls, the same uncomfortable heavy pews.

01:34:47.352 --> 01:34:51.906
and the same weak candles to light up the dark room.

01:34:51.966 --> 01:34:59.338
He was right, there are a few people inside, two women sitting together on the front pew.

01:34:59.398 --> 01:35:10.624
Their eyes closed and heads down, and an old man who looks at Barry for a moment before returning to his prayers, but not one of them is interested in him.

01:35:10.684 --> 01:35:13.236
Good, Barry thinks.

01:35:13.296 --> 01:35:15.588
The place is practically empty.

01:35:15.648 --> 01:35:22.363
He looks to the right wall and sees what he's looking for, a large wooden box with two doors.

01:35:22.424 --> 01:35:26.764
Quietly he walks past the pews and stops near the box.

01:35:26.824 --> 01:35:31.573
He pauses for a moment to try and remember what he has to do next.

01:35:31.634 --> 01:35:36.426
But then the door to the church opens again and three women enter.

01:35:36.486 --> 01:35:42.778
As he does not want them to see his face, he pulls open the left door to the box and enters.

01:35:42.838 --> 01:35:44.838
A confessional.

01:35:44.898 --> 01:35:47.670
As soon as he's inside, he hates it.

01:35:47.730 --> 01:35:55.298
It's small, so small, and it reminds him of his prison cell back in the joy.

01:35:55.358 --> 01:36:04.537
but there is no reply, and he can see no movement behind the grill that separates his small space from the other.

01:36:04.597 --> 01:36:18.405
So he tries to make himself comfortable on the small chair, but after a few minutes he realizes how tired he is, and he thinks that maybe he can close his eyes for a moment and even rest his head against the grill.

01:36:18.465 --> 01:36:22.108
But he mustn't sleep, he tells himself.

01:36:22.168 --> 01:36:23.838
He mustn't.

01:36:23.898 --> 01:36:35.688
But he does, and he dreams of the suitcase buried under his brother's old garage, and he dreams of the money inside and the paradise in the Bahamas.

01:36:35.748 --> 01:36:37.210
Hello?

01:36:37.271 --> 01:36:43.126
Says a voice, and Barry wakes up and looks around in confusion and fear.

01:36:43.186 --> 01:36:45.108
Is there someone there?

01:36:45.168 --> 01:36:46.710
Says the voice.

01:36:46.770 --> 01:36:49.376
Barry remembers where he is.

01:36:49.436 --> 01:36:56.656
Yes, I'm here," he says, and for a moment there is silence before he remembers what to say.

01:36:56.716 --> 01:36:59.268
Bless me, Father, for I have sinned.

01:36:59.329 --> 01:37:02.250
And again there is a long silence.

01:37:02.311 --> 01:37:08.014
Barry tries to see the face through the grille, but there is only a dark shape.

01:37:08.074 --> 01:37:11.046
Finally the voice replies.

01:37:11.106 --> 01:37:12.668
I see.

01:37:12.728 --> 01:37:14.222
Go on.

01:37:14.282 --> 01:37:17.406
First, father, I need to know something.

01:37:17.466 --> 01:37:21.857
I need to know that anything I say here is not to be repeated.

01:37:21.917 --> 01:37:24.368
That's how this works, isn't it?

01:37:24.428 --> 01:37:26.688
That is true.

01:37:26.748 --> 01:37:28.498
My son."

01:37:28.558 --> 01:37:34.520
The voice says, and Barry thinks that it's a calm and honest voice.

01:37:34.580 --> 01:37:36.690
Is that father O'Connor?

01:37:36.750 --> 01:37:39.951
He asks, remembering his old priest.

01:37:40.011 --> 01:37:41.743
O'Connor?

01:37:41.803 --> 01:37:45.518
No, son, my name is Keen.

01:37:45.578 --> 01:37:50.402
I believe Father O'Connor passed away nearly ten years ago.

01:37:50.462 --> 01:37:53.414
Barry nods in the dark space.

01:37:53.474 --> 01:37:56.718
But does it matter that you're not my priest?

01:37:56.778 --> 01:37:58.590
Can you still listen?

01:37:58.650 --> 01:38:02.764
Any man can listen, says the quiet voice.

01:38:02.823 --> 01:38:06.332
But can you promise not to tell anyone?

01:38:06.392 --> 01:38:08.798
I promise, my son.

01:38:08.858 --> 01:38:11.570
I will not tell a soul.

01:38:11.630 --> 01:38:15.412
And Barry nods again and feels ready to speak.

01:38:15.472 --> 01:38:18.643
Father, I need to ask for something from you.

01:38:18.703 --> 01:38:21.044
I need to ask for sanctuary.

01:38:21.104 --> 01:38:22.555
Sanctuary?

01:38:22.615 --> 01:38:23.906
Yes, Father.

01:38:23.966 --> 01:38:26.167
I need somewhere to hide for a few days.

01:38:26.227 --> 01:38:27.227
A week, maybe.

01:38:27.287 --> 01:38:28.578
Can you do that?

01:38:28.638 --> 01:38:31.169
Can you help me hide here in the church?

01:38:31.229 --> 01:38:33.480
There is another silence.

01:38:33.540 --> 01:38:35.194
Maybe?

01:38:35.254 --> 01:38:37.507
But why, my son?

01:38:37.567 --> 01:38:40.548
I can only help you if you tell me why.

01:38:40.608 --> 01:38:43.869
God, you see, is in the detail.

01:38:43.929 --> 01:38:50.231
If you want to confess and receive God's forgiveness, you must tell me everything.

01:38:50.291 --> 01:38:54.733
Okay, Father, but remember, you promise not to tell us all.

01:38:54.793 --> 01:38:56.314
I do.

01:38:56.374 --> 01:38:58.264
Barry nods.

01:38:58.324 --> 01:39:00.857
My name is Barry Brennan.

01:39:00.917 --> 01:39:02.850
Do you know the name?

01:39:02.910 --> 01:39:04.932
Another pause.

01:39:04.992 --> 01:39:07.483
I do, my son.

01:39:07.543 --> 01:39:10.576
It's on every radio and every television.

01:39:10.635 --> 01:39:15.499
You are the prisoner, the one that escaped from Mount Joy Prison.

01:39:15.558 --> 01:39:19.262
My son, you should let me take you to the guards.

01:39:19.322 --> 01:39:21.663
No, Barry shouts.

01:39:21.723 --> 01:39:24.896
You promised father, no one can know.

01:39:24.956 --> 01:39:28.154
Then you must tell me everything.

01:39:28.214 --> 01:39:30.500
There's not much to tell.

01:39:30.560 --> 01:39:34.041
I escaped from the Guard Ivan that was taking me to hospital.

01:39:34.101 --> 01:39:38.522
I told him I had a pain in my side and the doctors thought it was my appendix.

01:39:38.582 --> 01:39:44.373
When I was in the van, it stopped and I hit one of the guards, took his keys and ran.

01:39:44.433 --> 01:39:46.733
That was two days ago.

01:39:46.793 --> 01:39:50.934
Well, God will judge you for this, my son.

01:39:50.994 --> 01:39:54.364
But tell me, why did you stay in the city?

01:39:54.424 --> 01:39:59.166
Why not run for the countryside or hop on a ferry out of here?

01:39:59.226 --> 01:40:01.728
Well, Father, there's...

01:40:01.788 --> 01:40:05.760
There's something I need to get before I can go.

01:40:05.820 --> 01:40:07.892
And that's why I'm here.

01:40:07.952 --> 01:40:12.765
I need to wait for a few days until they think I'm miles from the city.

01:40:12.825 --> 01:40:14.666
Then I can get this...

01:40:14.726 --> 01:40:16.978
thing, and I can go.

01:40:17.038 --> 01:40:22.882
And I promise, Father, after this, I will never commit another crime again.

01:40:22.942 --> 01:40:26.292
I will lead a quiet and honest life.

01:40:26.352 --> 01:40:30.481
And Barry imagines the beaches of the Bahamas.

01:40:30.541 --> 01:40:33.472
I see, says the voice.

01:40:33.532 --> 01:40:35.573
I want to help you.

01:40:35.633 --> 01:40:39.174
And if you confess all your sins to me, then maybe I can.

01:40:39.234 --> 01:40:43.336
But, like I say, God is in the detail.

01:40:43.396 --> 01:40:46.236
What is this thing you need?

01:40:46.296 --> 01:40:48.567
Barry hesitates.

01:40:48.627 --> 01:40:50.638
He thinks about lying.

01:40:50.698 --> 01:40:51.658
But why?

01:40:51.718 --> 01:40:54.796
The priest promises not to tell a soul.

01:40:54.856 --> 01:41:00.625
It's a suitcase, Father, and inside there is half a million euros.

01:41:00.685 --> 01:41:05.446
And yes, it is stolen, but I promise, Father, no one needs it now.

01:41:05.506 --> 01:41:13.350
I stole it more than eight years ago from a bank security van, a bank father, and they have insurance and all that, so they don't need it.

01:41:13.410 --> 01:41:16.060
No one needs it now, apart from me.

01:41:16.120 --> 01:41:20.032
And this time, the silence is heavy and tense.

01:41:20.092 --> 01:41:24.384
And Barry can hear the voices of the women as they leave the church.

01:41:24.444 --> 01:41:29.956
He wonders if there are still any people there, but he thinks there are not.

01:41:30.016 --> 01:41:33.628
Well, says the quiet voice.

01:41:33.688 --> 01:41:37.950
I understand, my son, and I will help you.

01:41:38.010 --> 01:41:42.572
I can let you sleep here for a few days, and I can bring you some food.

01:41:42.633 --> 01:41:44.794
Oh, Father, thank you, Father.

01:41:44.854 --> 01:41:48.659
But like I say, God is in the detail.

01:41:48.719 --> 01:41:52.034
Where exactly is this suitcase?

01:41:52.094 --> 01:41:53.184
What?

01:41:53.244 --> 01:41:54.355
Why?

01:41:54.415 --> 01:41:55.385
Why?

01:41:55.445 --> 01:41:59.877
Because I need to know that you aren't going to hurt anyone to get it.

01:41:59.937 --> 01:42:02.318
The voice says seriously.

01:42:02.378 --> 01:42:04.068
Barry laughs.

01:42:04.128 --> 01:42:06.079
Oh no, father, I promise.

01:42:06.139 --> 01:42:08.220
It's in an old garage of my brother's.

01:42:08.280 --> 01:42:10.770
On a bit of land next to the house road.

01:42:10.830 --> 01:42:14.822
I can go at night, dig up the concrete and get it no problem.

01:42:14.882 --> 01:42:16.812
Well, I tell you what.

01:42:16.872 --> 01:42:18.528
You wait here.

01:42:18.588 --> 01:42:20.845
I'm going to close the church.

01:42:20.906 --> 01:42:25.030
Then maybe I can find you that bit of food and some other clothes.

01:42:25.090 --> 01:42:29.824
And for the first time in two days Barry Brennan feels relief.

01:42:29.884 --> 01:42:33.066
He can really taste the freedom now.

01:42:33.126 --> 01:42:34.758
Ah, thank you Father.

01:42:34.818 --> 01:42:35.838
Thank you.

01:42:35.898 --> 01:42:40.032
You know, I'm sure there's a bit of spare money for the church.

01:42:40.092 --> 01:42:41.013
3,000 maybe?

01:42:41.073 --> 01:42:42.124
What do you think?

01:42:42.184 --> 01:42:43.956
But there is no answer.

01:42:44.015 --> 01:42:46.964
There is, however, a strange noise.

01:42:47.024 --> 01:42:50.926
Something moving against the door of the confessional.

01:42:50.986 --> 01:42:52.798
Something heavy.

01:42:52.858 --> 01:42:55.943
Something like one of the heavy pews.

01:42:56.004 --> 01:43:00.590
And Barry tries to push the door open, but he cannot.

01:43:00.650 --> 01:43:09.088
And before Barry Brennan begins to shout and scream to the empty church, he hears the soft voice one more time.

01:43:09.148 --> 01:43:13.582
Now, son, I told you that any man can listen, didn't I?

01:43:13.642 --> 01:43:14.733
It says.

01:43:14.793 --> 01:43:18.286
And I also told you that God is in the detail.

01:43:18.346 --> 01:43:22.752
And an important detail, a very important detail.

01:43:22.812 --> 01:43:26.425
is that you're in the wrong side of the confessional.

01:43:26.485 --> 01:43:31.366
I just stopped in to confess about stealing another bottle of whiskey from the shop.

01:43:31.427 --> 01:43:35.078
But you know, I think I can do that next time.

01:43:35.138 --> 01:43:38.859
Yes, I think I might have a lot more to confess tomorrow.

01:43:38.919 --> 01:43:42.070
No, you did say howthrode.

01:43:42.130 --> 01:43:46.431
And then there is only the sound of Barry Brennan's shouts.

01:43:46.491 --> 01:43:49.778
And there is no one in the church to hear those.

01:43:49.838 --> 01:43:54.630
Well, apart from God, I suppose.

01:43:54.690 --> 01:44:03.258
The car is big and old, but it's a classic.

01:44:03.318 --> 01:44:10.285
and it moves along the road like a shark, the evening sun reflecting off the red paint.

01:44:10.344 --> 01:44:16.906
The roof is down, and Dan enjoys the feeling of the wind in his hair.

01:44:16.966 --> 01:44:20.317
It calls him and helps him to stay awake.

01:44:20.377 --> 01:44:27.878
He's tired, very tired, and he still feels sick from the beer and whiskey of last night.

01:44:27.938 --> 01:44:30.059
Soon he can stop.

01:44:30.119 --> 01:44:34.982
Find a quieter area off the road and push back the chair and sleep.

01:44:35.042 --> 01:44:36.802
But not yet.

01:44:36.862 --> 01:44:43.696
The Mojave Desert is still all around him with its flat orange sounds and dead bushes.

01:44:43.756 --> 01:44:48.820
The plan was to get to Flagstaff, but it's too many miles away.

01:44:48.880 --> 01:44:55.793
So instead he just wants to get as far from the town called Needles as he can before the night comes.

01:44:55.853 --> 01:44:57.283
Needles.

01:44:57.343 --> 01:44:59.573
He hates that town.

01:44:59.634 --> 01:45:01.544
And that girl.

01:45:01.604 --> 01:45:04.915
He looks at his right hand on the steering wheel.

01:45:04.975 --> 01:45:07.276
The skin is raw.

01:45:07.336 --> 01:45:11.387
And he tries to remember exactly what happened last night.

01:45:11.447 --> 01:45:12.787
But he cannot.

01:45:12.847 --> 01:45:15.028
And maybe he should not.

01:45:15.088 --> 01:45:16.992
Stupid girl.

01:45:17.052 --> 01:45:20.026
But anyway, why think about it?

01:45:20.086 --> 01:45:25.269
Just drive, get a few more miles from the town and from her.

01:45:25.329 --> 01:45:31.532
He hits the button for the radio, and an old song fills the silence of the desert.

01:45:31.592 --> 01:45:33.963
A good song, he thinks.

01:45:34.023 --> 01:45:35.274
A real song.

01:45:35.334 --> 01:45:37.905
All guitars and rock and roll.

01:45:37.965 --> 01:45:40.118
He feels a little better.

01:45:40.178 --> 01:45:43.831
Maybe it's time for the first drink of the day after all.

01:45:43.891 --> 01:45:50.625
And he takes the small flask from the pocket of his denim jacket, removes the lid and swallows.

01:45:50.685 --> 01:45:53.998
For a second, the taste is foul.

01:45:54.058 --> 01:45:59.752
a horrible reminder of last night, of the girl and of the hotel room.

01:45:59.812 --> 01:46:08.295
But then the hot whiskey starts to fill him and he smiles, puts his head back and laughs loudly into the desert.

01:46:08.355 --> 01:46:10.016
Route 66!

01:46:10.076 --> 01:46:13.917
He shouts and he turns the volume of the radio up.

01:46:13.977 --> 01:46:17.648
He can forget about last night, about the girl.

01:46:17.707 --> 01:46:21.698
His shirt has a little blood on it and his hand is sore.

01:46:21.758 --> 01:46:25.899
But he can get a new shirt and the whiskey will help the pain.

01:46:25.959 --> 01:46:31.691
No, this is his holiday, his escape from all those idiots back in California.

01:46:31.751 --> 01:46:34.711
Forget needles and forget the girl.

01:46:34.771 --> 01:46:37.496
Just relax and enjoy it.

01:46:37.557 --> 01:46:41.982
She was not the first girl, and he knows she will not be the last.

01:46:42.042 --> 01:46:51.295
Maybe there's one waiting for him in Flagstaff, and this one, he hopes, will have a little more respect for him and won't laugh at him.

01:46:51.355 --> 01:46:55.756
So he drives, and for a while he feels better.

01:46:55.816 --> 01:46:58.307
The sun is lower in the sky now.

01:46:58.367 --> 01:47:06.792
It's a little less hot, and the radio keeps him company with old songs about love and women, rock and roll and country music.

01:47:06.852 --> 01:47:08.536
real music.

01:47:08.596 --> 01:47:12.850
But then the songs stop, and the news report starts.

01:47:12.910 --> 01:47:21.286
There's something about needles, something about trouble on Route 66, and he tries to listen, but the signal is weak.

01:47:21.346 --> 01:47:37.156
A young, dead, the police are looking a man, probably alone and thought to be extremely dangerous, any information call, and then the signal fails, and there is only the sound of the old classic engine and the silence of the desert.

01:47:37.216 --> 01:47:40.094
Now, now it can be her.

01:47:40.154 --> 01:47:45.255
He looks at his hand again and tries to make a fist, but it hurts.

01:47:45.315 --> 01:47:49.146
And he thinks of the white shirt in the trunk of the car.

01:47:49.206 --> 01:47:51.787
The white shirt with blood on it.

01:47:51.847 --> 01:47:54.278
What did he do last night?

01:47:54.338 --> 01:47:56.048
He tries to remember.

01:47:56.108 --> 01:47:59.344
He tries to remember exactly what happened.

01:47:59.404 --> 01:48:01.691
He remembers the bar clearly.

01:48:01.751 --> 01:48:06.153
He remembers buying her a drink, and then another, and another.

01:48:06.213 --> 01:48:10.294
He remembers that afterwards they were in the hotel room, kissing.

01:48:10.354 --> 01:48:13.416
But then something happened and she laughed.

01:48:13.476 --> 01:48:16.897
She laughed at him, but he cannot remember why.

01:48:16.957 --> 01:48:20.548
It was something stupid, something he said or did.

01:48:20.608 --> 01:48:22.049
And then...

01:48:22.109 --> 01:48:24.940
But the rest is impossible to remember.

01:48:25.000 --> 01:48:28.418
He woke up late in the hotel room and was alone.

01:48:28.478 --> 01:48:33.136
He saw the blood, but there was only a little, and his hand hurt.

01:48:33.196 --> 01:48:34.298
And he knew.

01:48:34.318 --> 01:48:35.754
Yeah.

01:48:35.814 --> 01:48:38.539
He could not remember.

01:48:38.599 --> 01:48:40.040
But he knew.

01:48:40.100 --> 01:48:44.612
And he thinks of all the times he watched his father hit his mother.

01:48:44.671 --> 01:48:45.842
Jesus!

01:48:45.902 --> 01:48:49.474
He says, and he does not feel tired anymore.

01:48:49.534 --> 01:48:55.076
He puts his foot on the accelerator and the car starts to speed along the old road.

01:48:55.136 --> 01:48:56.777
Forget sleeping.

01:48:56.837 --> 01:48:58.688
Forget Flagstaff.

01:48:58.748 --> 01:49:03.809
He needs to find another route and a motel to stay in for a few days.

01:49:03.869 --> 01:49:07.790
A cheap motel where they only want a name and some cash.

01:49:07.850 --> 01:49:10.091
The sun is almost down now.

01:49:10.151 --> 01:49:12.791
The sky a deep orange and red.

01:49:12.851 --> 01:49:14.962
And the road is empty.

01:49:15.022 --> 01:49:19.123
Or it is until he speeds past the large billboard.

01:49:19.183 --> 01:49:22.334
Then suddenly the road is not empty.

01:49:22.394 --> 01:49:24.564
There is a car behind him.

01:49:24.624 --> 01:49:27.941
Its red and blue lights fill the evening.

01:49:28.001 --> 01:49:32.322
and the sound of the siren fills his ears.

01:49:32.342 --> 01:49:39.186
No, he groans as he sees it in the mirror, and he thinks for a moment about not stopping.

01:49:39.246 --> 01:49:42.064
but there's nowhere for him to go.

01:49:42.124 --> 01:49:45.827
Jesus, just stay cool Dan, just stay cool.

01:49:45.887 --> 01:49:49.510
He tries to tell himself as the red car slows down.

01:49:49.570 --> 01:49:57.537
So he stops on the edge of the desert, hides the flask of whiskey under the seat and lights a cigarette to cover the smell.

01:49:57.597 --> 01:50:05.595
God, he wishes he could remember last night, not just the girl's expression when he hit her, but everything.

01:50:05.655 --> 01:50:12.194
In the mirror he sees the cop car park behind him and he watches a young tall cop get out.

01:50:12.254 --> 01:50:13.274
Just one.

01:50:13.334 --> 01:50:14.086
Good.

01:50:14.146 --> 01:50:18.470
If things get serious then maybe he can manage if there's just one.

01:50:18.530 --> 01:50:21.924
He watches the cop walk slowly to the car.

01:50:21.985 --> 01:50:25.780
The guy's eyes are covered by silver sunglasses.

01:50:25.840 --> 01:50:29.054
And there's something strange about his uniform.

01:50:29.114 --> 01:50:30.414
Dan thinks.

01:50:30.474 --> 01:50:36.067
But before he can decide what, the cop is at the door, and Dan drops his cigarette.

01:50:36.127 --> 01:50:40.019
Evening officer, he says and tries to look sorry.

01:50:40.079 --> 01:50:42.901
I know I was going a little too fast, I'm sorry.

01:50:42.960 --> 01:50:46.722
The cop looks at him and nods, but says nothing.

01:50:46.782 --> 01:50:51.575
Dan wants to see the eyes behind the glasses, but cannot.

01:50:51.635 --> 01:50:53.706
I suppose it's this road.

01:50:53.766 --> 01:50:57.197
The desert makes it easy to forget to watch his speed.

01:50:57.257 --> 01:51:00.778
He says and he thinks he sees the cop look at his hand.

01:51:00.838 --> 01:51:02.089
Jesus.

01:51:02.149 --> 01:51:03.499
Is this it?

01:51:03.559 --> 01:51:06.390
Did he really do something to that girl?

01:51:06.450 --> 01:51:08.391
More than hit her once.

01:51:08.451 --> 01:51:11.102
Did he do what they said on the radio?

01:51:11.162 --> 01:51:13.072
Was she dead?

01:51:13.132 --> 01:51:14.563
Yep.

01:51:14.623 --> 01:51:16.154
The cop says.

01:51:16.214 --> 01:51:18.874
These roads are funny like that.

01:51:18.934 --> 01:51:22.036
Lots of people have problems out here.

01:51:22.096 --> 01:51:24.503
This is a nice car.

01:51:24.563 --> 01:51:26.390
A classic.

01:51:26.450 --> 01:51:27.892
Yours?

01:51:27.952 --> 01:51:30.453
And his voice is slow.

01:51:30.513 --> 01:51:33.784
Yes, sir, I have my license here in the registration.

01:51:33.844 --> 01:51:36.436
The cop nods but does not take them.

01:51:36.496 --> 01:51:39.488
I need to ask you to get out of the car.

01:51:39.548 --> 01:51:41.659
Dan tries to smile.

01:51:41.719 --> 01:51:43.630
Is there a problem, officer?

01:51:43.690 --> 01:51:46.442
And he looks at the gun on the cop's belt.

01:51:46.502 --> 01:51:49.183
If he can get the gun, then he can escape.

01:51:49.243 --> 01:51:54.256
Forget California, go north to Canada or Alaska.

01:51:54.316 --> 01:51:55.788
But he waits.

01:51:55.848 --> 01:52:00.082
Maybe this is something else, a routine check, a formality.

01:52:00.142 --> 01:52:01.963
Be cool, Dan.

01:52:02.023 --> 01:52:03.514
He tells himself.

01:52:03.574 --> 01:52:06.086
Just get out of the car, please.

01:52:06.146 --> 01:52:08.128
The cop repeats.

01:52:08.188 --> 01:52:11.340
Dan nods and gets out.

01:52:11.400 --> 01:52:15.964
And he thinks again that there is something strange about the cop's uniform.

01:52:16.024 --> 01:52:17.494
It's small.

01:52:17.554 --> 01:52:20.526
Too small for a tall man like him.

01:52:20.586 --> 01:52:24.408
I need to see in the trunk of the car, sir.

01:52:24.468 --> 01:52:25.808
Jesus.

01:52:25.868 --> 01:52:26.929
The shirt.

01:52:26.989 --> 01:52:28.800
The shirt is in the trunk.

01:52:28.860 --> 01:52:30.430
Is that what he wants?

01:52:30.490 --> 01:52:31.661
The trunk?

01:52:31.721 --> 01:52:33.272
Can I ask why?

01:52:33.332 --> 01:52:35.412
Just routine.

01:52:35.472 --> 01:52:37.454
Just routine.

01:52:37.514 --> 01:52:39.014
Maybe it is.

01:52:39.074 --> 01:52:43.246
And the shirt is in his bag, at the bottom, where it cannot be seen.

01:52:43.306 --> 01:52:44.532
Okay.

01:52:44.592 --> 01:52:48.810
He walks around to the trunk, the cop behind him.

01:52:48.870 --> 01:52:50.962
And he pushes the button.

01:52:51.022 --> 01:52:53.254
For a moment he does nothing.

01:52:53.314 --> 01:52:56.586
He remembers the baseball bat he keeps in there.

01:52:56.646 --> 01:53:02.259
If the cop asks to look in his bag, he can reach in, grab the bat and take a swing.

01:53:02.319 --> 01:53:04.420
Maybe it can work.

01:53:04.481 --> 01:53:07.852
Sir, the cop says.

01:53:07.912 --> 01:53:09.124
Sure.

01:53:09.184 --> 01:53:12.587
And Dan starts to lift the lid of the trunk.

01:53:12.647 --> 01:53:18.102
But in the reflection of the red paint, he sees the cop raise his hand.

01:53:18.162 --> 01:53:21.984
And in the soft evening light he sees the knife.

01:53:22.044 --> 01:53:24.946
And for a moment he does not understand.

01:53:25.006 --> 01:53:26.898
But then he moves.

01:53:26.957 --> 01:53:29.070
He is almost quick enough.

01:53:29.130 --> 01:53:33.442
But the knife still cuts him and he screams and falls to the floor.

01:53:33.502 --> 01:53:37.026
The cop falls too and there is a moment of confusion.

01:53:37.086 --> 01:53:41.251
But then Dan pushes himself up onto his feet and runs.

01:53:41.312 --> 01:53:45.226
He sees the cop car and runs to it and pulls open the door.

01:53:45.286 --> 01:53:47.910
That's when he sees the bodies.

01:53:47.970 --> 01:53:50.303
One is clearly a police woman.

01:53:50.362 --> 01:53:53.986
But the other, a short man, has no uniform.

01:53:54.046 --> 01:53:55.165
Jesus!

01:53:55.225 --> 01:53:59.524
He screams, and he remembers the news story on the radio.

01:53:59.584 --> 01:54:06.467
A young, dead, the police are looking a man, probably alone and thought to be extremely dangerous.

01:54:06.527 --> 01:54:12.430
Any information call, and he starts to run again, not on the road now but into the desert.

01:54:12.490 --> 01:54:17.823
He wants to turn to look back to see if the man is following him, but he does not dare.

01:54:17.883 --> 01:54:24.110
He thinks that if he keeps running, he can escape and find help, find the real police.

01:54:24.170 --> 01:54:28.930
But the sun is gone now, and darkness is all around him.

01:54:28.990 --> 01:54:32.554
Now everything from last night is coming back to him.

01:54:32.614 --> 01:54:39.672
He remembers hitting the girl, he remembers her shouting at him, and he remembers telling her to get out.

01:54:39.732 --> 01:54:45.594
And he remembers the last thing she said before she closed the door to the hotel room.

01:54:45.654 --> 01:54:49.100
I hope you die and rot in the desert!"

01:54:49.160 --> 01:54:55.171
she shouted, blood still on her face, and he almost laughs in disbelief.

01:54:55.231 --> 01:55:01.213
But then he hears someone running behind him, and he screams instead.

01:55:01.273 --> 01:55:10.765
The Fall Brandon tries not to think as he walks through the snow that reaches up to his waist.

01:55:10.826 --> 01:55:13.640
He is confused and weak.

01:55:13.700 --> 01:55:19.716
He tries not to remember the expression on Greg's face as the bullet hit him in the back.

01:55:19.776 --> 01:55:22.388
You said it was easy.

01:55:22.448 --> 01:55:29.970
Brandon calls out into the forest of silent trees that surround him, his voice full of emotion.

01:55:30.030 --> 01:55:32.702
You said no one could get hurt.

01:55:32.762 --> 01:55:35.372
But someone did get hurt.

01:55:35.432 --> 01:55:40.722
And now Brandon is alone, and he feels tired and lost.

01:55:40.782 --> 01:55:49.206
and he knows that the man in the dark winter coat and black hat is near, and he thinks that the next bullet is for him.

01:55:49.266 --> 01:55:56.658
He tries to move faster through the snow, but it's so deep, and it is so cold.

01:55:56.718 --> 01:56:00.305
His clothes are wet and his skin froze him.

01:56:00.365 --> 01:56:05.796
He wants to put his hand in his coat pocket, but he has to keep the grey pistol ready.

01:56:05.856 --> 01:56:08.928
He remembers the way that Greg fell.

01:56:08.988 --> 01:56:13.889
One moment they were running, the next, there was the sound of the gun and a scream.

01:56:13.949 --> 01:56:16.100
Then Greg was on the floor.

01:56:16.160 --> 01:56:24.624
Brandon wanted to stop and help him, but Greg told him to run, to escape and to not say anything to anyone.

01:56:24.684 --> 01:56:31.460
And then Brandon heard another gunshot, so he ran and left his brother in the snow to die alone.

01:56:31.521 --> 01:56:33.842
You said it was easy!

01:56:33.902 --> 01:56:39.255
He shouts again and recalls the first time his brother had told him about the plan.

01:56:39.315 --> 01:56:40.646
Listen to me.

01:56:40.706 --> 01:56:45.439
His brother said at a dark table in the corner at the lake Louise Inn.

01:56:45.499 --> 01:56:47.070
It's easy, Bran.

01:56:47.130 --> 01:56:49.001
I know exactly what to do.

01:56:49.061 --> 01:56:51.132
Brandon put his beer down.

01:56:51.192 --> 01:56:52.864
But a kid, Greg.

01:56:52.924 --> 01:56:54.624
I don't want to hurt a kid.

01:56:54.684 --> 01:56:55.826
What?

01:56:55.886 --> 01:56:58.928
His brother said, his eyes full of anger.

01:56:58.988 --> 01:57:00.518
You think I do?

01:57:00.579 --> 01:57:02.940
Idiot, we won't hurt anyone.

01:57:03.000 --> 01:57:05.342
We just take the kid in the truck.

01:57:05.402 --> 01:57:07.433
Then we phone the hotel.

01:57:07.493 --> 01:57:11.808
We tell them to tell the parents they can have the kid when they leave us the money.

01:57:11.868 --> 01:57:13.070
It's easy.

01:57:13.130 --> 01:57:14.761
No one can get hurt.

01:57:14.821 --> 01:57:17.112
But Brandon was not so sure.

01:57:17.172 --> 01:57:19.534
He knew his brother was a criminal.

01:57:19.594 --> 01:57:26.617
He knew that he brewed illegal beer to sell and that he drove drugs across Alberta and British Columbia.

01:57:26.677 --> 01:57:28.908
But Brandon did not do that.

01:57:28.968 --> 01:57:31.069
He did not do any of that.

01:57:31.129 --> 01:57:33.480
He worked in the lumber yard.

01:57:33.540 --> 01:57:36.360
And he tried to do things right.

01:57:36.420 --> 01:57:38.912
He tried to be better than that.

01:57:38.972 --> 01:57:45.465
But now, Brandon, his brother said, I know you need the money.

01:57:45.525 --> 01:57:46.726
You don't have a job.

01:57:46.766 --> 01:57:48.375
Your truck is in pieces.

01:57:48.435 --> 01:57:51.034
You can't find work if you can't drive.

01:57:51.094 --> 01:57:52.805
What does Sally say?

01:57:52.865 --> 01:57:55.056
Brandon shook his head.

01:57:55.116 --> 01:57:57.688
Sally left me last month.

01:57:57.748 --> 01:58:01.510
He said, and he called the waiter for another beer.

01:58:01.570 --> 01:58:02.921
Tell me again.

01:58:02.981 --> 01:58:05.337
Tell me everything again.

01:58:05.397 --> 01:58:13.653
Now alone in the forest he thinks he can hear the sound of feet in the snow behind him, and he tries to move faster.

01:58:13.713 --> 01:58:31.726
There is another sound too, a sound that he recognises, a sound that's getting nearer, it's like constant thunder in the distance, and he pushes through the snow and he thinks that maybe if he can get to the sound he can flee into the mountains and never come back.

01:58:31.786 --> 01:58:35.017
Look every year, Greg said.

01:58:35.077 --> 01:58:37.498
Hundreds of tourists come here, right?

01:58:37.558 --> 01:58:39.308
Most of them are rich.

01:58:39.368 --> 01:58:41.809
And some of them are very rich.

01:58:41.869 --> 01:58:48.562
They want to ski in the spring, hike in the summer, and spend their money in the hotel bar in the spa.

01:58:48.622 --> 01:58:51.833
Some of these people are millionaires, Brandon.

01:58:51.893 --> 01:58:53.353
Millionaires?

01:58:53.413 --> 01:58:55.184
Think about that.

01:58:55.244 --> 01:58:58.889
Greg drank some more of his beer and looked around the bar.

01:58:58.949 --> 01:59:00.043
Look at them.

01:59:00.103 --> 01:59:02.144
Their clothes are better than ours.

01:59:02.204 --> 01:59:04.334
Their cars and trucks are better.

01:59:04.394 --> 01:59:06.354
Everything they have is better.

01:59:06.414 --> 01:59:08.425
And listen, I don't hate them for that.

01:59:08.485 --> 01:59:09.265
I don't.

01:59:09.325 --> 01:59:13.946
I just know that if we ask them for a small amount, then they can pay.

01:59:14.006 --> 01:59:15.386
I mean, 20,000.

01:59:15.446 --> 01:59:16.396
No more.

01:59:16.456 --> 01:59:18.866
If you ask for more, you have to wait.

01:59:18.926 --> 01:59:22.087
And then there's the risk that they contact the police.

01:59:22.147 --> 01:59:25.018
They can get 20,000 from the bank at bank.

01:59:25.078 --> 01:59:27.728
We tell them they only have six hours.

01:59:27.789 --> 01:59:31.202
We tell him to leave the money where we say, or...

01:59:31.262 --> 01:59:32.714
or what, Greg?

01:59:32.774 --> 01:59:33.904
Or what?

01:59:33.964 --> 01:59:37.858
On that first day, Brandon said no to the plan.

01:59:37.918 --> 01:59:40.622
On the second day, he said no again.

01:59:40.682 --> 01:59:44.164
On the third day, his truck stopped on the highway.

01:59:44.224 --> 01:59:47.668
He had about 300 bucks left in the world.

01:59:47.728 --> 01:59:49.480
Sally was gone.

01:59:49.540 --> 01:59:52.821
Their cabin by the lake was cold and empty.

01:59:52.881 --> 01:59:55.611
There were no jobs for him anywhere.

01:59:55.671 --> 01:59:59.102
So on the fourth day, he said yes.

01:59:59.162 --> 02:00:01.763
The sound is louder now.

02:00:01.823 --> 02:00:04.304
Yes, it's a sound he knows.

02:00:04.364 --> 02:00:06.448
A sound he knows well.

02:00:06.508 --> 02:00:11.524
He can see nothing because of the trees, but he knows he is very near.

02:00:11.584 --> 02:00:14.776
He feels warmer now, and this scares him.

02:00:14.836 --> 02:00:19.218
In Canada, in the mountains, when you're cold, you're okay.

02:00:19.278 --> 02:00:22.739
But when you're warm, you were in trouble.

02:00:22.799 --> 02:00:24.570
What time is it now?

02:00:24.630 --> 02:00:26.221
He does not know.

02:00:26.281 --> 02:00:32.454
He only knows that it was three in the afternoon when they first heard the sound of the police sirens.

02:00:32.514 --> 02:00:34.034
We have to go!

02:00:34.094 --> 02:00:35.852
Brandon shouted.

02:00:35.912 --> 02:00:36.730
How?

02:00:36.790 --> 02:00:38.452
How can they know we're here?"

02:00:38.512 --> 02:00:39.724
said Greg.

02:00:39.784 --> 02:00:44.369
Brandon pulled him from the old deserted cabin and they ran for Greg's truck.

02:00:44.428 --> 02:00:51.908
The sound of the sirens was still in the distance, and Brandon thought that maybe they were still okay and that maybe they could leave.

02:00:51.968 --> 02:00:54.300
Maybe they could escape together.

02:00:54.360 --> 02:01:02.456
But then there was that gunshot, and the man in the dark winter coat and black hat who appeared from behind the trees.

02:01:02.516 --> 02:01:11.052
So Brandon ran, and he got to the truck and managed to drive to the road, where for a few minutes he thought he was free.

02:01:11.112 --> 02:01:14.776
But then another truck appeared on the road behind him.

02:01:14.836 --> 02:01:16.549
But which kid?

02:01:16.609 --> 02:01:20.774
Brandon asked, as they planned everything in Greg's cabin.

02:01:20.834 --> 02:01:24.737
There's a family who comes here, maybe three or four times a year.

02:01:24.797 --> 02:01:27.860
The wife is a pretty little thing with red hair.

02:01:27.920 --> 02:01:31.614
I think she has family here, or maybe she comes from the area.

02:01:31.674 --> 02:01:32.785
I don't know.

02:01:32.845 --> 02:01:34.647
The husband is rich.

02:01:34.707 --> 02:01:36.058
Very rich.

02:01:36.118 --> 02:01:37.450
An American.

02:01:37.510 --> 02:01:39.770
The kid looks about eleven.

02:01:39.830 --> 02:01:43.800
and they let him walk into the town from the hotel sometimes.

02:01:43.860 --> 02:01:46.834
So we watch, and we wait.

02:01:46.894 --> 02:01:49.346
When he's alone, we take him.

02:01:49.406 --> 02:01:51.228
I've found the hotel.

02:01:51.288 --> 02:01:53.530
Six hours, that's it.

02:01:53.590 --> 02:01:56.862
That's enough time for them to get the money from Banff.

02:01:56.922 --> 02:02:00.334
But not enough to do anything else like phone the cops.

02:02:00.394 --> 02:02:01.664
It's easy.

02:02:01.724 --> 02:02:03.615
So that's what they did.

02:02:03.675 --> 02:02:06.526
They took the kid as he walked into town.

02:02:06.586 --> 02:02:09.256
There was no one on the snowy road.

02:02:09.316 --> 02:02:10.937
No one saw them.

02:02:10.997 --> 02:02:15.048
But they covered Greg's plates to be sure and they wore masks.

02:02:15.108 --> 02:02:19.530
The kids screamed at first, but they gagged him and tied his hands.

02:02:19.590 --> 02:02:24.862
Greg made the call to the hotel and they drove to the cabin in the Yoho National Park.

02:02:24.922 --> 02:02:26.382
Then they waited.

02:02:26.442 --> 02:02:30.374
Six hours to leave them the money in a safe place.

02:02:30.434 --> 02:02:33.065
No police or the kid dies.

02:02:33.125 --> 02:02:35.576
That's what Greg told the hotel.

02:02:35.636 --> 02:02:38.047
Even if that part of it was not true.

02:02:38.107 --> 02:02:40.548
So how did it all go wrong?

02:02:40.608 --> 02:02:48.154
There are fewer trees now, and the sound is louder, much louder, like the shouting of a giant.

02:02:48.214 --> 02:02:50.396
The tachycoir waterfalls.

02:02:50.456 --> 02:02:58.372
Brandon watches the clear water tumble onto the rocks, through the snow and down to the frozen forest below.

02:02:58.432 --> 02:03:00.174
Why here?

02:03:00.234 --> 02:03:01.906
He doesn't know.

02:03:01.966 --> 02:03:06.480
He tried to escape from the truck that chased him but he crashed on the highway.

02:03:06.540 --> 02:03:11.656
The next thing he knew he was holding his shoulder and walking confused and weak.

02:03:11.716 --> 02:03:13.488
The falls.

02:03:13.548 --> 02:03:15.388
So beautiful.

02:03:15.448 --> 02:03:18.112
Just like when he and Greg were kids.

02:03:18.171 --> 02:03:22.876
He wants to sit down to look at them and moves closer to the edge.

02:03:22.936 --> 02:03:25.298
But no, he cannot sit down.

02:03:25.358 --> 02:03:28.220
He's sure that the man with the gun is near.

02:03:28.280 --> 02:03:31.213
So instead he stands on the edge.

02:03:31.272 --> 02:03:35.706
He feels warm and weak, and his shoulder hurts.

02:03:35.766 --> 02:03:38.537
And he can't remember where Greg is.

02:03:38.597 --> 02:03:42.470
He said it was easy, he says.

02:03:42.530 --> 02:03:46.002
But it wasn't, a voice replies.

02:03:46.062 --> 02:03:49.223
And Brandon turns and sees the dark coat.

02:03:49.283 --> 02:03:56.008
Only this time there is no black hat, just long red hair, and a rifle.

02:03:56.068 --> 02:03:57.898
How?

02:03:57.958 --> 02:04:00.394
How did you find us?

02:04:00.454 --> 02:04:03.310
You only had a few hours to get the money.

02:04:03.370 --> 02:04:09.381
My husband went for the money, says the woman, rifling one hand's black hat in the other.

02:04:09.441 --> 02:04:12.452
But I went to the road where you took my son.

02:04:12.512 --> 02:04:16.915
I saw the tracks from your truck, and I told the police.

02:04:16.975 --> 02:04:20.286
But I made sure I found the cabin first.

02:04:20.347 --> 02:04:25.019
I know these mountains better than they do, and better than you.

02:04:25.079 --> 02:04:28.430
I'm sorry, Brandon says.

02:04:28.490 --> 02:04:31.068
I really am sorry.

02:04:31.128 --> 02:04:35.276
The boy is okay, he's in the back room of the cabin.

02:04:35.336 --> 02:04:38.439
I know, the woman says.

02:04:38.500 --> 02:04:40.842
Are you going to shoot me?

02:04:40.902 --> 02:04:42.524
He asks.

02:04:42.584 --> 02:04:46.348
There is only the sound of the waterfall for a moment.

02:04:46.408 --> 02:04:50.241
Then the woman turns and moves back into the trees.

02:04:50.301 --> 02:04:57.022
He feels his leg shake and falls down onto the red and white snow by the waterfall.

02:04:57.082 --> 02:05:03.700
I already did," she calls back to him as she slowly disappears from sight.

02:05:03.760 --> 02:05:06.772
The old school tie.

02:05:06.832 --> 02:05:14.016
The police car stops in the driveway of the school and the two detectives get out and look around.

02:05:14.076 --> 02:05:23.934
Detective Smith is older, his hair a mixture of black and gray, his stomach large and his expression usually unhappy.

02:05:23.994 --> 02:05:30.460
The other detective West is younger and thinner, but with an empty look in his eye.

02:05:30.521 --> 02:05:34.113
Look at this place, says Smith.

02:05:34.173 --> 02:05:41.533
In the distance, the church towers, cathedral and university buildings of the city of Oxford are all visible.

02:05:41.593 --> 02:05:49.344
Around the old but impressive school there are green gardens and grounds that continue for miles.

02:05:49.404 --> 02:05:56.488
And at the windows of the building there are the thirty faces of serious young men who watch them strangely.

02:05:56.548 --> 02:06:01.069
God, what kind of kid goes to a school like this?

02:06:01.129 --> 02:06:04.620
West nods, but does not say anything.

02:06:04.680 --> 02:06:11.771
After only two weeks of working with Smith, he already knows that it's best to just nod.

02:06:11.831 --> 02:06:13.422
Come on.

02:06:13.482 --> 02:06:16.752
Smith says, and they walk to the entrance.

02:06:16.812 --> 02:06:25.994
Inside, the school smells of wood and polish, and on the walls there are antique pictures of old teachers and respected students.

02:06:26.054 --> 02:06:28.256
Not like my school.

02:06:28.316 --> 02:06:29.798
Sir?

02:06:29.858 --> 02:06:32.320
I went to a real school.

02:06:32.380 --> 02:06:34.092
A comprehensive.

02:06:34.152 --> 02:06:35.512
You?

02:06:35.572 --> 02:06:37.774
A grammar school, sir.

02:06:37.834 --> 02:06:40.958
And Smith nods his head and says nothing.

02:06:41.018 --> 02:06:47.382
The secretary outside the headmaster's room tells them they can enter, but Smith stops.

02:06:47.442 --> 02:06:50.323
Listen, West, I do the talking.

02:06:50.383 --> 02:06:52.264
You make the notes.

02:06:52.324 --> 02:06:57.338
The best thing a good detective can do is listen and observe.

02:06:57.398 --> 02:06:59.150
Write that down.

02:06:59.210 --> 02:07:00.602
Yes, sir.

02:07:00.662 --> 02:07:04.454
The headmaster is a man about the same age as Smith.

02:07:04.514 --> 02:07:07.466
But there the comparison ends.

02:07:07.526 --> 02:07:11.590
Smith looks tired, old and unhappy.

02:07:11.650 --> 02:07:17.606
The headmaster, Mr. Bowen, does not exactly look happy, but he looks content.

02:07:17.666 --> 02:07:24.410
content with his job, his life, his expensive house and his two expensive cars.

02:07:24.470 --> 02:07:27.431
Smith hates him instantly.

02:07:27.491 --> 02:07:33.083
The secretary, a young and pretty blonde, enters the room and sits by the table.

02:07:33.143 --> 02:07:38.202
The headmaster tells them to sit in the same voice he uses for his students.

02:07:38.262 --> 02:07:43.212
Miss White here is to take notes and give you some more details about Mr. Fletcher.

02:07:43.272 --> 02:07:44.882
Mr. Fletcher?

02:07:44.942 --> 02:07:46.593
asks Smith.

02:07:46.653 --> 02:07:48.034
Yes.

02:07:48.094 --> 02:07:52.245
Bowen stands by the large windows and does not look at them.

02:07:52.305 --> 02:07:55.116
He's why you're here, don't you know that?

02:07:55.176 --> 02:08:00.308
No, the chief inspector told us to come, but he didn't say why.

02:08:00.368 --> 02:08:02.189
Ah, yes, James.

02:08:02.249 --> 02:08:04.736
We play golf together, good man.

02:08:04.796 --> 02:08:10.716
Well, the problem is that we can't find one of our members of staff, Mr Fletcher.

02:08:10.776 --> 02:08:14.287
A missing person, I see.

02:08:14.347 --> 02:08:16.690
When did you last see him?

02:08:16.750 --> 02:08:18.322
Miss White?

02:08:18.381 --> 02:08:21.384
His last class was yesterday at four.

02:08:21.444 --> 02:08:24.536
He was teaching the upper sixth form boys, geometry.

02:08:24.596 --> 02:08:27.738
They say he finished class at five and left as normal.

02:08:27.798 --> 02:08:29.250
I see.

02:08:29.310 --> 02:08:31.482
Then he went home?

02:08:31.542 --> 02:08:36.446
Mr. Fletcher lives on the grounds of the school several of the older teachers do.

02:08:36.506 --> 02:08:39.598
He lives alone on the second floor in the east wing.

02:08:39.658 --> 02:08:42.599
Two other teachers live there, but neither saw him.

02:08:42.659 --> 02:08:49.190
Smith is about to speak when a loud hammering sound starts, and Bowen shakes his head.

02:08:49.250 --> 02:08:51.791
Sorry about that building work.

02:08:51.851 --> 02:08:54.882
Several areas in the school are being renovated.

02:08:54.942 --> 02:08:56.852
Smith speaks up.

02:08:56.912 --> 02:09:00.153
And did he leave the school last night?

02:09:00.214 --> 02:09:01.394
We don't know.

02:09:01.454 --> 02:09:02.514
He normally doesn't.

02:09:02.574 --> 02:09:06.415
He takes his meals in the room, but last night he didn't call for anything.

02:09:06.475 --> 02:09:08.304
Is that strange?

02:09:08.364 --> 02:09:09.892
Not uncommon.

02:09:09.952 --> 02:09:13.988
Sometimes he prefers to have a sherry and a cigar and nothing more.

02:09:14.048 --> 02:09:17.713
But when he didn't come to breakfast this morning, we began to worry.

02:09:17.773 --> 02:09:19.896
Smith looks at the clock.

02:09:19.956 --> 02:09:26.746
Mr. Bowen, normally we need to wait 48 hours until we can start a missing person investigation.

02:09:26.806 --> 02:09:31.400
Bowen turns from the window and gives Smith an annoyed look.

02:09:31.460 --> 02:09:33.851
Your chief inspector said you could help.

02:09:33.911 --> 02:09:35.222
Was he wrong?

02:09:35.282 --> 02:09:38.273
Smith sighs and shakes his head.

02:09:38.333 --> 02:09:41.814
Does Mr. Fletcher have any good friends here?

02:09:41.874 --> 02:09:44.466
Or more importantly, enemies?

02:09:44.526 --> 02:09:48.357
He is a solitary man, but respected.

02:09:48.417 --> 02:09:52.748
He is the oldest teacher at Barnaby's, practically part of the building.

02:09:52.808 --> 02:09:55.207
Bowen stops and thinks.

02:09:55.267 --> 02:09:58.468
He doesn't like our new music teacher, Mr. Cliff.

02:09:58.528 --> 02:10:01.190
Didn't they have some sort of fight recently, Miss White?

02:10:01.250 --> 02:10:03.070
I don't think so.

02:10:03.130 --> 02:10:05.131
She says quietly.

02:10:05.191 --> 02:10:07.312
And what about the students?

02:10:07.372 --> 02:10:09.563
Smith asks them both.

02:10:09.623 --> 02:10:14.994
The students, replies Bowen in an insulted tone.

02:10:15.054 --> 02:10:21.085
Detective, the young men at this school are very clever and come from very important families.

02:10:21.145 --> 02:10:25.106
In a few years they will be politicians, judges or doctors.

02:10:25.166 --> 02:10:27.396
They are not criminals.

02:10:27.456 --> 02:10:30.327
Smith breathes out with a puff.

02:10:30.387 --> 02:10:31.787
No.

02:10:31.847 --> 02:10:35.258
Well, I suppose we could have a look around the school.

02:10:35.318 --> 02:10:37.930
Good, says Bowen.

02:10:37.990 --> 02:10:42.662
Show them out, Miss White, and the headmaster does not look at them again.

02:10:42.722 --> 02:10:46.305
Jesus, says Smith in the corridor.

02:10:46.365 --> 02:10:48.546
Who does he think he is?

02:10:48.606 --> 02:10:52.300
Just because he plays golf with the chief inspector?

02:10:52.360 --> 02:10:54.952
Waste of bloody time this is West.

02:10:55.012 --> 02:10:56.354
Did you hear him?

02:10:56.414 --> 02:10:58.786
This Fletcher likes a sherry.

02:10:58.846 --> 02:11:00.687
He's drunk somewhere.

02:11:00.747 --> 02:11:02.298
That's all this is.

02:11:02.358 --> 02:11:04.530
In some pub in the city.

02:11:04.590 --> 02:11:07.122
And West nods again.

02:11:07.182 --> 02:11:12.268
They walk along the corridors for five minutes and reach an internal courtyard.

02:11:12.328 --> 02:11:15.602
Outside they can see the renovation work.

02:11:15.662 --> 02:11:23.997
There's scaffolding on the face of the old building, a new wall on the side of it and three or four workmen are sitting on a bench eating lunch.

02:11:24.058 --> 02:11:31.292
They cross the courtyard, and Smith stops to pick up a school tie on the floor by the new wall.

02:11:31.352 --> 02:11:36.114
Politicians and doctors, they can't even dress themselves.

02:11:36.174 --> 02:11:39.106
And Smith puts the tie in his pocket.

02:11:39.166 --> 02:11:44.070
Sir, look, West says and points to the top of a tower.

02:11:44.130 --> 02:11:45.211
What?

02:11:45.271 --> 02:11:47.683
There are two people watching us, sir.

02:11:47.743 --> 02:11:48.904
And?

02:11:48.964 --> 02:11:50.984
Well, just observing, sir.

02:11:51.044 --> 02:11:52.675
Maybe there's a connection.

02:11:52.735 --> 02:11:54.466
Smith's eyes.

02:11:54.526 --> 02:11:57.887
I do the thinking, he says.

02:11:57.947 --> 02:12:00.428
Okay, one look and then we go.

02:12:00.488 --> 02:12:04.732
We can also check one or two of the local pubs on the way back to the city.

02:12:04.792 --> 02:12:11.138
The door to the tower is in another corridor, and when they open it they hear footsteps on the stairs.

02:12:11.198 --> 02:12:12.279
Who's that?

02:12:12.339 --> 02:12:13.810
Smith shouts.

02:12:13.870 --> 02:12:18.022
The footsteps stop and miss White's steps into view.

02:12:18.082 --> 02:12:19.254
You?

02:12:19.314 --> 02:12:21.886
Were you watching us from the roof?

02:12:21.946 --> 02:12:24.217
No, well, not really.

02:12:24.277 --> 02:12:26.538
The secretary says quietly.

02:12:26.598 --> 02:12:28.330
Who else is with you?

02:12:28.389 --> 02:12:30.090
Mr. Fletcher.

02:12:30.150 --> 02:12:31.112
What?

02:12:31.172 --> 02:12:32.229
No.

02:12:32.290 --> 02:12:35.870
Be honest now, or there might be trouble for you.

02:12:35.930 --> 02:12:40.754
It's my fault," a voice says, and a young man steps forward.

02:12:40.814 --> 02:12:43.176
Miss White came to speak to me.

02:12:43.236 --> 02:12:44.436
Oh, really?

02:12:44.496 --> 02:12:45.728
And who are you?

02:12:45.788 --> 02:12:48.540
I'm the music teacher, Mr. Cliff.

02:12:48.600 --> 02:12:51.722
Ah, well, isn't that interesting?

02:12:51.782 --> 02:12:54.174
No, it's not what you think.

02:12:54.234 --> 02:12:58.608
I came here to tell Mr. Cliff what the headmaster said about the argument.

02:12:58.668 --> 02:13:00.098
Go on.

02:13:00.158 --> 02:13:02.221
Look, says Cliff.

02:13:02.281 --> 02:13:03.882
It was nothing, really.

02:13:03.942 --> 02:13:08.314
Only Fletcher said something about Miss White, something no gentleman should say.

02:13:08.374 --> 02:13:11.096
I told him to apologise, and then we argued.

02:13:11.156 --> 02:13:14.317
But the last time I saw him was yesterday morning.

02:13:14.377 --> 02:13:15.788
It's true!"

02:13:15.848 --> 02:13:17.398
adds Miss White.

02:13:17.458 --> 02:13:20.720
Well, we might need to talk to you again at another time.

02:13:20.780 --> 02:13:24.132
Smith says and turns to go back to the courtyard.

02:13:24.192 --> 02:13:25.862
But then he stops.

02:13:25.922 --> 02:13:29.674
How did you know Mr. Cliff was here, Miss White?

02:13:29.734 --> 02:13:31.741
The Secretary blushes.

02:13:31.801 --> 02:13:34.418
We meet here, sometimes.

02:13:34.478 --> 02:13:35.969
For lunch.

02:13:36.029 --> 02:13:38.280
Ah, I see.

02:13:38.340 --> 02:13:42.432
Then the hammering they heard in the headmaster's office starts again.

02:13:42.492 --> 02:13:44.904
And the policeman go to the door.

02:13:44.964 --> 02:13:47.786
Detective, says the secretary.

02:13:47.846 --> 02:13:50.106
You asked about the students.

02:13:50.166 --> 02:13:52.218
They hate Mr. Fletcher.

02:13:52.278 --> 02:13:55.210
They say he punishes them for every mistake.

02:13:55.269 --> 02:13:58.942
Well, students normally don't like teachers, do they?

02:13:59.002 --> 02:14:02.006
Fletcher is worse, says Cliff.

02:14:02.066 --> 02:14:04.030
He's a dinosaur.

02:14:04.090 --> 02:14:06.621
I think he hits the boy sometimes.

02:14:06.681 --> 02:14:08.292
But I can't prove it.

02:14:08.352 --> 02:14:09.912
Smith nods.

02:14:09.972 --> 02:14:11.693
I'm sure it's nothing.

02:14:11.753 --> 02:14:17.825
Back in the courtyard, Smith stops by the workman, who are still sitting and eating their lunch.

02:14:17.885 --> 02:14:20.616
Listen and observe, West.

02:14:20.676 --> 02:14:25.957
I suppose you didn't notice, but those two are having some sort of romantic affair.

02:14:26.017 --> 02:14:29.140
Er, well, yes, I did notice, sir.

02:14:29.200 --> 02:14:30.772
You did.

02:14:30.832 --> 02:14:31.974
Good.

02:14:32.034 --> 02:14:37.477
I can teach you a few things, I think, West, and Smith nods to the workmen.

02:14:37.537 --> 02:14:40.189
Should we speak to the students, sir?

02:14:40.249 --> 02:14:43.592
West asks as they walk back to the car.

02:14:43.652 --> 02:14:45.112
What for?

02:14:45.172 --> 02:14:47.394
Well, to see if they know anything.

02:14:47.454 --> 02:14:50.096
Smith takes the tie from his pocket.

02:14:50.156 --> 02:14:52.127
They're children, West.

02:14:52.187 --> 02:14:54.458
They can't even put a tie on.

02:14:54.518 --> 02:14:56.518
Let's get back to the city.

02:14:56.578 --> 02:14:58.979
And he throws the tie on the floor.

02:14:59.039 --> 02:15:00.790
West nods.

02:15:00.850 --> 02:15:03.381
It's best to just nod.

02:15:03.441 --> 02:15:06.922
But there is something that does not make sense.

02:15:06.982 --> 02:15:09.223
The hammering noise.

02:15:09.283 --> 02:15:12.164
They heard it in the headmaster's office.

02:15:12.224 --> 02:15:16.166
And they heard it in the corridor with Miss White and Mr Cliff.

02:15:16.226 --> 02:15:20.693
And he looks back at the workman who were enjoying a slow lunch.

02:15:20.753 --> 02:15:23.600
but if these men are not hammering.

02:15:23.660 --> 02:15:25.430
Come on West!

02:15:25.490 --> 02:15:28.581
Smith shouts, and West nods.

02:15:28.641 --> 02:15:36.964
Yes, it's best to just nod, and somewhere in the dark Mr. Fletcher stops hammering his foot against the new wall.

02:15:37.024 --> 02:15:40.885
He is tired now, very tired.

02:15:40.945 --> 02:15:44.246
He tries to remember exactly what happened.

02:15:44.306 --> 02:15:50.209
He remembers hitting that idiot of a boy Harris because he made a mistake in the geometry class.

02:15:50.269 --> 02:15:58.164
He remembers another boy telling him to stop, then another boy and another, and then they were all on top of him.

02:15:58.224 --> 02:16:20.369
At first they only hit him, but then he felt schooled ties being wrapped around his hands and feet and mouth, and they lifted him up and carried him to the new wall which was nearly finished, and they put him behind it in a deep, dark corner when no one could see him, and the light disappeared as they put the last bricks in place.

02:16:20.429 --> 02:16:29.008
He tried desperately to shout, but he could not, and he tried to move, but he could not.

02:16:29.068 --> 02:16:34.601
And for a while he thought that he would die in the deep dark corner behind the wall.

02:16:34.661 --> 02:16:38.480
But everything will be okay now, he knows.

02:16:38.541 --> 02:16:44.044
Because after hours of struggling, his foot is now finally free to move.

02:16:44.103 --> 02:16:49.116
And the police will be here soon, he is sure, and they will hear.

02:16:49.176 --> 02:16:53.038
So he continues to hammer his foot against the wall.

02:16:53.098 --> 02:16:58.552
And he hopes that someone hears him soon.

02:16:58.612 --> 02:17:08.449
The Poachers "'My God, just look at that, Junior!'

02:17:08.510 --> 02:17:13.732
Owen says with a smile and he points to the valley and green forest below them.

02:17:13.792 --> 02:17:18.274
"'Abba true where in forest, what a beauty, Alad!'

02:17:18.334 --> 02:17:25.376
And he looks to his son, Owen Junior, and he wants to see a smile on the boy's pale face.

02:17:25.436 --> 02:17:29.324
But like always, there's nothing but disinterest.

02:17:29.384 --> 02:17:32.961
We saw it last year, Junior says.

02:17:33.021 --> 02:17:35.161
It just looks the same.

02:17:35.221 --> 02:17:36.852
The same?

02:17:36.912 --> 02:17:39.702
Well, I suppose, but look at it's son.

02:17:39.762 --> 02:17:41.763
Look at how beautiful it is.

02:17:41.823 --> 02:17:44.933
Can there be a finer place in Wales?

02:17:44.994 --> 02:17:46.624
I don't think so.

02:17:46.684 --> 02:17:49.235
But Junior only shrugs.

02:17:49.294 --> 02:17:51.505
Owen shakes his head.

02:17:51.565 --> 02:17:56.918
He remembers when he came here with his father to poach a few rabbits as a child.

02:17:56.978 --> 02:18:00.279
He loved the walk up from the town of Sennibridge.

02:18:00.339 --> 02:18:04.521
He loved the hours of walking and hunting in the forests and valley.

02:18:04.581 --> 02:18:06.352
But not Junior.

02:18:06.412 --> 02:18:10.745
Junior said that to walk there was stupid because they had a car.

02:18:10.804 --> 02:18:15.647
And despite all of Owen's arguments, the teenager did not change his mind.

02:18:15.707 --> 02:18:20.441
Owen looks back at the car and hopes that no one can see it from the main road.

02:18:20.502 --> 02:18:25.296
He lifts the two gun bags onto his shoulder and starts to walk.

02:18:25.356 --> 02:18:27.068
Come on, son.

02:18:27.128 --> 02:18:30.791
This is going to be great fun, he says.

02:18:30.852 --> 02:18:34.032
And Junior follows him but says nothing.

02:18:34.092 --> 02:18:37.611
The forest is alive with life.

02:18:37.672 --> 02:18:42.074
It's the second week of April and spring is certainly here.

02:18:42.134 --> 02:18:45.466
The grass below their feet is green and fresh.

02:18:45.526 --> 02:18:52.079
The trees are heavy with leaves and the air smells so good that Owen forgets about Junior's mood.

02:18:52.139 --> 02:18:53.820
Smell that, son.

02:18:53.880 --> 02:18:54.821
Good, eh?

02:18:54.881 --> 02:18:58.984
That's the scent of life, the scent of the real whales.

02:18:59.044 --> 02:19:01.105
Junior lifts his head.

02:19:01.165 --> 02:19:03.126
I can't smell anything.

02:19:03.186 --> 02:19:04.047
What?

02:19:04.107 --> 02:19:07.360
The flowers, the trees, spring.

02:19:07.420 --> 02:19:08.730
Oh, that.

02:19:08.790 --> 02:19:15.070
And the boy pulls something out of his pocket, and Owen sees that it's his MP3 player.

02:19:15.130 --> 02:19:16.854
Whoa, whoa, whoa.

02:19:16.915 --> 02:19:19.075
He says, wait a minute, son.

02:19:19.095 --> 02:19:20.816
You don't need that today.

02:19:20.876 --> 02:19:21.746
What?

02:19:21.807 --> 02:19:23.927
But I want to listen to my music.

02:19:23.987 --> 02:19:25.278
Music?

02:19:25.338 --> 02:19:27.679
Listen to the forest lad.

02:19:27.739 --> 02:19:29.979
Listen to the birds in the wind.

02:19:30.040 --> 02:19:32.390
No, you don't need that today.

02:19:32.450 --> 02:19:36.672
He repeats and he takes the MP3 player from the boy's hands.

02:19:36.732 --> 02:19:40.804
And he tries to avoid the angry look in the boy's dark eyes.

02:19:40.864 --> 02:19:42.434
Dark eyes?

02:19:42.494 --> 02:19:44.164
Just like his mothers.

02:19:44.224 --> 02:19:48.226
and dark hair too, just like his mothers.

02:19:48.286 --> 02:19:56.182
Owen is tall and fair, with blue eyes and a healthy complexion from years of work and activity outside.

02:19:56.242 --> 02:20:06.550
Junior is short for his age, short and thin with a pale and unhealthy complexion from hours of playing his computer games and watching television.

02:20:06.610 --> 02:20:12.590
When Owen was a boy of fourteen, he played rugby or football every day.

02:20:12.650 --> 02:20:19.916
He ran to the school in the village just for fun, and he spent every Sunday in the forest with his dad.

02:20:19.976 --> 02:20:22.317
Junior does not like sports.

02:20:22.377 --> 02:20:30.073
He does not run, and the last time he came to the forest with Owen, he said that it was too cold and that the forest was boring.

02:20:30.134 --> 02:20:33.416
But Owen does not like to think about that too much.

02:20:33.476 --> 02:20:37.059
His wife, Rhea, is a good woman, and pretty too.

02:20:37.119 --> 02:20:41.282
But sometimes he cannot help but look at Junior and wonder.

02:20:41.342 --> 02:20:43.804
So he tries not to think about it.

02:20:43.864 --> 02:20:51.592
Rhea says that it's just his age, and that all teenage boys prefer computer games and television to walks in the forest.

02:20:51.652 --> 02:20:57.456
And yes, the last time they came to the forest it was a bit cold, and they had nothing to do.

02:20:57.516 --> 02:20:59.368
But not this time.

02:20:59.428 --> 02:21:03.011
No, this time the sun is high and hot.

02:21:03.072 --> 02:21:13.496
and this time Owen has his old guns over his shoulder, and he remembers the way that Junior's expressionless face changed when he saw them the night before.

02:21:13.556 --> 02:21:20.230
These were your grandad's guns, he said to him in the kitchen, and one day they can be yours if you want.

02:21:20.290 --> 02:21:21.310
Really?

02:21:21.370 --> 02:21:23.050
And I can shoot things?

02:21:23.110 --> 02:21:24.701
Animals and things?

02:21:24.761 --> 02:21:28.469
Of course lad, but I need to show you how first.

02:21:28.529 --> 02:21:30.006
But I know how.

02:21:30.066 --> 02:21:31.128
You do?

02:21:31.188 --> 02:21:34.150
Yeah, I saw how in films and on television.

02:21:34.210 --> 02:21:35.511
Oh, in laughed.

02:21:35.572 --> 02:21:38.826
Oh, well, I need to show you how to do it safely.

02:21:38.886 --> 02:21:42.930
So we need to go up to the old Abba-Trawaran forest.

02:21:42.990 --> 02:21:45.902
And Owen knew the boy was interested then.

02:21:45.962 --> 02:21:51.713
And for the first time in a long time, he thought that maybe there was something they could do together.

02:21:51.773 --> 02:21:54.880
Father and Son, the way it should be.

02:21:54.940 --> 02:21:58.231
But now, in the forest, he's not so sure.

02:21:58.291 --> 02:22:04.304
The boy looks bored already, and he's not looking at the gun bags with interest anymore.

02:22:04.364 --> 02:22:07.096
Well, maybe when he gets to Chuchuan.

02:22:07.155 --> 02:22:12.148
Soon they are deep in the forest, and Owen finds a small clearing.

02:22:12.208 --> 02:22:16.520
He puts the guns next to a tree and takes them out of the bags carefully.

02:22:16.580 --> 02:22:20.232
And yes, there is the interest in the boy's eyes.

02:22:20.293 --> 02:22:22.074
So what can we shoot?

02:22:22.134 --> 02:22:23.776
Junior asks.

02:22:23.836 --> 02:22:26.838
At this time of year, we want to look for rabbit's son.

02:22:26.898 --> 02:22:29.670
But if we can get a hair, even better.

02:22:29.730 --> 02:22:31.040
What about deer?

02:22:31.100 --> 02:22:32.532
Can we shoot them?

02:22:32.592 --> 02:22:33.752
Dear?

02:22:33.812 --> 02:22:34.934
No, son.

02:22:34.994 --> 02:22:36.284
They're too big.

02:22:36.345 --> 02:22:39.006
We don't want to carry a deer back to the car.

02:22:39.066 --> 02:22:40.568
Someone might see us.

02:22:40.628 --> 02:22:41.558
So?

02:22:41.618 --> 02:22:45.822
And Owen realises that the boy does not understand what poaching is.

02:22:45.882 --> 02:22:49.744
Well, you see, really, we aren't supposed to be here.

02:22:49.804 --> 02:22:50.833
What?

02:22:50.894 --> 02:22:54.003
Junior says, a scared look in his eyes.

02:22:54.063 --> 02:22:55.284
Why not?

02:22:55.344 --> 02:23:00.015
Well, to some people, shooting here is illegal, they call it poaching.

02:23:00.075 --> 02:23:01.596
Which people?

02:23:01.656 --> 02:23:05.847
Well, the police, for example, and the forestry commission.

02:23:05.907 --> 02:23:08.178
Junior looks around the forest.

02:23:08.238 --> 02:23:10.119
You mean it's a crime?

02:23:10.179 --> 02:23:11.689
It's illegal.

02:23:11.749 --> 02:23:13.985
Owen thinks about what to say.

02:23:14.045 --> 02:23:18.952
Well, it is, son, but only like downloading films for free.

02:23:19.012 --> 02:23:21.462
That's a crime, that's illegal.

02:23:21.522 --> 02:23:23.833
But you still do that, don't you?

02:23:23.893 --> 02:23:25.784
Well, this is just like that.

02:23:25.844 --> 02:23:27.574
No one gets hurt.

02:23:27.634 --> 02:23:30.756
We take one or two rabbits home and we have a little fun.

02:23:30.816 --> 02:23:32.256
That's okay, isn't it?

02:23:32.316 --> 02:23:33.956
Junior nods.

02:23:34.016 --> 02:23:35.588
I suppose so.

02:23:35.648 --> 02:23:37.709
But what if someone sees us?

02:23:37.770 --> 02:23:39.841
Ah, good question, son.

02:23:39.901 --> 02:23:44.254
No, not many people come to this side of the forest at this time of the year.

02:23:44.314 --> 02:23:49.689
But if you see someone, you just put the gun down gently in a bush or under some leaves.

02:23:49.749 --> 02:23:53.082
Remember to do it nice and gently and to say hello.

02:23:53.142 --> 02:23:55.714
If you have no gun, then there's no problem.

02:23:55.774 --> 02:23:58.226
We can come back for the gun another day.

02:23:58.286 --> 02:23:59.197
Okay?

02:23:59.257 --> 02:24:06.962
So remember, if you see or hear someone, put it down nice and gently, and there'll be no problem.

02:24:07.023 --> 02:24:20.832
Junior looks a bit happier now, and for twenty minutes Owen talks to him about how to hold the gun, and how to walk with it, while his son listens carefully and asks sensible questions that show a real interest.

02:24:20.892 --> 02:24:35.581
For the first time in many years, Owen and Junior seem to be exactly what they are, father and son, and Owen notices that the boy actually looks a little bit like him after all, and that he also smiles sometimes.

02:24:35.641 --> 02:24:41.424
Sure, he likes films and television and not sports, but maybe this can be their thing.

02:24:41.484 --> 02:24:46.394
It doesn't have to be every weekend if Junior doesn't want to, but occasionally.

02:24:46.454 --> 02:24:52.414
Then maybe in a few years they can start to go for a pint, like Owen and his father did.

02:24:52.474 --> 02:24:59.706
Right then, son, he says, feeling extremely happy and enjoying the excitement in the boy's eyes.

02:24:59.766 --> 02:25:04.957
Now I'm going to load the guns, then we can follow the path there into the forest.

02:25:05.017 --> 02:25:07.729
Do you remember the rules about the gun, son?

02:25:07.789 --> 02:25:09.050
Yes, Dad.

02:25:09.110 --> 02:25:13.014
Keep the gun pointed down to the ground unless I see a rabbit.

02:25:13.074 --> 02:25:15.986
Never point the gun at you or anyone else.

02:25:16.046 --> 02:25:19.490
Keep to your left but not directly behind you.

02:25:19.550 --> 02:25:23.612
Don't shoot and as I can clearly see that there's no one near the target.

02:25:23.672 --> 02:25:28.785
And don't forget there are two shots, and that I should empty the gun before I give it back to you."

02:25:28.845 --> 02:25:41.399
Exactly," says Owen, and he feels full of pride and thinks what a wonderful day this is, out in the beauty of the Welsh countryside with his son, and for a while they walk.

02:25:41.459 --> 02:25:48.362
And he's happy about how carefully Junior holds the gun, and how he listens and does exactly what he should.

02:25:48.422 --> 02:25:52.845
But then they reach another clearing, and they hear something behind the trees.

02:25:52.905 --> 02:25:53.936
Tat!

02:25:53.996 --> 02:25:55.496
Junior whispers.

02:25:55.556 --> 02:25:57.347
And he looks scared.

02:25:57.407 --> 02:25:59.088
What's that noise?

02:25:59.148 --> 02:26:00.649
Is it the police?

02:26:00.709 --> 02:26:03.390
And Owen signals him to be quiet.

02:26:03.450 --> 02:26:06.832
He tries to see through the trees, but he cannot.

02:26:06.892 --> 02:26:10.237
He thinks there is someone or something moving.

02:26:10.297 --> 02:26:16.731
and it's big, bigger than a rabbit or a hare, but he does not think it can be a person.

02:26:16.791 --> 02:26:19.652
He never sees people up here at this time of year.

02:26:19.712 --> 02:26:21.952
In the summer maybe, but not now.

02:26:22.012 --> 02:26:25.203
Could it be a park ranger from the Forestry Commission?

02:26:25.263 --> 02:26:29.083
Did someone see the car parked in the trees and call the police?

02:26:29.143 --> 02:26:38.670
There is a snap from behind the trees, and Owen thinks that maybe he should put his gun down in a bush, nice and carefully like he told Junior to do.

02:26:38.730 --> 02:26:47.829
Behind him he can hear Junior, who sounds like he is very scared, but then Owen sees what is behind the trees.

02:26:47.889 --> 02:26:51.120
Brown hair, big gentle eyes.

02:26:51.180 --> 02:26:55.093
He laughs and turns back to Junior to tell him to stay calm.

02:26:55.153 --> 02:27:01.836
But before he can do this, Junior, who does not know what is making the noise, remembers his dad's words.

02:27:01.896 --> 02:27:05.287
If you have no gun, then there's no problem.

02:27:05.347 --> 02:27:07.698
But he does not remember the other words.

02:27:07.758 --> 02:27:10.089
Put it down gently.

02:27:10.149 --> 02:27:16.492
Owen sees the boy throw the gun and tries to shout that it's just a deer, but it's too late.

02:27:16.552 --> 02:27:18.832
There is a loud bang.

02:27:18.892 --> 02:27:24.785
Owen feels an incredible pain in his behind, and he screams and falls to the floor.

02:27:24.845 --> 02:27:48.072
As he lies there, shouting and screaming and trying to pull the pieces of shot from his skin, he looks up at the beautiful blue sky and green trees and magnificent forest, and he thinks that maybe next time they should just go to the cinema instead, or maybe play a computer game, or watch television.

02:27:48.132 --> 02:27:55.894
The Card Shark Are you sitting comfortably?

02:27:55.954 --> 02:27:57.264
Good.

02:27:57.324 --> 02:28:00.788
I want to tell you a little story that I know.

02:28:00.848 --> 02:28:03.530
Now it's not a very nice story.

02:28:03.590 --> 02:28:07.724
And I can't promise you that there will be a very happy ending.

02:28:07.784 --> 02:28:09.916
But it's a true story.

02:28:09.976 --> 02:28:13.279
And that's something that's very important to me.

02:28:13.340 --> 02:28:15.702
And I want you to remember that.

02:28:15.762 --> 02:28:19.762
Yeah, you need to remember that the truth is important to me.

02:28:19.822 --> 02:28:21.350
And you know what?

02:28:21.410 --> 02:28:24.801
It's something that's very important to you, too.

02:28:24.861 --> 02:28:26.831
Or it should be.

02:28:26.891 --> 02:28:30.182
So relax a little and listen.

02:28:30.242 --> 02:28:37.224
See, there was once a young man called Jimmy Lane, an Englishman from the great city of London.

02:28:37.284 --> 02:28:40.844
Well, Jimmy was a nobody in many ways.

02:28:40.904 --> 02:28:43.997
You know what I mean when I say a nobody.

02:28:44.057 --> 02:28:47.954
I mean Jimmy wasn't rich, and he wasn't too bright, you know?

02:28:48.014 --> 02:28:50.066
Not very clever, really.

02:28:50.126 --> 02:28:54.272
And he wasn't a funny guy, and he didn't have much luck with the ladies.

02:28:54.332 --> 02:28:56.774
I know all this to be true.

02:28:56.834 --> 02:29:02.946
I know it because I can read people, and I could see all this when I look to Jimmy Lane.

02:29:03.006 --> 02:29:08.310
However, Jimmy had a talent, a skill, an ability.

02:29:08.370 --> 02:29:11.051
You see, Jimmy could play cards.

02:29:11.111 --> 02:29:15.984
Poker, Blackjack, Rummy, you name the game and Jimmy could play it.

02:29:16.044 --> 02:29:18.905
Though he especially liked poker.

02:29:18.966 --> 02:29:23.292
With a deck of cards in his hands, Jimmy was like a magician.

02:29:23.352 --> 02:29:28.158
He could cut and shuffle the deck like he had eight arms instead of two.

02:29:28.218 --> 02:29:38.171
And he could remember every card he saw in a split second, and could use his fingers and little tricks to put the cards where he wanted them in the deck.

02:29:38.231 --> 02:29:40.841
Oh boy, what a talent!

02:29:40.901 --> 02:29:43.270
What a skill Jimmy had.

02:29:43.330 --> 02:29:45.711
And he had something else too.

02:29:45.771 --> 02:29:47.991
He had a choice.

02:29:48.051 --> 02:29:51.332
A choice about what to do with this talent.

02:29:51.392 --> 02:29:54.962
You see there are two things you can do with a talent like that.

02:29:55.022 --> 02:29:57.443
You can either play the game right.

02:29:57.503 --> 02:29:59.063
You play it straight.

02:29:59.123 --> 02:30:01.914
This way you enter the competitions.

02:30:01.974 --> 02:30:03.894
You play your best.

02:30:03.954 --> 02:30:06.094
And you play by the rules.

02:30:06.154 --> 02:30:08.575
Then slowly you win respect.

02:30:08.635 --> 02:30:11.574
And slowly you make a name for yourself.

02:30:11.634 --> 02:30:14.916
Or there's the other thing you can do.

02:30:14.976 --> 02:30:16.528
You can cheat.

02:30:16.588 --> 02:30:19.332
Do you know what I mean when I say cheat?

02:30:19.392 --> 02:30:28.000
I mean you use the little tricks, the fast fingers, and the good memory, but you use it to break the rules of the game.

02:30:28.060 --> 02:30:30.858
And you know what choice Jimmy Lane made?

02:30:30.918 --> 02:30:32.299
You got it.

02:30:32.359 --> 02:30:34.460
He decided to cheat.

02:30:34.520 --> 02:30:40.912
He decided to come to the biggest, richest, most important gambling city in the world.

02:30:40.972 --> 02:30:43.454
And he decided to cheat.

02:30:43.514 --> 02:30:44.704
That's right.

02:30:44.764 --> 02:30:46.005
He couldn't wait.

02:30:46.065 --> 02:30:48.426
He wanted everything now.

02:30:48.486 --> 02:30:49.777
He wanted money.

02:30:49.837 --> 02:30:51.137
He wanted to be famous.

02:30:51.197 --> 02:30:52.748
And he wanted respect.

02:30:52.808 --> 02:30:57.092
So yeah, he came here to Las Vegas.

02:30:57.152 --> 02:30:58.266
Vegas.

02:30:58.326 --> 02:30:58.886
Wow.

02:30:58.946 --> 02:31:00.068
What a city.

02:31:00.128 --> 02:31:03.150
The lights, the music, the atmosphere.

02:31:03.211 --> 02:31:06.074
And Jimmy thought he could take it all.

02:31:06.134 --> 02:31:07.045
Huh.

02:31:07.105 --> 02:31:08.516
What a kid.

02:31:08.577 --> 02:31:11.350
What a dumb, stupid kid.

02:31:11.410 --> 02:31:15.192
You see, you don't take from Las Vegas.

02:31:15.252 --> 02:31:17.702
Vegas takes from you.

02:31:17.762 --> 02:31:22.182
But Jimmy, poor, stupid Jimmy didn't know that.

02:31:22.242 --> 02:31:28.654
So he found a casino, a real casino, one of the best in the city.

02:31:28.714 --> 02:31:35.836
And he thought he could do it quickly, play the game, get the money, and run before anyone knew he was there.

02:31:35.896 --> 02:31:39.026
But he picked the wrong casino.

02:31:39.086 --> 02:31:41.661
He picked my casino.

02:31:41.721 --> 02:31:43.986
That was a bad decision.

02:31:44.046 --> 02:31:46.808
But worse than that was his next decision.

02:31:46.868 --> 02:31:53.814
That was when he decided he was good enough and clever enough and lucky enough to play at my table.

02:31:53.874 --> 02:31:57.446
To enter my golden chip competition.

02:31:57.506 --> 02:32:01.930
A competition where only the best of the best gets to play.

02:32:01.990 --> 02:32:05.230
How he made the money to enter, I don't know.

02:32:05.290 --> 02:32:08.822
I think he probably cheated to get that too.

02:32:08.882 --> 02:32:19.392
But he entered my competition and he used his tricks and his fast fingers and he won every game until it came to the final.

02:32:19.452 --> 02:32:24.390
Hank wins stops talking and puts his cigar back in his mouth.

02:32:24.450 --> 02:32:26.312
He looks calm.

02:32:26.372 --> 02:32:28.184
Happy almost.

02:32:28.244 --> 02:32:33.648
But there is something very dangerous about Hank win when he looks calm and happy.

02:32:33.708 --> 02:32:41.074
Some people say that the reason his casino is called shark pool is because Hank looks a little like a shark.

02:32:41.134 --> 02:32:43.813
He always wears a grey suit.

02:32:43.873 --> 02:32:46.924
He always walks in a slow manner.

02:32:46.984 --> 02:32:50.626
And his eyes are always cold and dark.

02:32:50.686 --> 02:32:54.497
But maybe there's another reason for the name of the casino.

02:32:54.557 --> 02:32:56.388
The truth, Jimmy.

02:32:56.448 --> 02:33:00.630
and Hank throws the end of the cigar into the pool in front of him.

02:33:00.690 --> 02:33:09.898
It is night time, and around the roof of the 30-floor casino and hotel, the city of Las Vegas is alive with colour.

02:33:09.958 --> 02:33:15.432
There are people everywhere, but here on the roof there are only four people.

02:33:15.492 --> 02:33:23.953
There is Hank Win and his assistants and bodyguards Kenny and Clive, who stand patiently and listen to his speech.

02:33:24.013 --> 02:33:26.054
And there is Jimmy Lane.

02:33:26.114 --> 02:33:28.584
Jimmy Lane does not stand.

02:33:28.644 --> 02:33:31.045
Jimmy Lane cannot stand.

02:33:31.105 --> 02:33:36.186
His hands are tied, his legs are tied, and his mouth is gagged.

02:33:36.246 --> 02:33:39.096
And there is blood on Jimmy Lane.

02:33:39.156 --> 02:33:45.644
Small cuts on his arms and chest, and he's naked apart from his trousers.

02:33:45.704 --> 02:33:48.581
Now, I'm not a cruel man, Jimmy.

02:33:48.641 --> 02:33:49.812
I'm really not.

02:33:49.872 --> 02:33:52.254
I'm a businessman, not a gangster.

02:33:52.314 --> 02:33:54.645
But this city is cruel, Jimmy.

02:33:54.705 --> 02:33:58.988
And sometimes I have to do things to protect my business.

02:33:59.048 --> 02:34:01.390
Do you understand that, Jimmy?

02:34:01.450 --> 02:34:06.042
Do you know how many kids like you come into my casino?

02:34:06.102 --> 02:34:07.204
No?

02:34:07.264 --> 02:34:08.464
Hundreds.

02:34:08.524 --> 02:34:09.965
Thousands.

02:34:10.025 --> 02:34:12.616
They all think they can win.

02:34:12.676 --> 02:34:16.589
They all think they can cheat and beat the casino.

02:34:16.649 --> 02:34:20.251
And Jimmy Lane shakes his head and tries to say something.

02:34:20.311 --> 02:34:22.569
And his blue eyes are wet.

02:34:22.629 --> 02:34:24.016
Wait a moment, Jimmy.

02:34:24.076 --> 02:34:25.416
Wait one moment."

02:34:25.476 --> 02:34:28.827
Hank says, and he puts his hand on Jimmy's head.

02:34:28.887 --> 02:34:31.308
And Jimmy tries to move away.

02:34:31.368 --> 02:34:34.709
I don't want you to say anything stupid, Jimmy.

02:34:34.769 --> 02:34:37.760
Because this is your last chance.

02:34:37.820 --> 02:34:40.780
He looks across the pool to his assistant.

02:34:40.840 --> 02:34:43.551
When did they last eat, Clive?

02:34:43.611 --> 02:34:45.411
Last week, Mr. When?

02:34:45.471 --> 02:34:47.132
You remember the Mexican?

02:34:47.192 --> 02:34:49.297
The one stealing from the bar?

02:34:49.357 --> 02:34:51.915
Ah yes, a week.

02:34:51.975 --> 02:34:54.068
Did you hear that, Jimmy?

02:34:54.128 --> 02:35:03.240
So before I take this gag out of your mouth, and before you think about saying something stupid again, think about this.

02:35:03.300 --> 02:35:06.934
Did you really not think that we were watching you?

02:35:06.994 --> 02:35:08.973
We knew you were cheating.

02:35:09.033 --> 02:35:12.084
We knew you were using your little tricks.

02:35:12.144 --> 02:35:18.596
The only reason you got to the final table to my table, Jimmy, is because I let you.

02:35:18.656 --> 02:35:21.866
And Hank takes Jimmy's face in his hands.

02:35:21.926 --> 02:35:25.347
The truth, Jimmy, I want the truth.

02:35:25.407 --> 02:35:30.248
It's very important to me, and right now it's very important to you.

02:35:30.308 --> 02:35:33.199
And he takes the gag from Jimmy's mouth.

02:35:33.259 --> 02:35:35.820
For a moment Jimmy is silent.

02:35:35.880 --> 02:35:38.652
Then he shouts as loud as he can.

02:35:38.712 --> 02:35:39.712
Help me!

02:35:39.772 --> 02:35:40.962
Somebody, we're on the roof!

02:35:41.022 --> 02:35:42.033
Help me!

02:35:42.093 --> 02:35:44.674
And for a moment Hank is silent.

02:35:44.734 --> 02:35:46.174
Then he laughs.

02:35:46.234 --> 02:35:48.405
And the two assistants laugh.

02:35:48.465 --> 02:35:52.767
And then the back of Hank's hand slaps Jimmy's face hard.

02:35:52.827 --> 02:35:55.898
Oh Jimmy, you really are a stupid kid.

02:35:55.958 --> 02:35:59.923
We're miles up above one of the loudest cities in the world.

02:35:59.983 --> 02:36:01.817
No one can hear you.

02:36:01.877 --> 02:36:04.138
No one but me and the boys.

02:36:04.198 --> 02:36:06.980
So Jimmy, one last chance.

02:36:07.040 --> 02:36:08.190
Tell me.

02:36:08.250 --> 02:36:09.960
Tell me the truth.

02:36:10.020 --> 02:36:14.799
And Jimmy looks at the pool and the clear water, and he nods.

02:36:14.859 --> 02:36:17.186
Okay, okay, you're right, Mr. Win.

02:36:17.246 --> 02:36:18.928
Everything you say about me is right.

02:36:18.988 --> 02:36:19.849
I was a nobody.

02:36:19.909 --> 02:36:20.760
I was never rich.

02:36:20.820 --> 02:36:21.951
I was never too clever.

02:36:22.011 --> 02:36:23.712
But I can play cards.

02:36:23.772 --> 02:36:29.608
I started playing when I was seven or eight, with friends at school, online, in local competitions.

02:36:29.668 --> 02:36:30.568
And I'm good.

02:36:30.628 --> 02:36:32.140
I mean, I'm really good.

02:36:32.200 --> 02:36:33.781
And yeah, that's why I came here.

02:36:33.841 --> 02:36:34.942
I wanted to win.

02:36:35.002 --> 02:36:37.184
I wanted the respect you talked about.

02:36:37.244 --> 02:36:38.225
Is that a crime?

02:36:38.285 --> 02:36:39.332
Is that wrong?

02:36:39.392 --> 02:36:41.348
Hank slaps him again.

02:36:41.408 --> 02:36:45.070
No, Jimmy, but cheating in my casino is.

02:36:45.130 --> 02:36:46.850
I didn't cheat.

02:36:46.910 --> 02:36:49.281
Jimmy shouts and he's angry now.

02:36:49.341 --> 02:36:50.602
I never cheat.

02:36:50.662 --> 02:36:54.284
You can hit me or you can try to scare me, but I never cheat.

02:36:54.344 --> 02:36:56.704
Hank win laughs.

02:36:56.765 --> 02:36:58.415
Scare you.

02:36:58.475 --> 02:37:00.576
You think we want to scare you.

02:37:00.636 --> 02:37:04.518
Clive, let them out, he says.

02:37:04.578 --> 02:37:09.250
Clive nods and pushes a button on the control panel next to the pool.

02:37:09.310 --> 02:37:12.811
Below the water a metal door slides open.

02:37:12.871 --> 02:37:15.152
For a moment there is nothing.

02:37:15.212 --> 02:37:19.803
But then two long gray shapes appear in the clear water.

02:37:19.863 --> 02:37:22.464
And Jimmy starts to shout again.

02:37:22.524 --> 02:37:24.254
You cheated Jimmy.

02:37:24.314 --> 02:37:26.165
Four aces?

02:37:26.225 --> 02:37:28.356
I know you put those cards in.

02:37:28.416 --> 02:37:30.306
I don't know how, but I know.

02:37:30.366 --> 02:37:31.907
No Mr. Win, I didn't.

02:37:31.967 --> 02:37:32.517
I promise.

02:37:32.577 --> 02:37:33.007
I didn't.

02:37:33.067 --> 02:37:33.878
I don't cheat.

02:37:33.938 --> 02:37:35.197
I never cheat.

02:37:35.257 --> 02:37:42.809
For a moment Hank Wind stops, and maybe there's a second where he believes poor Jimmy Lane.

02:37:42.869 --> 02:37:50.232
But then the second is over, and he kicks Jimmy in the back and Jimmy falls into the pool with a scream.

02:37:50.292 --> 02:37:54.154
Hank Wind does not stop to watch what happens next.

02:37:54.214 --> 02:37:55.904
He never does.

02:37:55.964 --> 02:37:58.458
You see, he's not a cruel man.

02:37:58.518 --> 02:38:01.562
But he sometimes has to do cruel things.

02:38:01.622 --> 02:38:02.813
Come on.

02:38:02.873 --> 02:38:07.184
He says to Clive and Kenny, and they move back to the stairs.

02:38:07.244 --> 02:38:10.844
But Mr. Nguyen, how did you know he cheated?

02:38:10.904 --> 02:38:13.345
We didn't see anything on the cameras.

02:38:13.404 --> 02:38:14.865
Clive asks.

02:38:14.925 --> 02:38:16.695
Hank shakes his head.

02:38:16.755 --> 02:38:17.995
Idiot.

02:38:18.055 --> 02:38:23.116
I always take the aces out of the pack and make sure that I get the kings.

02:38:23.176 --> 02:38:27.417
He says, and he goes back into the casino to play another game.

02:38:27.477 --> 02:38:36.364
But for a moment Clive and Kenny wait, stepping backwards so that the waves caused by the feeding frenzy do not get at them.

02:38:36.424 --> 02:38:39.116
For a moment Clive is silent.

02:38:39.176 --> 02:38:44.430
Then he slowly says something as if he does not himself believe what he's saying.

02:38:44.490 --> 02:38:49.617
I, I changed the pack of cards before the last game.

02:38:49.677 --> 02:38:51.990
I never told Mr. Win.

02:38:52.050 --> 02:38:55.191
You mean, Clive nods.

02:38:55.251 --> 02:39:03.624
The aces were in the pack, and for a little while more they wait, until the surface of the pool is calm again.

02:39:03.684 --> 02:39:11.316
I guess he was telling the truth then, and they shut the door on what remains of Jimmy Lane.

02:39:11.376 --> 02:39:21.934
The tube The air of the tube platform is hot and dry, and the light's weak and unnatural.

02:39:21.994 --> 02:39:31.444
Sarah considers sitting in one of the seats by the wall of the tunnel, but she's so tired that she thinks that maybe she'll fall asleep if she rests.

02:39:31.504 --> 02:39:37.480
So she stands, and she looks around at her fellow commuters and other passengers.

02:39:37.540 --> 02:39:39.742
Twenty tired faces.

02:39:39.802 --> 02:39:44.588
Some talking, but most are silent and waiting like her.

02:39:44.648 --> 02:39:47.190
She looks at the electronic clock.

02:39:47.250 --> 02:39:49.113
It is 7.36.

02:39:49.173 --> 02:39:52.687
Her train should be here in two minutes.

02:39:52.747 --> 02:40:00.444
For a moment she thinks about how quiet the platform is, and how normally she has to fight to get on the train in the evening.

02:40:00.504 --> 02:40:03.006
But there will be no fighting today.

02:40:03.066 --> 02:40:11.728
The few people that are here look half dead, exhausted from a long day at work and made passive by the slow journey home.

02:40:11.788 --> 02:40:20.562
Home Sarah thinks about her cozy sofa and the Chinese takeaways she will eat while watching TV.

02:40:20.622 --> 02:40:28.020
She can get it as she passes the restaurant, buy a bottle of wine from the local shop and be in bed by 10 o'clock.

02:40:28.080 --> 02:40:32.198
It is a comforting but also rather depressing thought.

02:40:32.258 --> 02:40:33.830
But that's life.

02:40:33.890 --> 02:40:36.774
Work, eat, sleep.

02:40:36.834 --> 02:40:43.832
She hears the train in the dark tunnel and tells herself to wake up and to focus for another 40 minutes.

02:40:43.892 --> 02:40:47.846
The 40 minutes from Liverpool Street to North Acton.

02:40:47.906 --> 02:40:54.830
The train speeds out of the tunnel, and she enjoys the feeling of the wind in her dark brown hair.

02:40:54.890 --> 02:40:59.714
As it stops, she sees her reflection in the mirror of the carriage.

02:40:59.774 --> 02:41:03.096
I look like death, she thinks.

02:41:03.156 --> 02:41:05.588
The doors of the carriage is open.

02:41:05.648 --> 02:41:09.310
A few people get off and Sarah and the commuters get on.

02:41:09.370 --> 02:41:13.104
Inside the carriage it's even hotter than on the platform.

02:41:13.164 --> 02:41:15.255
But it's almost empty too.

02:41:15.315 --> 02:41:18.107
She finds a seat and sits down.

02:41:18.167 --> 02:41:20.699
Do not fall asleep.

02:41:20.759 --> 02:41:22.280
She tells herself.

02:41:22.340 --> 02:41:24.302
She looks around the carriage.

02:41:24.362 --> 02:41:27.074
There are only eight other people there.

02:41:27.134 --> 02:41:31.685
A young couple holding hands and talking quietly on the seat opposite her.

02:41:31.745 --> 02:41:34.576
Two businessmen a few seats to her right.

02:41:34.636 --> 02:41:37.326
An old lady two seats to her left.

02:41:37.386 --> 02:41:40.306
A mother and son at the far end of the carriage.

02:41:40.366 --> 02:41:44.147
And a strange looking man in a coat in the corner.

02:41:44.207 --> 02:41:46.608
A strange man.

02:41:46.668 --> 02:41:48.518
Is he strange?

02:41:48.578 --> 02:41:50.669
Or are you just being silly?

02:41:50.729 --> 02:41:52.279
She looks at him.

02:41:52.339 --> 02:41:56.561
She cannot see his face because he has the hood of his sports jacket up.

02:41:56.621 --> 02:41:58.522
But he seems to be asleep.

02:41:58.582 --> 02:42:02.044
And there's something about his face that she does not like.

02:42:02.104 --> 02:42:05.876
He has a short beard and pale, unhealthy skin.

02:42:05.936 --> 02:42:09.568
And she thinks that his eyes are probably unkind.

02:42:09.628 --> 02:42:13.439
The train starts, and she shakes her head.

02:42:13.500 --> 02:42:18.522
You are an idiot, she tells herself and pulls a book out of her bag.

02:42:18.582 --> 02:42:24.859
On the front, there's a picture of a man with a short beard and pale skin and unkind eyes.

02:42:24.919 --> 02:42:27.002
She almost laughs.

02:42:27.062 --> 02:42:29.564
You need to stop reading this rubbish.

02:42:29.624 --> 02:42:33.020
You're losing your mind, she tells herself.

02:42:33.080 --> 02:42:39.578
The man in the corner is probably just a normal guy and not some horrible character from one of her thrillers.

02:42:39.638 --> 02:42:42.810
For ten minutes she reads her crime novel.

02:42:42.870 --> 02:42:50.678
She starts to feel better, more relaxed and less concerned about the reason why the tube is so quiet today.

02:42:50.738 --> 02:42:53.510
Then the train stops at another station.

02:42:53.570 --> 02:42:57.711
There are loud voices on the platform and Sarah knows why.

02:42:57.771 --> 02:43:04.793
The doors slide open and 10 or 12 students from Chancery High School enter the carriage.

02:43:04.853 --> 02:43:11.532
The old woman two seats away from her does not look happy and she moves to sit in the seat next to Sarah.

02:43:11.592 --> 02:43:13.631
Do you mind, dear?

02:43:13.691 --> 02:43:17.013
No, of course not, Sarah says and smiles.

02:43:17.073 --> 02:43:21.406
The poor old woman must not feel too comfortable next to the noisy kids.

02:43:21.466 --> 02:43:24.057
But Sarah knows that they are okay.

02:43:24.118 --> 02:43:27.119
She can spot the bad kids from a mile away.

02:43:27.180 --> 02:43:29.902
She is, after all, a teacher.

02:43:29.962 --> 02:43:36.706
No, these kids are okay, but they are loud, and it's impossible for Sarah not to hear their conversation.

02:43:36.766 --> 02:43:42.258
And when she hears the topic that they are talking about, she suddenly feels cold.

02:43:42.318 --> 02:43:44.479
Yeah, he killed another one, didn't he?

02:43:44.539 --> 02:43:45.970
Last Friday, they reckon.

02:43:46.030 --> 02:43:47.912
The tallest of the kids says.

02:43:47.972 --> 02:43:49.774
I know, Sikh, isn't it?

02:43:49.834 --> 02:43:51.716
I mean, how many is that now?

02:43:51.776 --> 02:43:53.328
asks another.

02:43:53.388 --> 02:43:54.600
Sikhs?

02:43:54.660 --> 02:43:56.412
No, man, seven.

02:43:56.472 --> 02:43:57.632
Is it?

02:43:57.692 --> 02:44:00.906
Man, you know, the Sikh thing is that no one knows who it is.

02:44:00.966 --> 02:44:04.738
I don't know if it's a man, a woman, some kid, you get me?

02:44:04.798 --> 02:44:07.570
No, the sick thing is the way he kills them.

02:44:07.630 --> 02:44:16.600
He uses something strange, not a knife, but something long and thin, and he stabs them again and again and they just bleed and bleed.

02:44:16.660 --> 02:44:21.214
Yeah, but the weird thing is none of them try to fight, says the tall kid.

02:44:21.274 --> 02:44:22.625
How do you know that?

02:44:22.685 --> 02:44:24.466
Because the newspapers said it.

02:44:24.526 --> 02:44:28.090
They said none of the victims looked like they tried to fight.

02:44:28.150 --> 02:44:32.714
They just, I don't know, let this guy stab them again and again.

02:44:32.774 --> 02:44:35.456
And now there is silence in the carriage.

02:44:35.516 --> 02:44:39.469
And Sarah sees that everyone is listening to the conversation.

02:44:39.529 --> 02:44:41.730
But she does not want to listen.

02:44:41.790 --> 02:44:46.820
And she takes her MP3 player from her bag and puts her headphones in.

02:44:46.880 --> 02:44:53.182
She does not want to think about their conversation or the horrible reason why the tube is so quiet.

02:44:53.242 --> 02:44:57.072
She does not want to think about the stories in the newspapers.

02:44:57.132 --> 02:45:02.364
She does not want to think about what the police are calling the tube murders.

02:45:02.424 --> 02:45:06.004
She closes her eyes and thinks about her sofa.

02:45:06.064 --> 02:45:09.105
Her Chinese takeaway and her bed.

02:45:09.165 --> 02:45:15.600
After a few more minutes she feels the train stop and she is aware of the kids getting off.

02:45:15.660 --> 02:45:20.894
Just listen to the music she tells herself, relax and rest.

02:45:20.954 --> 02:45:23.056
But don't sleep.

02:45:23.116 --> 02:45:24.846
Don't.

02:45:24.907 --> 02:45:37.547
But she is exhausted and her head begins to drop and she feels herself falling asleep and she dreams about dark tunnels and strange men watching her.

02:45:37.607 --> 02:45:42.700
Then she feels a sharp pain in her arm, and she almost screams.

02:45:42.760 --> 02:45:47.574
She looks to see what the pain is and pulls her headphones from her ears.

02:45:47.634 --> 02:45:51.216
I'm sorry, dear, the old woman says.

02:45:51.276 --> 02:45:55.600
And Sarah sees that the woman's cloth bag is touching her arm.

02:45:55.660 --> 02:45:57.332
Did I wake you up?

02:45:57.392 --> 02:45:59.624
It must be something in my bag.

02:45:59.684 --> 02:46:01.735
I have all my knitting things in here."

02:46:01.796 --> 02:46:05.139
The old woman looks very upset and apologetic.

02:46:05.199 --> 02:46:08.412
It's okay, Sarah says and smiles.

02:46:08.472 --> 02:46:10.374
I was just surprised.

02:46:10.434 --> 02:46:12.296
Oh, you poor thing.

02:46:12.356 --> 02:46:13.878
You look exhausted.

02:46:13.938 --> 02:46:16.260
I'll move my bag so you can sleep.

02:46:16.320 --> 02:46:17.310
It's okay.

02:46:17.370 --> 02:46:18.665
I don't want to sleep.

02:46:18.725 --> 02:46:20.628
No, you relax."

02:46:20.688 --> 02:46:22.389
The old woman says.

02:46:22.449 --> 02:46:25.870
And Sarah thinks the old lady has a kind smile.

02:46:25.930 --> 02:46:31.751
Sarah nods and is about to put her headphones back in her ears when she feels someone watching her.

02:46:31.811 --> 02:46:34.531
And she looks at the corner of the carriage.

02:46:34.591 --> 02:46:36.562
The strange man.

02:46:36.622 --> 02:46:40.552
The man who looks like the evil character from her thriller.

02:46:40.613 --> 02:46:42.603
His eyes are open now.

02:46:42.663 --> 02:46:44.764
And they are watching her.

02:46:44.824 --> 02:46:48.428
She feels a cold, shiver move over her body.

02:46:48.488 --> 02:46:51.700
And suddenly she thinks that she feels a little ill.

02:46:51.760 --> 02:46:53.342
Is he watching her?

02:46:53.402 --> 02:46:54.903
She looks again.

02:46:54.963 --> 02:47:01.328
And yes, his hood is still covering his face, but she is sure that his eyes are focused on her.

02:47:01.388 --> 02:47:03.660
She tries to stay calm.

02:47:03.720 --> 02:47:06.382
There are still several people in the carriage.

02:47:06.443 --> 02:47:09.504
The young couple is still there and the two businessmen.

02:47:09.564 --> 02:47:13.566
And the next station is North Acton, her station.

02:47:13.626 --> 02:47:15.908
You just need to get home.

02:47:15.968 --> 02:47:17.588
She tells herself.

02:47:17.648 --> 02:47:20.630
And she feels the train slow down.

02:47:20.690 --> 02:47:23.151
And she knows she must get up.

02:47:23.211 --> 02:47:25.202
Is this your stop, dear?

02:47:25.262 --> 02:47:27.042
The old woman asks.

02:47:27.102 --> 02:47:28.664
Sarah nods.

02:47:28.724 --> 02:47:30.104
Oh, good.

02:47:30.164 --> 02:47:31.865
You're nearly home.

02:47:31.925 --> 02:47:34.536
Nearly time to get some sleep.

02:47:34.596 --> 02:47:36.680
Sarah tries to smile.

02:47:36.740 --> 02:47:38.583
Are you okay, dear?"

02:47:38.643 --> 02:47:40.424
You look a little pale.

02:47:40.484 --> 02:47:42.724
Yes, just tired.

02:47:42.784 --> 02:47:45.026
You should put some water on your face.

02:47:45.086 --> 02:47:46.436
You'll feel better.

02:47:46.496 --> 02:47:47.937
Sarah nods.

02:47:47.997 --> 02:47:49.798
I will, thank you.

02:47:49.858 --> 02:47:51.809
I will, she thinks.

02:47:51.868 --> 02:47:55.550
But only if the strange man stays on the train.

02:47:55.610 --> 02:47:59.212
If he follows me, I will shout and scream for help.

02:47:59.272 --> 02:48:03.574
She stands up and looks in the corner of the carriage one more time.

02:48:03.634 --> 02:48:06.336
But it looks like the man is asleep.

02:48:06.396 --> 02:48:09.278
Go, go now, she tells herself.

02:48:09.338 --> 02:48:12.950
The door's open and she quickly walks through them.

02:48:13.010 --> 02:48:17.952
For a few seconds she walks and then turns back to look at the tube train.

02:48:18.012 --> 02:48:19.754
No one is there.

02:48:19.814 --> 02:48:23.075
No one gets off apart from her, she is sure.

02:48:23.135 --> 02:48:26.656
The train starts again and she relaxes a little.

02:48:26.716 --> 02:48:32.226
The platform at North Acton is quiet, but it always is at this time of the evening.

02:48:32.286 --> 02:48:38.168
She can see the stairs to the street and she wants to walk to them, but she feels so weak.

02:48:38.228 --> 02:48:41.278
She decides to go into the station toilets.

02:48:41.338 --> 02:48:43.679
All she needs is a little water.

02:48:43.739 --> 02:48:47.040
She enters the ladies and stands in front of the mirror.

02:48:47.100 --> 02:48:51.201
Her arm hurts, so she takes her jacket off.

02:48:51.261 --> 02:48:55.804
And in the mirror she can see a small drop of blood on her skin.

02:48:55.864 --> 02:48:57.296
It's okay, dear.

02:48:57.356 --> 02:48:59.058
You'll feel better in a moment.

02:48:59.118 --> 02:49:01.880
I gave you a little medicine to help you sleep.

02:49:01.940 --> 02:49:04.572
Sarah jumps at the sound of the voice.

02:49:04.632 --> 02:49:08.235
And in the mirror she sees the reflection of the old woman.

02:49:08.296 --> 02:49:09.556
What?

02:49:09.616 --> 02:49:11.178
She tries to say.

02:49:11.238 --> 02:49:13.720
But then she falls to the floor.

02:49:13.780 --> 02:49:15.542
Help me.

02:49:15.602 --> 02:49:18.104
she says in a weak voice.

02:49:18.164 --> 02:49:20.586
The old woman smiles.

02:49:20.646 --> 02:49:22.858
But it is a horrible smile.

02:49:22.918 --> 02:49:24.879
I am helping you dear.

02:49:24.939 --> 02:49:26.620
You poor thing.

02:49:26.680 --> 02:49:29.722
So tired, so exhausted.

02:49:29.782 --> 02:49:31.904
You can't live like that.

02:49:31.964 --> 02:49:33.866
But it's okay now.

02:49:33.926 --> 02:49:35.847
I'm here to help you."

02:49:35.907 --> 02:49:40.989
And the old woman takes a long, thin knitting needle from her bag.

02:49:41.049 --> 02:49:44.738
And Sarah remembers what the kid said on the tube.

02:49:44.798 --> 02:49:47.440
He uses something strange.

02:49:47.500 --> 02:49:50.812
Not a knife, but something long and thin.

02:49:50.872 --> 02:49:53.864
And he stabs them again and again.

02:49:53.924 --> 02:49:57.405
And they just bleed and bleed.

02:49:57.466 --> 02:49:59.996
She tries to scream.

02:50:00.056 --> 02:50:02.018
But it is too late.

